<p>CEO Initial and Surname Signature Date J.E.Naidoo 02 January 2026</p>', 'Corporate Social Responsibility Policy PURPOSE Landscapers INC Directors support the principles set out in the various codes and standards and the aim of this policy is to translate that support into a set of guidelines and standards that set a common approach for Landscapers INC and provide practical guidance for our managers and employees on the ground. SCOPE This policy is applicable to all staff, customers, shareholders (if applicable), at Landscapers INC POLICY STATEMENT Landscapers INC seeks to be a good corporate citizen in everything that it does. We have therefore determined to bring together our existing operating principles into one framework policy under the heading of Corporate Social Responsibility (CSR). The principles encompassed in this policy cover all areas of Landscapers INC operations and have been developed and continue to be reviewed against and updated by reference to relevant codes of corporate governance and international standards. SECTION 1 CODE OF BUSINESS ETHICS This code applies to all of the operations of Landscapers INC and its subsidiaries and sets out the minimum standards which the Directors of Landscapers INC expects from staff in their internal and external dealings with colleagues, customers, stakeholders and third parties. 1.1 Basic Standards of Conduct a) We will conduct every aspect of our business with honesty, integrity and openness, respecting human rights and the interests of our employees, customers and third parties. b) We will respect the legitimate interests of third parties with whom we have dealings in the course of our business. c) We will maintain the highest standards of integrity – for example, we will not promise more than we can reasonably deliver or make commitments we cannot or do not intend to keep. 1.2 Landscapers INC a) Is committed to creating and maintaining a safe and healthy working environment for its employees. b) Will strive to create a workplace in which there is mutual trust and respect and where every person feels responsible for the performance and reputation of our company. c) Will respect the individual and each other’s rights, customs and traditions including the right to freedom of association and the right to decide whether or not to join a trade union and will negotiate in good faith with the properly elected representatives of its employees. d) Will work towards achieving a diverse workforce, recruiting, employing and promoting employees only on the basis of objective criteria and the qualifications and abilities needed for the job to be performed. e) Will maintain good communications with employees through our information and consultation procedures. f) Will assist employees in realising their potential. 1.3 Customers a) Landscapers INC is committed to providing safe, value for money, high quality, consistent, accessible and reliable services to its customers. Corporate Social Responsibility Policy POL 011 Version: 00 Effective Date: 02-01- 2026 Next Revision Date: 02-01-2027 1.4 Shareholders a) Landscapers INC will conduct its operations in accordance with the principles of good corporate governance. b) We will provide timely, regular and reliable information on the business to all our shareholders. 1.5 Business Partners and Stakeholders a) We aim to develop strong relationships with our suppliers, stakeholders and others with whom we have dealings, based on mutual trust, understanding and respect. b) In those dealings, we expect our partners to adhere to business principles consistent with our own. c) Landscapers INC companies will conduct their operations in accordance with the principles of fair competition and applicable regulations. 1.6 Compliance with Law a) All members of Landscapers INC will comply with the laws and regulations applicable wherever they do business. Appropriate training will be provided for employees as necessary. 1.7 Business Integrity a) No Landscapers INC employee shall offer, give, seek or receive, either directly or indirectly, inducements or other improper advantages for business or financial gain and no employee may offer, give, seek or receive any gift or payment which is, or could be construed as, such. If an employee is in any doubt as to whether he or she may accept an offer, that employee should discuss the issue with his or her manager or Director. b) Landscapers INC accounting and other records and supporting documents must accurately describe and reflect the nature of the underlying transactions. c) No undisclosed or unrecorded account, fund or asset will be established or maintained. d) Landscapers INC will not facilitate, support, tolerate or condone any form of money laundering. 1.8 The Environment a) Landscapers INC is committed to making continuous improvement in the management of its Environmental impact. b) We will work with our partners to promote environmental care, increase understanding of environmental issues and disseminate good practice. 1.9 Community Involvement a) Landscapers INC strives to be a good corporate citizen and to fulfil our responsibilities to the societies and communities in which we operate. 1.10 Conflicts of interest and confidentiality a) Whilst Landscapers INC respects the privacy of its employees, all Landscapers INC employees are expected to avoid personal activities and financial interests, which could conflict with their responsibilities to Landscapers INC. b) Landscapers INC employees and consultants must not seek gain for themselves or others through misuse of their positions or company property. c) All actual and potential conflicts (including those arising from the activities or interests of close relatives or partners) should be disclosed to and discussed with an employee’s line manager. d) Information received by anyone in the course of his or her employment must not be used for personal gain or for any purpose other than that for which it was given. e) Where information is confidential, that confidentiality must be respected. SECTION 2 SAFETY AND SECURITY 2.1 Safety The health and safety of our employees and customers is our paramount concern. Safety underpins all our operations and our central motto is “If you cannot do it safely, don’t do it”. We have developed the following high-level health and safety policy, which underpins all of our operational health and safety policies: Corporate Social Responsibility Policy POL 011 Version: 00 Effective Date: 02-01- 2026 Next Revision Date: 02-01-2027 a) General Statement The Directors of Landscapers INC is committed to ensuring, so far as is reasonably practicable, the health, safety and welfare of all of its employees at work and also the safety of customers and others. b) This policy seeks continuous improvement and compliance with legislation, having proper regard to the protection of people, premises, property and the environment. It is based on the principles that: All injuries can be prevented The goal is zero injuries Safety is the responsibility of all employees Working safely is a condition of employment c) The Directors of Landscapers INC are tasked to ensure that so far as is reasonably practicable: There are adequate arrangements and organization for health and safety in place within their area of responsibility Responsibilities for carrying out these arrangements are clearly allocated All staff are given appropriate information, instruction and training Adequate supervision is provided to ensure compliance with policies and safe systems of work All other legal and statutory duties on health and safety incumbent upon Landscapers INC are complied with in all their operations and locations Performance targets are set to achieve a step change in safety performance Adequate resources are allocated, and competent persons are appointed to support the achievement of the above objectives. d) We will continually monitor the health and safety performance of our operations which will be subjected to periodic safety audits to assess performance. The nominated directors will: Present a monthly report on health and safety to the Safety Committee Report to the Directors all fatal and notifiable injuries to staff or other persons within 24 hours. The nine key safety principles with which all Landscapers INC employees are required to comply are set out below: Do not endanger yourself or others. Report any hazardous condition or practice that may cause injury to people property or the environment. Obey all rules, signs and instructions. If you do not understand speak to your manager before you start work. Keep your work area clean and tidy. Disorder causes accidents, wastes time, energy and materials. Wear protective clothing and equipment as required. Keep it in good condition, wear it correctly and ask for a replacement if it becomes damaged or unfit for use. All accidents, incidents and near misses must be reported to your manager. Seek immediate help and first aid (if necessary). Do not adjust modify or repair any piece of work equipment unless you are competent and authorized to do so. Use only the correct tools and equipment for the job. Check that they are in good condition before use and use them safely. Before lifting, assess the load and your capability to move it. Make sure you get help with any heavy or awkward items and follow approved techniques. If you have any suggestions to improve safety in your workplace, tell your supervisor or manager. We also require contractors to comply with this policy whilst they are working at our premises. 2.2 Security a) General Statement Security is a key issue for our staff and our business. We recognize the range of security issues which can arise and have implemented the following high-level security policy. b) The Directors of Landscapers INC is committed to ensuring, so far as is reasonably practicable, the security of our employees at work and our property. c) The threats to security are wide ranging, significant and, in the main, driven by external influences. The ability to control risks varies. However, we have the ability to address or Corporate Social Responsibility Policy POL 011 Version: 00 Effective Date: 02-01- 2026 Next Revision Date: 02-01-2027 mitigate all of the threats which apply. To be successful in this we need the co-operation and engagement of all of our staff. d) Security is an area in which we seek continuous development and improvement and compliance with existing and emerging legislation. We will heighten the profile of security to ensure all members of staff understand why they should and how they can play their part. Our efforts will bring greater personal and corporate security and business benefit. e) This statement provides guidance and direction to all employees on what is both required and expected of them. We are confident our staff will appreciate the reasons for this and the benefits to them, their colleagues, and our property. f) We aim to achieve, so far as is reasonably practicable: - A secure environment for customers using our services A secure environment for staff in their work Security of our property Security of our systems and processes g) We will develop systems to monitor our performance on security within our operations. All operations will be subject to periodic inspection and review. h) Landscapers INC will provide regular reports on security to the Management Directors. i) Landscapers INC recognizes that this security policy needs to be supported by actions and processes to ensure delivery. The following measures are in place to assist this: A security strategy is in place, which underpins our policy, identifies the key strategic security issues for the business and the approach being taken to address these. This is designed to provide a structure, which allows measurement, review and ongoing refinement. j) A specialist security company has been engaged which operates across all of South Africa. k) The importance of security is emphasized to all staff through regular security briefings and a ‘Security’ section has been created on our HSE MS system. This provides security advice and guidance and contact details for key security personnel. It also ensures our security specialists can be accessed by all our staff when they are needed. l) Staff is actively encouraged to report security concerns. To support this there is a confidential telephone reporting system which allows staff to report any concerns with anonymity. This is specifically designed to give staff the confidence to report their concerns even in circumstances where they may find conventional line management routes difficult. m) This approach to security allows us to ensure we have a process to translate our policy into effective implementation within the organization. It also allows us to formally recognize and demonstrate our commitment to the need for continuous development and improvement in this important aspect of our business. We also require contractors to comply with this policy whilst they are working at our premises. SECTION 3 EMPLOYMENT In formulating its employment policies, Landscapers INC is guided by the BCEA, EA. These Guidelines encourage companies to foster openness, sustainability and respect for employee rights. Our employment policies cover all employees of Landscapers INC. 3.1 Equal Opportunities and Diversity Policy a) The Directors of Landscapers INC is committed to equality of opportunity both in the provision of services to the public and as an employer. This policy sets out Landscapers INC commitment to treat equally and with fairness at all times its employees, customers, contractors and those who come into contact with the company. b) We are committed to seeking continuous improvement and compliance with legislation based on the following principles. Everyone has the right to be treated with dignity and respect. We will not discriminate on the grounds of race, gender, disability, nationality, religion, philosophical belief, political belief, age, sexual orientation, family status, trade union activity or any other factor. We will adopt fair and inclusive practices throughout our operations and will seek to eliminate all prejudice, discrimination, bullying and harassment. All employees have a personal responsibility for the practical application of this policy in their day-to-day activities and must support the policy at all times. Non-compliance with this policy will be treated seriously and will not be tolerated. Corporate Social Responsibility Policy POL 011 Version: 00 Effective Date: 02-01- 2026 Next Revision Date: 02-01-2027 c) The Directors of Landscapers INC are required to ensure: They create a productive and safe working environment, promoting diversity and inclusion in their workforce; They develop new practices, as appropriate, to ensure all employees, contractors and customers are treated fairly; and They can demonstrate continuous improvement in practices to promote diversity and equal opportunities for all. Legislation and Codes of Practice d) We will comply with and exceed where possible, current national and international legislation and relevant codes of practice in the countries where we operate. We will monitor our compliance with this policy and the requirements of relevant underpinning legislation as appropriate. Partner Organizations We are committed to actively working with partner organizations to ensure our policies, procedures and practices are in line with best practice. Practices and Standard Operating Procedures e) Each division and operating company will put in place practices and standard operating procedures to ensure the commitments in this policy are applied and implemented throughout the organization. Access to Company Premises f) We will take all reasonable steps to ensure that our buildings and premises are accessible to disabled employees, customers and visitors. Access to Vehicles g) We will also take reasonable steps to ensure that our vehicles are accessible to customers and staff and comply with the relevant legislation for the country in which they operate. Access to Information h) We will seek to ensure that information is made available to our customers and employees in alternative formats as required. Recruitment i) All recruitment will be carried out with regard to fairness, equality and consistency for all candidates at all times. Recruitment practices will be inclusive, and we will endeavour to ensure there are no barriers to employment of suitable candidates. Staff Training j) We will provide our staff with the necessary guidance and training to ensure the effective implementation of this policy and to ensure we are an inclusive employer and service provider. Complaints k) Any employee who feels that he or she has grounds for complaint in relation to bullying, discrimination, harassment or victimization has the right to pursue the complaint through our grievance procedures. Customers who feel they have grounds for complaint may pursue these through our operating company customer complaints procedures. We will ensure our complaints/feedback procedures can be accessed and used by everyone. Reporting l) We are committed to monitoring and reporting on our actions and achievements in relation to implementing this Diversity policy both internally and externally. Audit n) We are committed to ensuring that our operations comply with the requirements of this policy and will periodically audit its implementation. 3.2 Human Rights Landscapers INC supports the principles of Declaration of Human Rights. We will adhere to the following principles in respect of our staff. Corporate Social Responsibility Policy POL 011 Version: 00 Effective Date: 02-01- 2026 Next Revision Date: 02-01-2027 a) We will treat all employees fairly and honestly, regardless of where they work. All staff will have agreed terms and conditions in accordance with local law or practice and will be given appropriate job skills training. b) We will pay a fair wage reflecting local markets and conditions. We will always meet any national minimum wage. c) Working hours shall not be excessive. They shall comply with industry guidelines and national standards where they exist. d) We will not employ illegal child labour, forced or bonded labour, forced overtime or condone illegal child labour. e) Employees have the rights of freedom of association and collective bargaining. We respect the right of our employees to choose whether or not to join a trade union without influence or interference from management. Furthermore, we support the right of our employees to exercise that right through a secret ballot. f) We will negotiate in good faith with the properly elected representatives of our employees. g) We will abide by the non-discrimination laws in every country where we operate. h) We will not use or condone the use of corporal punishment, mental or physical coercion or verbal abuse. We have disciplinary procedures for any member of staff whose conduct falls below the required standard. i) We have formal grievance procedures through which staff can raise personal and work- related issues. j) All staff will be given reasonable access to bathroom and rest facilities. 3.3 Data Protection a) We will comply with the relevant principles governing data protection. SECTION 4 CUSTOMER AND COMMUNITY 4.1 Customers Our vision is to transform Road Transport. Landscapers INC wants to lead the way in transforming the way goods are moved for our customers. By aiming for the top in everything that we do, and helping each other, we can deliver the highest levels of safety and service and give greater customer and employee satisfaction. We will share all the success of our Company and reach our destination as the number one transport provider. We will:- act in accordance with fair business, marketing and advertising practices and take all reasonable steps to ensure the safety of our services respect the human rights of our customers – our securi', true, 'manager', 'POL 011', '00', null, null, 'x/H&S-20260923T091746Z-1-001/H&S/Policies/11.Corporate Social Responsibility Policy.pdf', true, '{}'::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, category = excluded.category, content = excluded.content, text = excluded.text, locked = excluded.locked, owner_role = excluded.owner_role, doc_code = excluded.doc_code, revision = excluded.revision, effective_date = excluded.effective_date, review_date = excluded.review_date, _src = excluded._src, _seed = excluded._seed, data = excluded.data where public.docs._seed and public.docs.updated_by is not distinct from public.docs.created_by;
insert into public.docs (id, title, category, content, text, locked, owner_role, doc_code, revision, effective_date, review_date, _src, _seed, data) values ('doc-policy-12-2a317e', 'Personal Protective Equipment Policy', 'policy', '<p>Personal Protective Equipment Policy PPE Policy POL-012 Version: 00 Effective Date: 02-01-2026 Next Revision Date: 02-01-2027</p>
<p>Landscapers INC as a Company remains dedicated to the safety of its employees and identifies that certain risks are associated with its business in the form of Landscaping activities. This Policy is aimed at enforcing all employees involved in the activities of its business to ensure that they are protected at all times.</p>
<p>We are committed to:</p>
<ul><li>Protecting the Health and Safety of our employees within the workplace.</li><li>Protecting the Health and Safety of all who may be affected by our business activities.</li><li>Supply Protective Clothing and Equipment free of charge.</li></ul>
<p>It is the responsibility of every employee:</p>
<ul><li>To ensure that the personal protective equipment issued is worn at the workplace.</li><li>To ensure that the PPE is in a good condition and to report any defective PPE to their supervisor.</li></ul>
<p>Management will conduct regular inspections to ensure that all PPE is fit for purpose/use and replace accordingly.</p>
<p>It is important to note that wearing and the use of PPE is a legal requirement (OHSACT 85 OF 1993:GSR2), and should any employee not comply they will be dealt with in accordance with the Company Disciplinary Code and Procedure.</p>
<h3>APPROVAL</h3>
<p>CEO Initial and Surname Signature Date J.E.Naidoo 02 January 2026</p>', 'Personal Protective Equipment Policy PPE Policy POL-012 Version: 00 Effective Date: 02-01-2026 Next Revision Date: 02-01-2027 Landscapers INC as a Company remains dedicated to the safety of its employees and identifies that certain risks are associated with its business in the form of Landscaping activities. This Policy is aimed at enforcing all employees involved in the activities of its business to ensure that they are protected at all times. We are committed to: Protecting the Health and Safety of our employees within the workplace. Protecting the Health and Safety of all who may be affected by our business activities. Supply Protective Clothing and Equipment free of charge. It is the responsibility of every employee: To ensure that the personal protective equipment issued is worn at the workplace. To ensure that the PPE is in a good condition and to report any defective PPE to their supervisor. Management will conduct regular inspections to ensure that all PPE is fit for purpose/use and replace accordingly. It is important to note that wearing and the use of PPE is a legal requirement (OHSACT 85 OF 1993:GSR2), and should any employee not comply they will be dealt with in accordance with the Company Disciplinary Code and Procedure. APPROVAL CEO Initial and Surname Signature Date J.E.Naidoo 02 January 2026', true, 'manager', 'POL 012', '00', '2026-01-02', '2027-01-02', 'x/H&S-20260923T091746Z-1-001/H&S/Policies/12.Personal Protective Equipment Policy.pdf', true, '{}'::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, category = excluded.category, content = excluded.content, text = excluded.text, locked = excluded.locked, owner_role = excluded.owner_role, doc_code = excluded.doc_code, revision = excluded.revision, effective_date = excluded.effective_date, review_date = excluded.review_date, _src = excluded._src, _seed = excluded._seed, data = excluded.data where public.docs._seed and public.docs.updated_by is not distinct from public.docs.created_by;
insert into public.docs (id, title, category, content, text, locked, owner_role, doc_code, revision, effective_date, review_date, _src, _seed, data) values ('doc-policy-13-a10cae', 'HIV Policy', 'policy', '<p>HIV Policy PURPOSE This policy aims to develop a sense of responsibility within the Role players of Landscapers INC - being the Employers, Employees and Unions, the families and communities in which employees live. SCOPE The Role players will strive to address the economic and social conditions under which the employees live, which may increase the risk of HIV infection.</p>
<p>In all matters relating to HIV/AIDS the Role players will deal fairly and justly with employees who are living with this epidemic, in accordance with constitutional, regulatory and employment equity principles. The AIDS programme is a joint responsibility of the Role players (Employers, Employees and Unions), who must UNITE their efforts to deal with this responsibly.</p>
<h3>POLICY STATEMENT</h3>
<h3>UNDERLYING PRINCIPLES</h3>
<p>This HIV/AIDS policy is based on the recognition of six principles underlying this document. The Role players must be able to cope with the additional burden likely to be placed on it by HIV/AIDS and the steps taken to contain it.</p>
<p>The Role players will endeavour to manage this epidemic and reduce the effect on and consequences of HIV/AIDS for the Role player The Role players will endeavour to promote understanding of and lifelong learning about the HIV/AIDS epidemic.</p>
<p>All Role players commit to this policy, its implementation and sustainability. In all dealings regarding HIV/AIDS, the Role players will aim to balance the needs of people with HIV/AIDS against other employees as well as those of the employer. The particular role of STDs in the HIV epidemic shall be recognized by the Role players. The Role players will endeavour to provide all employees with access to STD counselling and treatment and to educate them about the links between STDs and HIV.</p>
<p>If the Role players cannot provide STD services directly, they will attempt to assist employees to obtain such services in the community (e.g. by referral of employees to the local Clinic.) DEALING WITH HIV/AIDS IN THE WORKPLACE NON-DISCRIMINATION</p>
<ul><li>The Role players will not unfairly discriminate against any employees, or applicants for jobs, on the basis of their actual or suspended HIV status, or that of their relatives. Any employees with HIV/AIDS will receive the same consideration in the working environment while symptom free, as any other able-bodied employee. Once the person becomes HIV-ill, the employee will receive the same consideration as any employee with any other illness or incapacity.</li><li>The Role players commit themselves to dealing with employees with HIV/AIDS in the same way as they deal with all other employees. Unfair discrimination, insensitivity, or rejection of employee’s known to have HIV/AIDS will not be tolerated. Disciplinary action will be taken against those who breach these Policy Guidelines.</li><li>Employees with HIV /AIDS will be afforded the usual opportunities for career development, such as training, conference attendance as well as promotion opportunities</li><li>There will be no unfair discrimination towards employees with HIV or unfair withholding of such opportunities.</li><li>An employee with HIV/AIDS is expected to meet the same performance requirements that apply to other employees, with reasonable, negotiated accommodation, as described in the Labour Relations Act, regarding incapacity.</li></ul>
<p>HIV Policy POL 013 Version: 00 Effective Date:</p>
<p>Next Revision Date:</p>
<h3>02-01-2026</h3>
<h3>02-01-2027</h3>
<h3>HIV TESTING/ SCREENING OF EMPLOYEES AND PROSPECTIVE CANDIDATES</h3>
<ul><li>In line with the principle of non-discrimination, employees and applicants for employment will NOT be required:</li><li>To have an HIV test, whether this is for employment, promotion or merit assessment.</li><li>To disclose their own HIV status, or any member of the family’s HIV status.</li></ul>
<p>Further, standard medical tests will not include HIV testing.</p>
<ul><li>When an employee is too ill to continue working, he/she can be medically boarded after consultation with relevant human resources and medical personnel. This is done in terms of standard boarding procedures.</li><li>All employees may be required to undergo periodic or regular medical check-ups by the employer or by Law, where this relates to an inherent requirement of the job. However, such tests exclude HIV testing.</li></ul>
<h3>BENEFITS</h3>
<ul><li>Employees with HIV/AIDS will be eligible for all the standard employee benefits.</li><li>An HIV test shall not be required for admission to any sector-controlled benefit scheme.</li></ul>
<p>However, certain non-sector benefit schemes may require pre-benefit HIV testing, resulting in limitations, exclusions or premium loading. In this case, the Role players will inform employees accordingly.</p>
<ul><li>The employer shall endeavour to use agencies that offer the most favourable benefits, and that are, as far as possible, viable and non-discriminatory, balancing the interests of all parties.</li></ul>
<h3>CONFIDENTIALITY</h3>
<p>This right to privacy is a basic human right and also applicable to the employment situation. The following broad guidelines should assist the Role players in dealing with sensitive matters, and specifically with HIV/AIDS, STDs, and other personal or health issues.</p>
<ul><li>The road freight sector is committed to a workplace in which rights and obligations are recognized and where confidentiality and privacy are maintained.</li><li>An employee who is aware that he/she has HIV/AIDS is not required to disclose his/her HIV/AIDS diagnosis to anyone in the work place or to any Role players at any stage.</li><li>The employee may only be required to furnish the employer with a report on the fitness of the employee to do his/her normal job (i.e. Medical personnel can only report on the employee’s fitness to work, and not the actual diagnosis).</li><li>All personal details of all employees, including the actual or suspected HIV status of any employee, shall be strictly confidential.</li><li>Any information about an employee’s HIV status shall be revealed only with their express written consent, after counselling regarding the rights of and consequences for the employee.</li><li>If an employee with HIV/AIDS decides to disclose his/her diagnosis to a colleague, supervisor or manager, that person should take all reasonable measures to ensure that this information remains private and confidential.</li><li>The Role players will not tolerate any breaches of confidentiality. Any employee who breaches such confidentiality shall be subject to appropriate disciplinary measures, which could include dismissal.</li></ul>
<h3>REFUSAL TO WORK WITH EMPLOYEES HIV/AIDS</h3>
<ul><li>No employee shall be entitled to refuse to work with an employee with HIV/AIDS. Any prejudice or discrimination will be dealt with through established disciplinary procedures.</li><li>In the event of an employee/s, refusing to work with someone who is suspected to be, or is known to have HIV/AIDS, suitable persons within the Role players (or consultants) will counsel that employee/s.</li></ul>
<p>HIV Policy POL 013 Version: 00 Effective Date:</p>
<p>Next Revision Date:</p>
<h3>02-01-2026</h3>
<h3>02-01-2027</h3>
<ul><li>If after such efforts the employee/s still refuses to work alongside someone who has HIV/AIDS, then appropriate disciplinary action may be taken against the employee/s.</li></ul>
<h3>COUNSELING AND SUPPORT OF EMPLOYEES WITH HIV/AIDS</h3>
<ul><li>The employer will endeavour to provide all employees with HIV/AIDS with access to suitable counselling. This may be done either through the employer, or via referral to suitable community- based services.</li><li>Where possible, this counselling will also be available for employees whose partners may have HIV/AIDS, or who may have other infected family members.</li><li>The Role players will endeavour to assist employees needing hospital, hospice or other such facilities. To this end, the Role players may contribute donations to such facilities with a view to making them more accessible to employees and their families.</li><li>There are certain measures, which may extend the life span of a person with HIV (e.g. good health care and nutrition). To this end, the Role players will endeavour to provide employees with HIV/AIDS with nutritional supplements (e.g. vitamins) and prophylactic medication where this is feasible. Alternatively, the Role players will make employees aware of the existence and availability of such items, and possibly assist them with gaining access to these.</li></ul>
<h3>GRIEVANCE AND DISCIPLINARY PROCEDURES</h3>
<ul><li>An employee with HIV has the same rights, duties and responsibilities and shall be subject to the same rules of disciplinary and performance standards. They will be subject to reasonable accommodation as with all forms of illness or incapacity among all employees.</li><li>Where discrimination or prejudice occurs in consequence of an employee’s HIV status, that employee shall have recourse to the usual mechanisms and remedies for redress both with respect to internal remedies and in terms of labour legislation TERMINATION OF EMPLOYMENT DUE TO ILL HEALTH</li><li>Employees who contract HIV whilst in employment will continue in such employment until medically unfit to work.</li><li>No employee shall be dismissed solely on the basis of HIV.</li><li>As with other severe life limiting or life threatening conditions and permanent disability / employment may only be terminated as a result of HIV/AIDS if and when:</li><li>An employee is unable to carry out their normal work function or if the employee is unable to attend the required number of working days (provided all the sick leave and other  appropriate leave is fully utilized).</li><li>Incapacity to do the job will be the major criterion for boarding employees or dismissal. This will be dealt with in accordance with the schedules to the LRA regarding incapacity (e.g. finding suitable alternative employment).</li><li>HIV will not be a reason for unilateral medical boarding and the HIV status of an employee shall not play a role in retrenchment procedures WORKPLACE AIDS PROGRAMME INTRODUCTION The Trucking Against AIDS (TAA) programme is an initiative of the NBC RFI with the purpose of providing, supporting and maintaining an AIDS service infrastructure to Role players, where feasible. The TAA programme is committed to making the programme accessible to the Role players, who can draw upon the TAA manpower, services and expertise.</li></ul>
<h3>EDUCATION AND TRAINING</h3>
<ul><li>HIV/AIDS has given rise to unnecessary fears and concerns in workplace settings.</li><li>Education in the workplace will be provided to ensure that all employees under-stand:</li></ul>
<p>HIV Policy POL 013 Version: 00 Effective Date:</p>
<p>Next Revision Date:</p>
<h3>02-01-2026</h3>
<h3>02-01-2027</h3>
<ul><li>The facts on HIV/AIDS and STDs and the routes of transmission.</li><li>Their own personal risk of infection with HIV and STD’s.</li><li>Their legal rights with regard to HIV and AIDS, and the rights and duties of people with HIV and AIDS.</li><li>Role player’s policy with regard to HIV and AIDS.</li></ul>
<ul><li>In order to provide the most suitable education, the Role players will endeavour to train peer educators who will educate employees about HIV/AIDS and related issues.</li><li>This education will take place at times most suitable to the employer. Where possible, Peer Educators will be used to educate employees during and/or after working hours.</li><li>To ensure that all employees have access to this information, these educational sessions should be compulsory and in a language and educational level appropriate to each group of employees.</li></ul>
<h3>VOLUNTARY HIV TESTING</h3>
<p>It is recognized that HIV testing relating to employment is both discriminatory and not effective in keeping HIV out of a company. Therefore, as stated above, HIV testing will not be required.</p>
<p>It is further recognized that there may be employees who would like to know their HIV status for personal reasons, and the Role players will endeavour to make such services available and accessible to employee, either directly or through referral to suitable community-based services. The Role players will endeavour to make employees aware of services for HIV testing, counselling and treatment.</p>
<p>Where feasible, all services used for HIV testing will be selected on the basis that they comply with the HIV Testing guidelines of the South Africa Medical Association (SAMA) (attached here: Appendix 1). The NBC’s ‘Trucking against AIDS’ (TAA) Project Team, or other suitable service, can be consulted if the Role players are uncertain of these SAMA testing issues or any other difficulties relating to testing, counselling and medical care.</p>
<p>There may be certain categories of employment where an HIV test may become necessary. In such cases, the personnel department, the employee and medical staff will take the decision, ensuring that this is in line with all current legislation and the SAMA guidelines. The Role players will endeavour to make employees aware of services for HIV testing, counselling and treatment.</p>
<h3>INFECTION CONTROL AT WORK</h3>
<ul><li>All employees will be informed of essential infection control procedures, and, in particular, key employees will be trained to deal with workplace injuries.</li><li>Accessible and well-stocked first aid and infection control kits will be available throughout the workplace and in vehicles. These will include rubber gloves and resuscitation pieces.</li><li>Employees will be informed about this equipment and trained in its correct use.</li></ul>
<p>HIV Policy POL 013 Version: 00 Effective Date:</p>
<p>Next Revision Date:</p>
<h3>02-01-2026</h3>
<h3>02-01-2027</h3>
<h3>STD CARE AT WORK</h3>
<ul><li>The particular role of STDs in the HIV epidemic shall be recognized and all employees shall have access to STD counselling and treatment. During this counselling, the links between STDs and HIV will be fully explained.</li><li>Employees with STDs need effective, up-to-date (i.e. ‘Syndromic’) treatment via doctors or clinics.</li></ul>
<p>If the Role players cannot provide such STD services, then the Role players shall endeavour to make such services accessible to all employees, e.g. by referring employees to local services.</p>
<p>PLANNING, SUSTAINABILITY AND INNOVATION</p>
<ul><li>There is a legacy of failed AIDS interventions and the Role players must be committed to ensuring that the policies and programmes arising out of this document are successful.</li></ul>
<p>Monitoring and accountability should therefore be built into the programme.</p>
<ul><li>To this end a sufficient budget will be necessary to ensure that all the provisions as stated in the policy – access to voluntary testing, counselling, infection control, condoms, STD treatment, education and care – can be implemented and sustained.</li></ul>
<h3>RESPONSIBILITIES</h3>
<p>Compliance, monitoring and review Employers are responsible for keeping the workplace safe.</p>
<p>Employees must take an active part in keeping the workplace safe by complying with safety standards, rules and regulations.</p>
<p>The Role players will endeavour to manage this epidemic and reduce the effect on and consequences of HIV/AIDS for the Role player The Role players will endeavour to promote understanding of and lifelong learning about the HIV/AIDS epidemic.</p>
<p>All Role players commit to this policy, its implementation and sustainability. RELATED LEGISLATION AND DOCUMENTS</p>
<p>Occupational Health &amp; Safety Act (85 OF 1993) National Road Traffic Act 93 of 1996 FEEDBACK</p>
<p>The nature of the AIDS epidemic, the law, employee benefit schemes and medical expertise are constantly evolving. Therefore, this policy will be reviewed annually or as needed, to ensure that it keeps pace with any new developments.</p>
<p>The Policy shall be made available to all stakeholders on request and be prominently displayed within the organisation. The SHERQ Management System will form an integral part of Landscapers INC’s core business processes. The responsibility of management and all employees to implement this policy effectively ranks equally with their respective responsibilities for services, maintenance, quality, sales, costs, profitability, and similar matters of vital importance to the success of Landscapers INC.</p>
<p>According to the HPCSA Guidelines for Good Practice in the Health care professions The following are elements of informed consent:</p>
<ul><li>Consent must be voluntary and without constraint;</li><li>In the case of a HIV test, consent should preferably be written, although consent may be</li><li>implied;</li><li>Consent must not conflict with good morals or the Constitution;</li><li>The patient must be capable of consenting;</li><li>The patient must give the consent personally, unless proxy consent is applicable;</li><li>The patient should know why the medical practitioner needs the results of the test;</li><li>There should be sufficient information on the diagnosis, proposed treatment, expected</li><li>benefits, risks, alternative treatment, probable results, etc.;</li></ul>
<h3>APPROVAL</h3>
<p>CEO Initial and Surname Signature Date J.E.Naidoo 02 January 2026</p>', 'HIV Policy PURPOSE This policy aims to develop a sense of responsibility within the Role players of Landscapers INC - being the Employers, Employees and Unions, the families and communities in which employees live. SCOPE The Role players will strive to address the economic and social conditions under which the employees live, which may increase the risk of HIV infection. In all matters relating to HIV/AIDS the Role players will deal fairly and justly with employees who are living with this epidemic, in accordance with constitutional, regulatory and employment equity principles. The AIDS programme is a joint responsibility of the Role players (Employers, Employees and Unions), who must UNITE their efforts to deal with this responsibly. POLICY STATEMENT UNDERLYING PRINCIPLES This HIV/AIDS policy is based on the recognition of six principles underlying this document. The Role players must be able to cope with the additional burden likely to be placed on it by HIV/AIDS and the steps taken to contain it. The Role players will endeavour to manage this epidemic and reduce the effect on and consequences of HIV/AIDS for the Role player The Role players will endeavour to promote understanding of and lifelong learning about the HIV/AIDS epidemic. All Role players commit to this policy, its implementation and sustainability. In all dealings regarding HIV/AIDS, the Role players will aim to balance the needs of people with HIV/AIDS against other employees as well as those of the employer. The particular role of STDs in the HIV epidemic shall be recognized by the Role players. The Role players will endeavour to provide all employees with access to STD counselling and treatment and to educate them about the links between STDs and HIV. If the Role players cannot provide STD services directly, they will attempt to assist employees to obtain such services in the community (e.g. by referral of employees to the local Clinic.) DEALING WITH HIV/AIDS IN THE WORKPLACE NON-DISCRIMINATION The Role players will not unfairly discriminate against any employees, or applicants for jobs, on the basis of their actual or suspended HIV status, or that of their relatives. Any employees with HIV/AIDS will receive the same consideration in the working environment while symptom free, as any other able-bodied employee. Once the person becomes HIV-ill, the employee will receive the same consideration as any employee with any other illness or incapacity. The Role players commit themselves to dealing with employees with HIV/AIDS in the same way as they deal with all other employees. Unfair discrimination, insensitivity, or rejection of employee’s known to have HIV/AIDS will not be tolerated. Disciplinary action will be taken against those who breach these Policy Guidelines. Employees with HIV /AIDS will be afforded the usual opportunities for career development, such as training, conference attendance as well as promotion opportunities There will be no unfair discrimination towards employees with HIV or unfair withholding of such opportunities. An employee with HIV/AIDS is expected to meet the same performance requirements that apply to other employees, with reasonable, negotiated accommodation, as described in the Labour Relations Act, regarding incapacity. HIV Policy POL 013 Version: 00 Effective Date: Next Revision Date: 02-01-2026 02-01-2027 HIV TESTING/ SCREENING OF EMPLOYEES AND PROSPECTIVE CANDIDATES In line with the principle of non-discrimination, employees and applicants for employment will NOT be required: To have an HIV test, whether this is for employment, promotion or merit assessment. To disclose their own HIV status, or any member of the family’s HIV status. Further, standard medical tests will not include HIV testing. When an employee is too ill to continue working, he/she can be medically boarded after consultation with relevant human resources and medical personnel. This is done in terms of standard boarding procedures. All employees may be required to undergo periodic or regular medical check-ups by the employer or by Law, where this relates to an inherent requirement of the job. However, such tests exclude HIV testing. BENEFITS Employees with HIV/AIDS will be eligible for all the standard employee benefits. An HIV test shall not be required for admission to any sector-controlled benefit scheme. However, certain non-sector benefit schemes may require pre-benefit HIV testing, resulting in limitations, exclusions or premium loading. In this case, the Role players will inform employees accordingly. The employer shall endeavour to use agencies that offer the most favourable benefits, and that are, as far as possible, viable and non-discriminatory, balancing the interests of all parties. CONFIDENTIALITY This right to privacy is a basic human right and also applicable to the employment situation. The following broad guidelines should assist the Role players in dealing with sensitive matters, and specifically with HIV/AIDS, STDs, and other personal or health issues. The road freight sector is committed to a workplace in which rights and obligations are recognized and where confidentiality and privacy are maintained. An employee who is aware that he/she has HIV/AIDS is not required to disclose his/her HIV/AIDS diagnosis to anyone in the work place or to any Role players at any stage. The employee may only be required to furnish the employer with a report on the fitness of the employee to do his/her normal job (i.e. Medical personnel can only report on the employee’s fitness to work, and not the actual diagnosis). All personal details of all employees, including the actual or suspected HIV status of any employee, shall be strictly confidential. Any information about an employee’s HIV status shall be revealed only with their express written consent, after counselling regarding the rights of and consequences for the employee. If an employee with HIV/AIDS decides to disclose his/her diagnosis to a colleague, supervisor or manager, that person should take all reasonable measures to ensure that this information remains private and confidential. The Role players will not tolerate any breaches of confidentiality. Any employee who breaches such confidentiality shall be subject to appropriate disciplinary measures, which could include dismissal. REFUSAL TO WORK WITH EMPLOYEES HIV/AIDS No employee shall be entitled to refuse to work with an employee with HIV/AIDS. Any prejudice or discrimination will be dealt with through established disciplinary procedures. In the event of an employee/s, refusing to work with someone who is suspected to be, or is known to have HIV/AIDS, suitable persons within the Role players (or consultants) will counsel that employee/s. HIV Policy POL 013 Version: 00 Effective Date: Next Revision Date: 02-01-2026 02-01-2027 If after such efforts the employee/s still refuses to work alongside someone who has HIV/AIDS, then appropriate disciplinary action may be taken against the employee/s. COUNSELING AND SUPPORT OF EMPLOYEES WITH HIV/AIDS The employer will endeavour to provide all employees with HIV/AIDS with access to suitable counselling. This may be done either through the employer, or via referral to suitable community- based services. Where possible, this counselling will also be available for employees whose partners may have HIV/AIDS, or who may have other infected family members. The Role players will endeavour to assist employees needing hospital, hospice or other such facilities. To this end, the Role players may contribute donations to such facilities with a view to making them more accessible to employees and their families. There are certain measures, which may extend the life span of a person with HIV (e.g. good health care and nutrition). To this end, the Role players will endeavour to provide employees with HIV/AIDS with nutritional supplements (e.g. vitamins) and prophylactic medication where this is feasible. Alternatively, the Role players will make employees aware of the existence and availability of such items, and possibly assist them with gaining access to these. GRIEVANCE AND DISCIPLINARY PROCEDURES An employee with HIV has the same rights, duties and responsibilities and shall be subject to the same rules of disciplinary and performance standards. They will be subject to reasonable accommodation as with all forms of illness or incapacity among all employees. Where discrimination or prejudice occurs in consequence of an employee’s HIV status, that employee shall have recourse to the usual mechanisms and remedies for redress both with respect to internal remedies and in terms of labour legislation TERMINATION OF EMPLOYMENT DUE TO ILL HEALTH Employees who contract HIV whilst in employment will continue in such employment until medically unfit to work. No employee shall be dismissed solely on the basis of HIV. As with other severe life limiting or life threatening conditions and permanent disability / employment may only be terminated as a result of HIV/AIDS if and when: An employee is unable to carry out their normal work function or if the employee is unable to attend the required number of working days (provided all the sick leave and other appropriate leave is fully utilized). Incapacity to do the job will be the major criterion for boarding employees or dismissal. This will be dealt with in accordance with the schedules to the LRA regarding incapacity (e.g. finding suitable alternative employment). HIV will not be a reason for unilateral medical boarding and the HIV status of an employee shall not play a role in retrenchment procedures WORKPLACE AIDS PROGRAMME INTRODUCTION The Trucking Against AIDS (TAA) programme is an initiative of the NBC RFI with the purpose of providing, supporting and maintaining an AIDS service infrastructure to Role players, where feasible. The TAA programme is committed to making the programme accessible to the Role players, who can draw upon the TAA manpower, services and expertise. EDUCATION AND TRAINING HIV/AIDS has given rise to unnecessary fears and concerns in workplace settings. Education in the workplace will be provided to ensure that all employees under-stand: HIV Policy POL 013 Version: 00 Effective Date: Next Revision Date: 02-01-2026 02-01-2027 The facts on HIV/AIDS and STDs and the routes of transmission. Their own personal risk of infection with HIV and STD’s. Their legal rights with regard to HIV and AIDS, and the rights and duties of people with HIV and AIDS. Role player’s policy with regard to HIV and AIDS. In order to provide the most suitable education, the Role players will endeavour to train peer educators who will educate employees about HIV/AIDS and related issues. This education will take place at times most suitable to the employer. Where possible, Peer Educators will be used to educate employees during and/or after working hours. To ensure that all employees have access to this information, these educational sessions should be compulsory and in a language and educational level appropriate to each group of employees. VOLUNTARY HIV TESTING It is recognized that HIV testing relating to employment is both discriminatory and not effective in keeping HIV out of a company. Therefore, as stated above, HIV testing will not be required. It is further recognized that there may be employees who would like to know their HIV status for personal reasons, and the Role players will endeavour to make such services available and accessible to employee, either directly or through referral to suitable community-based services. The Role players will endeavour to make employees aware of services for HIV testing, counselling and treatment. Where feasible, all services used for HIV testing will be selected on the basis that they comply with the HIV Testing guidelines of the South Africa Medical Association (SAMA) (attached here: Appendix 1). The NBC’s ‘Trucking against AIDS’ (TAA) Project Team, or other suitable service, can be consulted if the Role players are uncertain of these SAMA testing issues or any other difficulties relating to testing, counselling and medical care. There may be certain categories of employment where an HIV test may become necessary. In such cases, the personnel department, the employee and medical staff will take the decision, ensuring that this is in line with all current legislation and the SAMA guidelines. The Role players will endeavour to make employees aware of services for HIV testing, counselling and treatment. INFECTION CONTROL AT WORK All employees will be informed of essential infection control procedures, and, in particular, key employees will be trained to deal with workplace injuries. Accessible and well-stocked first aid and infection control kits will be available throughout the workplace and in vehicles. These will include rubber gloves and resuscitation pieces. Employees will be informed about this equipment and trained in its correct use. HIV Policy POL 013 Version: 00 Effective Date: Next Revision Date: 02-01-2026 02-01-2027 STD CARE AT WORK The particular role of STDs in the HIV epidemic shall be recognized and all employees shall have access to STD counselling and treatment. During this counselling, the links between STDs and HIV will be fully explained. Employees with STDs need effective, up-to-date (i.e. ‘Syndromic’) treatment via doctors or clinics. If the Role players cannot provide such STD services, then the Role players shall endeavour to make such services accessible to all employees, e.g. by referring employees to local services. PLANNING, SUSTAINABILITY AND INNOVATION There is a legacy of failed AIDS interventions and the Role players must be committed to ensuring that the policies and programmes arising out of this document are successful. Monitoring and accountability should therefore be built into the programme. To this end a sufficient budget will be necessary to ensure that all the provisions as stated in the policy – access to voluntary testing, counselling, infection control, condoms, STD treatment, education and care – can be implemented and sustained. RESPONSIBILITIES Compliance, monitoring and review Employers are responsible for keeping the workplace safe. Employees must take an active part in keeping the workplace safe by complying with safety standards, rules and regulations. The Role players will endeavour to manage this epidemic and reduce the effect on and consequences of HIV/AIDS for the Role player The Role players will endeavour to promote understanding of and lifelong learning about the HIV/AIDS epidemic. All Role players commit to this policy, its implementation and sustainability. RELATED LEGISLATION AND DOCUMENTS Occupational Health & Safety Act (85 OF 1993) National Road Traffic Act 93 of 1996 FEEDBACK The nature of the AIDS epidemic, the law, employee benefit schemes and medical expertise are constantly evolving. Therefore, this policy will be reviewed annually or as needed, to ensure that it keeps pace with any new developments. The Policy shall be made available to all stakeholders on request and be prominently displayed within the organisation. The SHERQ Management System will form an integral part of Landscapers INC’s core business processes. The responsibility of management and all employees to implement this policy effectively ranks equally with their respective responsibilities for services, maintenance, quality, sales, costs, profitability, and similar matters of vital importance to the success of Landscapers INC. According to the HPCSA Guidelines for Good Practice in the Health care professions The following are elements of informed consent: Consent must be voluntary and without constraint; In the case of a HIV test, consent should preferably be written, although consent may be implied; Consent must not conflict with good morals or the Constitution; The patient must be capable of consenting; The patient must give the consent personally, unless proxy consent is applicable; The patient should know why the medical practitioner needs the results of the test; There should be sufficient information on the diagnosis, proposed treatment, expected benefits, risks, alternative treatment, probable results, etc.; APPROVAL CEO Initial and Surname Signature Date J.E.Naidoo 02 January 2026', true, 'manager', 'POL 013', '00', null, null, 'x/H&S-20260923T091746Z-1-001/H&S/Policies/13.HIV Policy.pdf', true, '{}'::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, category = excluded.category, content = excluded.content, text = excluded.text, locked = excluded.locked, owner_role = excluded.owner_role, doc_code = excluded.doc_code, revision = excluded.revision, effective_date = excluded.effective_date, review_date = excluded.review_date, _src = excluded._src, _seed = excluded._seed, data = excluded.data where public.docs._seed and public.docs.updated_by is not distinct from public.docs.created_by;
insert into public.docs (id, title, category, content, text, locked, owner_role, doc_code, revision, effective_date, review_date, _src, _seed, data) values ('doc-policy-14-16569a', 'Equity Policy', 'policy', '<p>Equity Policy Employment Equity Policy POL 014 Version: 00 Effective Date: 02-01-2026 Next Revision Date: 02-01-2027</p>
<h3>PURPOSE</h3>
<p>The purpose of the Employment Equity Policy is to:</p>
<ul><li>Promote the empowerment and advancement of previously disadvantaged employees in order to create racial and gender equity;</li><li>Improve the level of representation of black senior management and in core skills groups; and</li><li>Identify and explore opportunities for employing people with disabilities.</li></ul>
<h3>SCOPE</h3>
<p>This policy is to guide Landscapers INC the importance of diversity in the workplace, that Employment Equity is an imperative business driver and therefore supports the principles and intent of the Employment Equity Act of 1998.</p>
<h3>POLICY STATEMENT</h3>
<p>Landscapers INC is committed to shaping an organizational environment which supports the development of each employee to his/her fullest potential regardless of race, gender, disability, religion, sexual orientation and political persuasion.</p>
<h3>RESPONSIBILITIES</h3>
<p>Landscapers INC will circulate this Employment Equity Policy (and a policy document on disclosure of interests) to employees for their retention.</p>
<p>Records management Staff must maintain all records relevant to administering this policy.</p>
<h3>RELATED LEGISLATION AND DOCUMENTS</h3>
<p>Employment Equity Act, No 55 of 1998 Recruitment Procedure FEEDBACK The Employment Equity Policy will be periodically reviewed to ensure continued relevance and adequacy. Employment Equity Policy shall be made available to all stakeholders on request and be prominently displayed within the organisation. The Employment Equity Policy Management System will form an integral part of the company’s core business processes. The responsibility of management and all employees to implement this policy effectively ranks equally with their respective responsibilities for services, maintenance, quality, sales, costs, profitability, and similar matters of vital importance to the success of the company whilst conforming to the ISO 9001-2015 Code of Practice.</p>
<h3>APPROVAL</h3>
<p>CEO Initial and Surname Signature Date J.E.Naidoo 02 January 2026</p>', 'Equity Policy Employment Equity Policy POL 014 Version: 00 Effective Date: 02-01-2026 Next Revision Date: 02-01-2027 PURPOSE The purpose of the Employment Equity Policy is to: Promote the empowerment and advancement of previously disadvantaged employees in order to create racial and gender equity; Improve the level of representation of black senior management and in core skills groups; and Identify and explore opportunities for employing people with disabilities. SCOPE This policy is to guide Landscapers INC the importance of diversity in the workplace, that Employment Equity is an imperative business driver and therefore supports the principles and intent of the Employment Equity Act of 1998. POLICY STATEMENT Landscapers INC is committed to shaping an organizational environment which supports the development of each employee to his/her fullest potential regardless of race, gender, disability, religion, sexual orientation and political persuasion. RESPONSIBILITIES Landscapers INC will circulate this Employment Equity Policy (and a policy document on disclosure of interests) to employees for their retention. Records management Staff must maintain all records relevant to administering this policy. RELATED LEGISLATION AND DOCUMENTS Employment Equity Act, No 55 of 1998 Recruitment Procedure FEEDBACK The Employment Equity Policy will be periodically reviewed to ensure continued relevance and adequacy. Employment Equity Policy shall be made available to all stakeholders on request and be prominently displayed within the organisation. The Employment Equity Policy Management System will form an integral part of the company’s core business processes. The responsibility of management and all employees to implement this policy effectively ranks equally with their respective responsibilities for services, maintenance, quality, sales, costs, profitability, and similar matters of vital importance to the success of the company whilst conforming to the ISO 9001-2015 Code of Practice. APPROVAL CEO Initial and Surname Signature Date J.E.Naidoo 02 January 2026', true, 'manager', 'POL 014', '00', '2026-01-02', '2027-01-02', 'x/H&S-20260923T091746Z-1-001/H&S/Policies/14.Equity Policy.pdf', true, '{}'::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, category = excluded.category, content = excluded.content, text = excluded.text, locked = excluded.locked, owner_role = excluded.owner_role, doc_code = excluded.doc_code, revision = excluded.revision, effective_date = excluded.effective_date, review_date = excluded.review_date, _src = excluded._src, _seed = excluded._seed, data = excluded.data where public.docs._seed and public.docs.updated_by is not distinct from public.docs.created_by;
insert into public.docs (id, title, category, content, text, locked, owner_role, doc_code, revision, effective_date, review_date, _src, _seed, data) values ('doc-policy-15-fbf845', 'Cell Phone Policy', 'policy', '<p>Revision:0</p>
<p>Cell Phone Policy</p>
<p>Purpose The purpose of this policy is to ensure that employees are aware of the rules and regulations to be followed whilst using company and personal cell phones during working hours.</p>
<p>Scope This policy applies to all employees at Landscapers INC.</p>
<p>Policy Guidelines The following are Landscapers INC guidelines for proper employee cell phone use during working hours. In general, cell phones should not be used when they could pose a security or safety risk , or when they distract from work tasks.</p>
<ul><li>Cell phone use by drivers is strictly prohibited while the vehicle is in motion. Drivers should stop their vehicle in a safe location off the road and away from traffic to engage in information taking. If it is not possible to pull over to the side of the road due to safety related issues, then the driver’s assistant (if and where applicable) must engage in information taking.</li><li>Cell phone use is not the only factor that can increase the safety risk of operating a motor vehicle. Drivers are also discouraged from other forms of “multi-tasking” (e.g.</li></ul>
<p>eating, reading, texting, taking notes), as these all impair the driver’s ability to operate the vehicle safely. Proper sleep and alertness are also a prerequisite to safe driving, so only drive when physically capable of doing so safely.</p>
<ul><li>Never use a cell phone whilst operating any machine handling equipment.</li><li>Employees are not allowed to play games, be on social media etc. on the mobile phones during working hours with the exception during your tea and lunch breaks.</li><li>Use of earphones and headphones are strictly prohibited during working hours.</li><li>The use of camera, video recording or audio recording on company premises and during working hours is strictly prohibited without the express prior permission from Senior Management and of the person/s subject to recording.</li></ul>
<p>Cell Phone Policy Effective Date: 02-01-2025 POL 015 Next Revision Date: 02-01-2026</p>
<p>Version: 00</p>
<ul><li>Landscapers INC understands that there are exceptions at the discretions of Management.</li><li>Cell phones should be turned off or set on silent mode or vibrate mode during meetings or in any circumstance where incoming calls may be disruptive.</li><li>Landscapers INC does not hold any responsibility for the loss of personal devices brought to the workplace.</li><li>The company prohibits the use of mobile phones in any area where using a mobile phone would be unsafe and would be distracting for the user or may create an unsafe working environment.</li><li>Disciplinary measures will be implemented should fail to follow the above.</li><li>This guideline supports the zero-incident culture we aim to achieve in our quest for world class Operational Excellence. We are committed to the safety of our colleagues.</li></ul>
<p>Approval</p>
<p>CEO Initial and Surname Signature Date J.E.NAIDOO 02 January 2026</p>
<p>Cell Phone Policy POL 015 Effective Date: 02-01-2026 Next Revision Date: 02-01-2027</p>', 'Revision:0 Cell Phone Policy Purpose The purpose of this policy is to ensure that employees are aware of the rules and regulations to be followed whilst using company and personal cell phones during working hours. Scope This policy applies to all employees at Landscapers INC. Policy Guidelines The following are Landscapers INC guidelines for proper employee cell phone use during working hours. In general, cell phones should not be used when they could pose a security or safety risk , or when they distract from work tasks. Cell phone use by drivers is strictly prohibited while the vehicle is in motion. Drivers should stop their vehicle in a safe location off the road and away from traffic to engage in information taking. If it is not possible to pull over to the side of the road due to safety related issues, then the driver’s assistant (if and where applicable) must engage in information taking. Cell phone use is not the only factor that can increase the safety risk of operating a motor vehicle. Drivers are also discouraged from other forms of “multi-tasking” (e.g. eating, reading, texting, taking notes), as these all impair the driver’s ability to operate the vehicle safely. Proper sleep and alertness are also a prerequisite to safe driving, so only drive when physically capable of doing so safely. Never use a cell phone whilst operating any machine handling equipment. Employees are not allowed to play games, be on social media etc. on the mobile phones during working hours with the exception during your tea and lunch breaks. Use of earphones and headphones are strictly prohibited during working hours. The use of camera, video recording or audio recording on company premises and during working hours is strictly prohibited without the express prior permission from Senior Management and of the person/s subject to recording. Cell Phone Policy Effective Date: 02-01-2025 POL 015 Next Revision Date: 02-01-2026 Version: 00 Landscapers INC understands that there are exceptions at the discretions of Management. Cell phones should be turned off or set on silent mode or vibrate mode during meetings or in any circumstance where incoming calls may be disruptive. Landscapers INC does not hold any responsibility for the loss of personal devices brought to the workplace. The company prohibits the use of mobile phones in any area where using a mobile phone would be unsafe and would be distracting for the user or may create an unsafe working environment. Disciplinary measures will be implemented should fail to follow the above. This guideline supports the zero-incident culture we aim to achieve in our quest for world class Operational Excellence. We are committed to the safety of our colleagues. Approval CEO Initial and Surname Signature Date J.E.NAIDOO 02 January 2026 Cell Phone Policy POL 015 Effective Date: 02-01-2026 Next Revision Date: 02-01-2027', true, 'manager', 'POL 015', '00', '2025-01-02', '2026-01-02', 'x/H&S-20260923T091746Z-1-001/H&S/Policies/15.Cell Phone Policy.pdf', true, '{}'::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, category = excluded.category, content = excluded.content, text = excluded.text, locked = excluded.locked, owner_role = excluded.owner_role, doc_code = excluded.doc_code, revision = excluded.revision, effective_date = excluded.effective_date, review_date = excluded.review_date, _src = excluded._src, _seed = excluded._seed, data = excluded.data where public.docs._seed and public.docs.updated_by is not distinct from public.docs.created_by;
insert into public.docs (id, title, category, content, text, locked, owner_role, doc_code, revision, effective_date, review_date, _src, _seed, data) values ('doc-policy-16-99ab5e', 'Environmental Policy', 'policy', '<p>Environmental Policy PURPOSE The purpose of this policy is to establish our environmental objectives.</p>
<h3>SCOPE</h3>
<p>This policy applies to all Landscapers INC operations both on-site and off-site undertakings, covering transport and logistics.</p>
<h3>POLICY STATEMENT</h3>
<p>Landscapers INC understands the importance of managing risk related to environmental standards and is committed to maintain Management Systems and preventative controls in an effort to minimise exposure to anything, which may harm the environment.</p>
<p>By this means, Landscapers INC undertakes to: -</p>
<ul><li>Identify customer needs and provide a quality service that will satisfy these needs and adopt a responsibility of caring for the environment in all its activities and operations.</li><li>Promote the principle of sustainable development in the conduct of our operations and strive for continuous improvement in Health Safety &amp; Environmental performance.</li><li>Conduct our operations in a manner that minimises adverse environmental impacts and protects the health &amp; safety of our employees and the public.</li><li>Endeavour at all times to work within legal requirements of the National Environmental Management Act (Act 107 of 1998), and other relevant legal regulations and compliance with relevant environmental legislation and regulations directly applicable to the environmental aspects of our activities through all levels of local authorities and governmental organisations.</li><li>Adopt total Quality and Environmental management as a way of life and instil this as a culture within the Company through ongoing training, awareness programmes and a Quality Management System.</li><li>Work with suppliers to ensure that they assist us in meeting our quality standards and sharing environmental experiences.</li><li>Use the Quality and Environmental Management System to drive down costs by streamlining processes &amp; reducing wastage, thereby improving profitability and commitment to recovery and recycling as opposed to disposal where feasible.</li><li>Encourage, and assist where feasible, Contractors to comply with its ISO and SHERQ requirements and safeguard the reputation of Landscapers INC, especially regarding environmental concerns and issues.</li><li>Evaluate and review all operational activities to ensure that the environmental-related requirements of ISO and SHERQ systems are met and complied with.</li></ul>
<p>Measure our Carbon Footprint &amp; implement new ways to reduce this as much as possible. RESPONSIBILITIES Compliance, monitoring and review Landscapers INC shall establish and define:</p>
<p>(a) Roles, responsibilities and authority in order to facilitate effective environmental management. (b) Resources essential to the implementation and control of the environmental system, and provide them.</p>
<p>(c) Resources to include human, specialised skills, technological and financial and shall include defined roles and authorities for ensuring all the environmental management systems are established and implemented, and to provide reports of compliance.</p>
<p>(d) That the Managing Director ensures that the company’s environmental policy and procedures are fully documented, complied with and kept under review to meet with current requirements and legislation.</p>
<p>Reporting No additional reporting is required.</p>
<p>Records management Staff must maintain all records relevant to administering this policy in a recognised Landscapers INC recordkeeping system RELATED LEGISLATION AND DOCUMENTS</p>
<p>National Environmental Management Act (Act 107 of 1998) Occupational Health and Safety Act (85 of 1993) THE ENVIRONMENT CONSERVATION ACT, 1989 (ACT 73 OF 1989) DWAF</p>
<h3>APPROVAL</h3>
<p>CEO Initial and Surname Signature Date J.E.Naidoo 02 January 2026</p>
<p>Environmental Policy Effective Date:</p>
<h3>02-01-2026</h3>
<h3>POL 016</h3>
<p>Next Revision Date:</p>
<h3>02-01-2027</h3>
<p>Version: 00</p>', 'Environmental Policy PURPOSE The purpose of this policy is to establish our environmental objectives. SCOPE This policy applies to all Landscapers INC operations both on-site and off-site undertakings, covering transport and logistics. POLICY STATEMENT Landscapers INC understands the importance of managing risk related to environmental standards and is committed to maintain Management Systems and preventative controls in an effort to minimise exposure to anything, which may harm the environment. By this means, Landscapers INC undertakes to: - Identify customer needs and provide a quality service that will satisfy these needs and adopt a responsibility of caring for the environment in all its activities and operations. Promote the principle of sustainable development in the conduct of our operations and strive for continuous improvement in Health Safety & Environmental performance. Conduct our operations in a manner that minimises adverse environmental impacts and protects the health & safety of our employees and the public. Endeavour at all times to work within legal requirements of the National Environmental Management Act (Act 107 of 1998), and other relevant legal regulations and compliance with relevant environmental legislation and regulations directly applicable to the environmental aspects of our activities through all levels of local authorities and governmental organisations. Adopt total Quality and Environmental management as a way of life and instil this as a culture within the Company through ongoing training, awareness programmes and a Quality Management System. Work with suppliers to ensure that they assist us in meeting our quality standards and sharing environmental experiences. Use the Quality and Environmental Management System to drive down costs by streamlining processes & reducing wastage, thereby improving profitability and commitment to recovery and recycling as opposed to disposal where feasible. Encourage, and assist where feasible, Contractors to comply with its ISO and SHERQ requirements and safeguard the reputation of Landscapers INC, especially regarding environmental concerns and issues. Evaluate and review all operational activities to ensure that the environmental-related requirements of ISO and SHERQ systems are met and complied with. Measure our Carbon Footprint & implement new ways to reduce this as much as possible. RESPONSIBILITIES Compliance, monitoring and review Landscapers INC shall establish and define: (a) Roles, responsibilities and authority in order to facilitate effective environmental management. (b) Resources essential to the implementation and control of the environmental system, and provide them. (c) Resources to include human, specialised skills, technological and financial and shall include defined roles and authorities for ensuring all the environmental management systems are established and implemented, and to provide reports of compliance. (d) That the Managing Director ensures that the company’s environmental policy and procedures are fully documented, complied with and kept under review to meet with current requirements and legislation. Reporting No additional reporting is required. Records management Staff must maintain all records relevant to administering this policy in a recognised Landscapers INC recordkeeping system RELATED LEGISLATION AND DOCUMENTS National Environmental Management Act (Act 107 of 1998) Occupational Health and Safety Act (85 of 1993) THE ENVIRONMENT CONSERVATION ACT, 1989 (ACT 73 OF 1989) DWAF APPROVAL CEO Initial and Surname Signature Date J.E.Naidoo 02 January 2026 Environmental Policy Effective Date: 02-01-2026 POL 016 Next Revision Date: 02-01-2027 Version: 00', true, 'manager', 'POL 016', '00', null, null, 'x/H&S-20260923T091746Z-1-001/H&S/Policies/16.Environmental Policy.pdf', true, '{}'::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, category = excluded.category, content = excluded.content, text = excluded.text, locked = excluded.locked, owner_role = excluded.owner_role, doc_code = excluded.doc_code, revision = excluded.revision, effective_date = excluded.effective_date, review_date = excluded.review_date, _src = excluded._src, _seed = excluded._seed, data = excluded.data where public.docs._seed and public.docs.updated_by is not distinct from public.docs.created_by;
insert into public.docs (id, title, category, content, text, locked, owner_role, doc_code, revision, effective_date, review_date, _src, _seed, data) values ('doc-policy-2-25a715', 'Smoking Policy', 'policy', '<p>Smoking Policy Effective Date: 02-01-2026 POL 002 Next Revision Date: 02-01-2026 Version: 00</p>
<p>Smoking Policy</p>
<p>Policy Motivation Landscapers INC acknowledges that medical evidence has shown that smoking is detrimental to the health of both active and passive smokers. Smoking cigarettes, cigars and pipes is also an irritation to many non-smokers and can aggravate various allergic conditions.</p>
<p>Policy Objective The objective of the above policy is to establish a pleasant and healthy working environment, considering the needs and rights of both smokers and non-smokers.</p>
<p>Policy Statement Landscapers INCacknowledges that its employees and clients have the right to work and do business in a pleasant environment with the cleanest air possible. Landscapers INCexpects all its employees, clients, and visitors to observe its smoking regulations (SA Tobacco Products Control Act 83 of 1993).</p>
<h3>AREAS WHERE SMOKING IS PROHIBITED</h3>
<ul><li>All Landscapers INC buildings (areas classified as work areas) except for areas identified specifically as smoking areas in such buildings.</li><li>Any area where there is a fire hazard.</li><li>Common areas, including lifts, entrance halls, corridors, waiting rooms, copy rooms, workshops, rest rooms, toilets.</li><li>Computer areas, wash bay and pits.</li><li>Training venues and conference rooms (smoke break to be allowed)</li><li>Areas where food is prepared.</li><li>Any area deemed by the site Landscapers INC is currently working( on site rules set by client) Application of this Policy The success of the application of the policy depends on the thoughtfulness, consideration and co-operation of smokers and non-smokers.  Should conflict arise between smokers and non-smokers, employees must be encouraged to compromise amongst themselves.  If this fails, the matter must be dealt with in terms of the grievance procedure.</li></ul>
<p>Responsibility Since it is the responsibility of line management to ensure that the Company’s policy is complied with, they are responsible for ensuring that: -</p>
<ul><li>All complaints raised are investigated immediately with a view to taking corrective action.</li><li>Persons raising complaints are not victimised in any way, and</li><li>Employees do not violate smoking regulations blatantly.</li><li>Disciplinary steps in terms of the Company’s disciplinary code and procedure must be initiated against employees who refuse to comply with smoking regulations.</li></ul>
<p>Communication Copies of this policy document must be distributed to all employees in the Company. Appropriate notices must be displayed in all areas where smoking is prohibited. Notices in client service areas must be client friendly.</p>
<p>Articles on the dangers related to smoking must be communicated on an on-going basis.</p>
<h3>APPROVAL</h3>
<p>CEO Initial and Surname Signature Date J.E.Naidoo</p>
<h3>02 January 2026</h3>', 'Smoking Policy Effective Date: 02-01-2026 POL 002 Next Revision Date: 02-01-2026 Version: 00 Smoking Policy Policy Motivation Landscapers INC acknowledges that medical evidence has shown that smoking is detrimental to the health of both active and passive smokers. Smoking cigarettes, cigars and pipes is also an irritation to many non-smokers and can aggravate various allergic conditions. Policy Objective The objective of the above policy is to establish a pleasant and healthy working environment, considering the needs and rights of both smokers and non-smokers. Policy Statement Landscapers INCacknowledges that its employees and clients have the right to work and do business in a pleasant environment with the cleanest air possible. Landscapers INCexpects all its employees, clients, and visitors to observe its smoking regulations (SA Tobacco Products Control Act 83 of 1993). AREAS WHERE SMOKING IS PROHIBITED All Landscapers INC buildings (areas classified as work areas) except for areas identified specifically as smoking areas in such buildings. Any area where there is a fire hazard. Common areas, including lifts, entrance halls, corridors, waiting rooms, copy rooms, workshops, rest rooms, toilets. Computer areas, wash bay and pits. Training venues and conference rooms (smoke break to be allowed) Areas where food is prepared. Any area deemed by the site Landscapers INC is currently working( on site rules set by client) Application of this Policy The success of the application of the policy depends on the thoughtfulness, consideration and co-operation of smokers and non-smokers. Should conflict arise between smokers and non-smokers, employees must be encouraged to compromise amongst themselves. If this fails, the matter must be dealt with in terms of the grievance procedure. Responsibility Since it is the responsibility of line management to ensure that the Company’s policy is complied with, they are responsible for ensuring that: - All complaints raised are investigated immediately with a view to taking corrective action. Persons raising complaints are not victimised in any way, and Employees do not violate smoking regulations blatantly. Disciplinary steps in terms of the Company’s disciplinary code and procedure must be initiated against employees who refuse to comply with smoking regulations. Communication Copies of this policy document must be distributed to all employees in the Company. Appropriate notices must be displayed in all areas where smoking is prohibited. Notices in client service areas must be client friendly. Articles on the dangers related to smoking must be communicated on an on-going basis. APPROVAL CEO Initial and Surname Signature Date J.E.Naidoo 02 January 2026', true, 'manager', 'POL 002', '00', '2026-01-02', '2026-01-02', 'x/H&S-20260923T091746Z-1-001/H&S/Policies/2.Smoking Policy.pdf', true, '{}'::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, category = excluded.category, content = excluded.content, text = excluded.text, locked = excluded.locked, owner_role = excluded.owner_role, doc_code = excluded.doc_code, revision = excluded.revision, effective_date = excluded.effective_date, review_date = excluded.review_date, _src = excluded._src, _seed = excluded._seed, data = excluded.data where public.docs._seed and public.docs.updated_by is not distinct from public.docs.created_by;
insert into public.docs (id, title, category, content, text, locked, owner_role, doc_code, revision, effective_date, review_date, _src, _seed, data) values ('doc-policy-3-a1d604', 'Security Policy', 'policy', '<p>Security Policy POL 003 Version: 00 Effective Date: 02-01-2026 Next Revision Date: 02-01-2027</p>
<p>Security Policy PURPOSE A Security policy ensures that Landscapers INC complies with the Occupational Safety and Health Act and relevant legislation. It provides guidelines for establishing and implementing programs that will reduce workplace hazards, protect lives and promote employee security. The purpose of this document is to provide a secure working environment by protecting ourselves, our assets and our operations against risk of injury, loss or damage from criminal, hostile or malicious acts.</p>
<h3>SCOPE</h3>
<p>This policy applies to all users of the Landscapers INC owned leased / hired facilities and equipment Landscapers INC policy identifies management and employee roles in health and safety. It provides for a work analysis to determine the presence of hazards. It also specifies hazard prevention and control procedures, and outlines required employee training. This policy also describes employee roles and the contribution staff make to the safe and secure use of Landscapers INC assets, equipment and information. POLICY STATEMENT Landscapers INC, together with our employees and contractors, will provide a secure working environment by protecting ourselves, our assets, equipment, information and our operations against risk of injury, loss or damage from criminal, hostile or malicious acts. We will routinely assess security risks and develop processes to ensure those risks are effectively managed.</p>
<p>We will comply with security measures when in place and have a duty to report to the appropriate authority any security breaches, unethical conduct, and conflicts of interest that come to our notice.</p>
<p>Our security performance will be monitored and measured on a continuing basis.</p>
<p>Equipment Security All general equipment must be located in suitable physical locations that:</p>
<ul><li>Reduce risks from environmental hazards, for example, heat, fire, smoke, water, dust and vibration.</li><li>Reduce the risk of theft, for example, if necessary, items such as brush cutters should be physically chained down.</li><li>Facilitate workstations handling sensitive data being positioned so as to eliminate the risk of the data being seen by unauthorized people.</li></ul>', 'Security Policy POL 003 Version: 00 Effective Date: 02-01-2026 Next Revision Date: 02-01-2027 Security Policy PURPOSE A Security policy ensures that Landscapers INC complies with the Occupational Safety and Health Act and relevant legislation. It provides guidelines for establishing and implementing programs that will reduce workplace hazards, protect lives and promote employee security. The purpose of this document is to provide a secure working environment by protecting ourselves, our assets and our operations against risk of injury, loss or damage from criminal, hostile or malicious acts. SCOPE This policy applies to all users of the Landscapers INC owned leased / hired facilities and equipment Landscapers INC policy identifies management and employee roles in health and safety. It provides for a work analysis to determine the presence of hazards. It also specifies hazard prevention and control procedures, and outlines required employee training. This policy also describes employee roles and the contribution staff make to the safe and secure use of Landscapers INC assets, equipment and information. POLICY STATEMENT Landscapers INC, together with our employees and contractors, will provide a secure working environment by protecting ourselves, our assets, equipment, information and our operations against risk of injury, loss or damage from criminal, hostile or malicious acts. We will routinely assess security risks and develop processes to ensure those risks are effectively managed. We will comply with security measures when in place and have a duty to report to the appropriate authority any security breaches, unethical conduct, and conflicts of interest that come to our notice. Our security performance will be monitored and measured on a continuing basis. Equipment Security All general equipment must be located in suitable physical locations that: Reduce risks from environmental hazards, for example, heat, fire, smoke, water, dust and vibration. Reduce the risk of theft, for example, if necessary, items such as brush cutters should be physically chained down. Facilitate workstations handling sensitive data being positioned so as to eliminate the risk of the data being seen by unauthorized people.', true, 'manager', 'POL 003', '00', '2026-01-02', '2027-01-02', 'x/H&S-20260923T091746Z-1-001/H&S/Policies/3.Security Policy.pdf', true, '{}'::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, category = excluded.category, content = excluded.content, text = excluded.text, locked = excluded.locked, owner_role = excluded.owner_role, doc_code = excluded.doc_code, revision = excluded.revision, effective_date = excluded.effective_date, review_date = excluded.review_date, _src = excluded._src, _seed = excluded._seed, data = excluded.data where public.docs._seed and public.docs.updated_by is not distinct from public.docs.created_by;
insert into public.docs (id, title, category, content, text, locked, owner_role, doc_code, revision, effective_date, review_date, _src, _seed, data) values ('doc-policy-4-b9a204', 'Drug and Alcohol Policy', 'policy', '<p>Drug and Alcohol Policy Drug &amp; Alcohol Policy POL 004 Version: 00 Effective Date: 02-01-2026 Next Revision Date: 02-01-2027</p>
<p>Purpose Landscapers INC recognises that an employee''s state of health may affect his/her ability to perform his/her job, may restrict the kind of work he/she can perform and may affect his/her future employability. Landscapers INC also recognises that alcohol and drug abuse rank as major health problems worldwide. This statement of policy is to inform employees of Landscapers INC’ viewpoint on physical or behavioural disorders resulting from use of drugs or alcohol, to encourage an enlightened attitude towards these disorders and to provide guidelines for consistent handling, throughout Landscapers INC, of problems resulting from alcohol and drug use. Landscapers INC has a ZERO tolerance to Drugs and Alcohol This policy applies to all staff of Landscapers INC.</p>
<p>Policy Landscapers INC will give the same consideration to employees with dependencies on alcohol and/or drugs as it does to employees having any other disease. Landscapers INC is concerned only with those situations where the use of alcohol or drugs interferes with the employee''s health, safety or job performance, adversely affects the job performance of other employees or is considered to be so serious as to be detrimental to Landscapers INC’ business. Landscapers INC will not intrude in the private lives of employees. In order to achieve successful treatment and rehabilitation and a return to productive employment with minimal disruption to personal, family and social life, it is most important that problems due to dependencies on alcohol and/or drugs are recognised early. Landscapers INC will assist an employee to obtain effective treatment and an employee who conscientiously seeks such help, will not place his or her job in jeopardy by doing so. The normal Company benefits that apply to any illness will be available to such an employee. The HR Department will assist in arranging treatment, and necessary absence from work will be regarded as sick leave or unpaid. Legal Drugs An employee may have legal access to drugs that might impair his/her work performance or create a risk of accidents. These would include alcohol and some drugs prescribed for an employee by a doctor. The employee is requested to advise the Supervisor or Line Manager if they are on prescribed drugs that may impair their work performance. An employee is prohibited from work whilst they affect his or her work performance. Where prescribed drugs cause impairment, it is in the best interests of the employee, his/her colleagues and Landscapers INC that he/she be given sick leave.</p>
<p>Illegal Drugs For the purpose of this policy, illegal drugs are those drugs that cannot be obtained by legal means or drugs which are legally obtainable but which have been obtained by illegal means. This includes all forms of narcotics, stimulants, tranquillisers and hallucinogens whose sale, purchase, transfer, use or possession is prohibited or restricted. Employees are prohibited from being on or arriving at Landscapers INC premises OR client sites whilst under the effects of such drugs.</p>
<p>Screening All prospective employees are medically examined before being accepted for permanent employment. All pre- employment medicals include testing for drug residues and evidence of alcohol abuse. These measures have been introduced to screen out prospective employees with drug or alcohol dependency problems. As per Occupational Health and Safety Act No 85 of 1993 Regulation 2a(1),(2),(3) Employees, and particularly those working in high-risk jobs, may be required from time to time to submit to a random urine drug screen. Such a procedure will form an integral part of accident or "near-miss" investigations and may be requested by Management or the HR Department in situations that give cause for concern.</p>
<h3>APPROVAL</h3>
<p>CEO Initial and Surname Signature Date J.E.Naidoo 02 January 2026</p>', 'Drug and Alcohol Policy Drug & Alcohol Policy POL 004 Version: 00 Effective Date: 02-01-2026 Next Revision Date: 02-01-2027 Purpose Landscapers INC recognises that an employee''s state of health may affect his/her ability to perform his/her job, may restrict the kind of work he/she can perform and may affect his/her future employability. Landscapers INC also recognises that alcohol and drug abuse rank as major health problems worldwide. This statement of policy is to inform employees of Landscapers INC’ viewpoint on physical or behavioural disorders resulting from use of drugs or alcohol, to encourage an enlightened attitude towards these disorders and to provide guidelines for consistent handling, throughout Landscapers INC, of problems resulting from alcohol and drug use. Landscapers INC has a ZERO tolerance to Drugs and Alcohol This policy applies to all staff of Landscapers INC. Policy Landscapers INC will give the same consideration to employees with dependencies on alcohol and/or drugs as it does to employees having any other disease. Landscapers INC is concerned only with those situations where the use of alcohol or drugs interferes with the employee''s health, safety or job performance, adversely affects the job performance of other employees or is considered to be so serious as to be detrimental to Landscapers INC’ business. Landscapers INC will not intrude in the private lives of employees. In order to achieve successful treatment and rehabilitation and a return to productive employment with minimal disruption to personal, family and social life, it is most important that problems due to dependencies on alcohol and/or drugs are recognised early. Landscapers INC will assist an employee to obtain effective treatment and an employee who conscientiously seeks such help, will not place his or her job in jeopardy by doing so. The normal Company benefits that apply to any illness will be available to such an employee. The HR Department will assist in arranging treatment, and necessary absence from work will be regarded as sick leave or unpaid. Legal Drugs An employee may have legal access to drugs that might impair his/her work performance or create a risk of accidents. These would include alcohol and some drugs prescribed for an employee by a doctor. The employee is requested to advise the Supervisor or Line Manager if they are on prescribed drugs that may impair their work performance. An employee is prohibited from work whilst they affect his or her work performance. Where prescribed drugs cause impairment, it is in the best interests of the employee, his/her colleagues and Landscapers INC that he/she be given sick leave. Illegal Drugs For the purpose of this policy, illegal drugs are those drugs that cannot be obtained by legal means or drugs which are legally obtainable but which have been obtained by illegal means. This includes all forms of narcotics, stimulants, tranquillisers and hallucinogens whose sale, purchase, transfer, use or possession is prohibited or restricted. Employees are prohibited from being on or arriving at Landscapers INC premises OR client sites whilst under the effects of such drugs. Screening All prospective employees are medically examined before being accepted for permanent employment. All pre- employment medicals include testing for drug residues and evidence of alcohol abuse. These measures have been introduced to screen out prospective employees with drug or alcohol dependency problems. As per Occupational Health and Safety Act No 85 of 1993 Regulation 2a(1),(2),(3) Employees, and particularly those working in high-risk jobs, may be required from time to time to submit to a random urine drug screen. Such a procedure will form an integral part of accident or "near-miss" investigations and may be requested by Management or the HR Department in situations that give cause for concern. APPROVAL CEO Initial and Surname Signature Date J.E.Naidoo 02 January 2026', true, 'manager', 'POL 004', '00', '2026-01-02', '2027-01-02', 'x/H&S-20260923T091746Z-1-001/H&S/Policies/4.Drug and Alcohol Policy.pdf', true, '{}'::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, category = excluded.category, content = excluded.content, text = excluded.text, locked = excluded.locked, owner_role = excluded.owner_role, doc_code = excluded.doc_code, revision = excluded.revision, effective_date = excluded.effective_date, review_date = excluded.review_date, _src = excluded._src, _seed = excluded._seed, data = excluded.data where public.docs._seed and public.docs.updated_by is not distinct from public.docs.created_by;
insert into public.docs (id, title, category, content, text, locked, owner_role, doc_code, revision, effective_date, review_date, _src, _seed, data) values ('doc-policy-5-33f27b', 'Business Ethics and Performance Policy', 'policy', '<p>Business Ethics and Performance Policy PURPOSE In line with the Landscapers INC Quality Policy and organizational goal of excellence, it is vital for Landscapers INC and its employees to abide by a strict ethical code. Since the integrity of its employees underlies Landscapers INC relationship with Customers, clients and communities, the highest standards of ethical business Conduct are required by employees in fulfilling their company responsibility. SCOPE This policy applies to and is binding upon all Landscapers INC employees, including supplementary Labour, contractors, and consultants. This policy incorporates the principles enshrined in the South African Constitution, as well as relevant statutes, corporate governance practices, and Landscapers INC Policies and Procedures.</p>
<h3>POLICY STATEMENT</h3>
<p>Ethics are an attitude towards doing business in the best possible way. Ethics include such things as living Landscapers INC values, sound corporate Governance, respect for diversity and Human rights, and acting with Integrity. Ethics involve a clear, conscious commitment to do the right thing at all times.</p>
<h3>PRINCIPLES</h3>
<p>Ethics is an attitude towards doing business in the best possible way. It does not simply mean doing what is legal in any situation; it also means doing what is ethically right.</p>
<p>Landscapers INC Ethical Values are as follows: -</p>
<ul><li>Respect, dignity, and equal opportunity.</li><li>Employee development.</li><li>Participation and empowerment.</li><li>Providing a safe and healthy environment.</li><li>Accepting accountability for actions and decisions.</li><li>Treating everyone fairly, consistently, sensitively, honestly and with respect for individual rights.</li><li>Acting with honesty, openness, and integrity in all dealings with colleagues, company, customers, and other stakeholders.</li><li>Complying with the law as well as Landscapers INC policies, codes of conduct and procedures.</li><li>Landscapers INC supports employees in their quest to abide by strict ethical Guidelines.</li></ul>
<h3>PERFORMANCE</h3>
<p>To enable Landscapers INC to deliver Customer Satisfaction, increased productivity is essential. The productivity can only be increased via training of employees to be efficient in all core activities that are critical for the success, profitability, and sustainability of this business, therefore these learning processes are to enhance the employee’s performance.</p>
<p>The Training and Development Programs are as follows</p>
<ul><li>Competency based Training and Development.</li><li>Competency based Performance Management.</li><li>Competency based Recruitment and Selection.</li><li>Competency based Assessment.</li></ul>
<p>Landscapers INC strives to be recognized as an equal opportunity employer, for this reason the following assessment criteria are used:</p>
<ul><li>Assessment of learning Potential</li><li>Assessment of Management Potential</li><li>Career Development Assessment</li><li>Assessment of suitability of students for various courses of study Business Ethics and Performance Policy POL 005 Version: 00 Effective Date:</li></ul>
<h3>02-01-2026</h3>
<p>Next Revision Date:</p>
<h3>02-01-2027</h3>
<p>These training and development programs will enable this organization to Co-ordinate individual activities to deliver Customer satisfaction efficiently and effectively. This will also enhance the reflexive competence in demonstrating the ability to reflect on, evaluate, adapt and improve in a variety of contexts.</p>
<h3>STRATEGIC OBJECTIVES</h3>
<p>Landscapers INC mission is to increase its market share in the Landscaping Sector therefore the strategic objective of Landscapers INC is to develop an integrated Education, Training and Human Resources Development Plan to unlock the full potential of all human resources within Landscapers INC, this will add value to the social and economic sustainability of the organization.</p>
<p>This strategic objective will be achieved through the development of employee’s competence levels as well as increased levels of return on costs and investment in education and training. It is encouraged to adopt a culture of life-long learning skills by creating:</p>
<ul><li>A workplace, which is an active learning environment</li><li>Opportunities to acquire new skills These learning interventions are to minimize or eliminate costly mistakes, which derive from incompetence and enhance cost efficiency and cost effectiveness.</li></ul>
<p>Together we begin a pilgrimage aimed at ensuring that we really achieve organizational excellence.</p>
<p>It is a fact that without rules our lives would be chaotic. Imagine what driving would be like if there were no rules of the road to guide and assist us.</p>
<p>Similarly, policies and procedures are intended to assist us in the performance of our work Although all employees are expected to comply with these policies and procedures, they are also expected to interpret them reasonably in order for them to achieve the full effect.</p>
<p>Whilst Management will keep these policies and procedures under constant review, employees who feel they should be amended are requested to present the matter to the relevant manager, who will then make the necessary amendments if so required.</p>
<h3>RESPONSIBILITIES</h3>
<p>Compliance, monitoring and review All employees have a responsibility to act in accordance with the ethical obligations and principles outlined in this policy. Compliance is a condition of employment.</p>
<p>Managers must ensure that this policy and its guidelines are strongly and clearly communicated to all employees.</p>
<p>It is Management’s endeavor to develop Human Resources by ensuring that employees acquire all necessary skills in cargo handling in accordance with Skills Development act, Sector Skills plan, and Workplace Skills plan.</p>
<p>Reporting No additional reporting is required.</p>
<p>Records management Staff must maintain all records relevant to administering this policy in a recognised recordkeeping system</p>
<h3>APPROVAL</h3>
<p>CEO Initial and Surname Signature Date J.E.NAIDOO 02 January 2026</p>', 'Business Ethics and Performance Policy PURPOSE In line with the Landscapers INC Quality Policy and organizational goal of excellence, it is vital for Landscapers INC and its employees to abide by a strict ethical code. Since the integrity of its employees underlies Landscapers INC relationship with Customers, clients and communities, the highest standards of ethical business Conduct are required by employees in fulfilling their company responsibility. SCOPE This policy applies to and is binding upon all Landscapers INC employees, including supplementary Labour, contractors, and consultants. This policy incorporates the principles enshrined in the South African Constitution, as well as relevant statutes, corporate governance practices, and Landscapers INC Policies and Procedures. POLICY STATEMENT Ethics are an attitude towards doing business in the best possible way. Ethics include such things as living Landscapers INC values, sound corporate Governance, respect for diversity and Human rights, and acting with Integrity. Ethics involve a clear, conscious commitment to do the right thing at all times. PRINCIPLES Ethics is an attitude towards doing business in the best possible way. It does not simply mean doing what is legal in any situation; it also means doing what is ethically right. Landscapers INC Ethical Values are as follows: - Respect, dignity, and equal opportunity. Employee development. Participation and empowerment. Providing a safe and healthy environment. Accepting accountability for actions and decisions. Treating everyone fairly, consistently, sensitively, honestly and with respect for individual rights. Acting with honesty, openness, and integrity in all dealings with colleagues, company, customers, and other stakeholders. Complying with the law as well as Landscapers INC policies, codes of conduct and procedures. Landscapers INC supports employees in their quest to abide by strict ethical Guidelines. PERFORMANCE To enable Landscapers INC to deliver Customer Satisfaction, increased productivity is essential. The productivity can only be increased via training of employees to be efficient in all core activities that are critical for the success, profitability, and sustainability of this business, therefore these learning processes are to enhance the employee’s performance. The Training and Development Programs are as follows Competency based Training and Development. Competency based Performance Management. Competency based Recruitment and Selection. Competency based Assessment. Landscapers INC strives to be recognized as an equal opportunity employer, for this reason the following assessment criteria are used: Assessment of learning Potential Assessment of Management Potential Career Development Assessment Assessment of suitability of students for various courses of study Business Ethics and Performance Policy POL 005 Version: 00 Effective Date: 02-01-2026 Next Revision Date: 02-01-2027 These training and development programs will enable this organization to Co-ordinate individual activities to deliver Customer satisfaction efficiently and effectively. This will also enhance the reflexive competence in demonstrating the ability to reflect on, evaluate, adapt and improve in a variety of contexts. STRATEGIC OBJECTIVES Landscapers INC mission is to increase its market share in the Landscaping Sector therefore the strategic objective of Landscapers INC is to develop an integrated Education, Training and Human Resources Development Plan to unlock the full potential of all human resources within Landscapers INC, this will add value to the social and economic sustainability of the organization. This strategic objective will be achieved through the development of employee’s competence levels as well as increased levels of return on costs and investment in education and training. It is encouraged to adopt a culture of life-long learning skills by creating: A workplace, which is an active learning environment Opportunities to acquire new skills These learning interventions are to minimize or eliminate costly mistakes, which derive from incompetence and enhance cost efficiency and cost effectiveness. Together we begin a pilgrimage aimed at ensuring that we really achieve organizational excellence. It is a fact that without rules our lives would be chaotic. Imagine what driving would be like if there were no rules of the road to guide and assist us. Similarly, policies and procedures are intended to assist us in the performance of our work Although all employees are expected to comply with these policies and procedures, they are also expected to interpret them reasonably in order for them to achieve the full effect. Whilst Management will keep these policies and procedures under constant review, employees who feel they should be amended are requested to present the matter to the relevant manager, who will then make the necessary amendments if so required. RESPONSIBILITIES Compliance, monitoring and review All employees have a responsibility to act in accordance with the ethical obligations and principles outlined in this policy. Compliance is a condition of employment. Managers must ensure that this policy and its guidelines are strongly and clearly communicated to all employees. It is Management’s endeavor to develop Human Resources by ensuring that employees acquire all necessary skills in cargo handling in accordance with Skills Development act, Sector Skills plan, and Workplace Skills plan. Reporting No additional reporting is required. Records management Staff must maintain all records relevant to administering this policy in a recognised recordkeeping system APPROVAL CEO Initial and Surname Signature Date J.E.NAIDOO 02 January 2026', true, 'manager', 'POL 005', '00', null, null, 'x/H&S-20260923T091746Z-1-001/H&S/Policies/5.Business Ethics and Performance Policy.pdf', true, '{}'::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, category = excluded.category, content = excluded.content, text = excluded.text, locked = excluded.locked, owner_role = excluded.owner_role, doc_code = excluded.doc_code, revision = excluded.revision, effective_date = excluded.effective_date, review_date = excluded.review_date, _src = excluded._src, _seed = excluded._seed, data = excluded.data where public.docs._seed and public.docs.updated_by is not distinct from public.docs.created_by;
insert into public.docs (id, title, category, content, text, locked, owner_role, doc_code, revision, effective_date, review_date, _src, _seed, data) values ('doc-policy-6-a6bcdb', 'Code of Conduct Policy', 'policy', '<p>Code of Conduct Policy</p>
<h3>PURPOSE</h3>
<p>A Code of Conduct policy ensures that Landscapers INC complies with the Basic Conditions of Employment Act, Occupational Safety and Health Act and relevant legislation. SCOPE The Code of Conduct is an important part of an overall framework within which employees are expected to work. The purpose of the Code is to provide guidance to employees of Landscapers INC in performing their duties as employees.</p>
<p>While the conduct of employees is primarily governed by the contract of employment, employees are required to comply with Landscapers INC wide range of policies and procedures. For the purposes of this Policy, employees are defined as individuals who are in the paid employment of the Landscapers INC whether full-time or part-time.</p>
<h3>POLICY STATEMENT</h3>
<p>General Conduct and Behaviour In the performance of their duty’s employees shall:</p>
<ul><li>Employees are expected to devote all their time, attention and ability to the business of and in all respects observe the lawful directions and requirements of Landscapers INC.</li><li>No employee may be directly or indirectly employed by any other business concern or perform any work for his or her own profit without the written permission of Landscapers INC.</li><li>Employees are expected to treat the property of Landscapers INC with due care, regard and respect. Company property must be used honestly and not to any employee''s benefit;</li><li>Employees are expected not to be in any unauthorized possession of company property, nor make any unauthorized use of company property. An employee may not allow a third party to do the same.</li><li>Employees are expected to make use of Landscapers INC grievance procedure to air and endeavor to settle any grievance, which may arise;</li><li>Employees are expected to report all accidents or damage to Landscapers INC property or a third party or client''s property and/or to employees on Landscapers INC premises, no matter how small the incident may appear to Landscapers INC.</li><li>Employees are expected not to victimize or to intimidate any other employee;</li><li>Employees are expected not to be in an intoxicated condition at any time whilst on duty or on Landscapers INC premises. No liquor, drugs or any other intoxicating substances Landscapers INC may be consumed whilst on duty or be brought into Landscapers INC premises without Landscapers INC authorization or permission;</li><li>Employees are expected to take the safety and wellbeing of other employees into account at all times;</li><li>Employees are expected to behave and conduct themselves at all times in an orderly and lawful manner;</li><li>Employees are expected not to bring any dangerous weapons onto Landscapers INC premises without the permission of Managing Director;</li><li>Employees are expected to declare a first schedule criminal record in terms of the Criminal Act No. 55 of 1977 or a criminal case pending against them immediately upon it occurring to Landscapers INC</li><li>All profits and benefits obtained by employees personally because of their employment in Landscapers INC must be declared. All outside interests, whether in competition with Landscapers INC or not, must be declared to Landscapers INC;</li><li>Employees are expected not to make any confidential information relating to Landscapers INC business activities known or available to any person inside or outside Landscapers INC;</li><li>Employees are expected to adhere to all Landscapers INC procedures, practices, rules and regulations;</li></ul>
<p>Code of Conduct Policy POL 006 Version: 00 Effective Date: 02-01-2026 Next Revision Date: 02-01-2027</p>
<ul><li>Employees are expected to make themselves available for work in the manner lawfully required by Landscapers INC. Employees are expected to perform all such duties and exercise all such powers in relation to the business of Landscapers INC as may from time to time be lawfully vested in or assigned to them by Landscapers INC;</li><li>Employees are expected to execute their duties and powers in a manner and to the standard lawfully required by Landscapers INC;</li><li>Employees are expected to treat all supervisors and managers of Landscapers INC with loyalty and due respect;</li><li>Employees are expected to keep to the legal working hours;</li><li>Employees are expected not to leave their assigned workplace or duties without prior permission of their immediate supervisors, except in cases of emergency;</li><li>Employees are expected not to distribute or put any notices on Landscapers INC premises without the prior permission of Landscapers INC;</li><li>Employees are expected to produce a doctor''s certificate to validate their illness if they are absent from work for more than two consecutive days because of illness;</li><li>Employees other than authorized managers are expected not to enter Landscapers INC restricted areas before or after working hours without permission of;</li><li>Employees are expected not to allow or facilitate unauthorized access to Landscapers INC premises;</li><li>Employees are expected not the fight on company premises. No horseplay amongst employees is allowed on company premises;</li><li>Employees are expected not to threaten or to assault other employees;</li><li>Employees are expected not to use obscene language or gestures whilst at work;</li><li>Employees are expected to undergo training and attend training courses at Landscapers INC request;</li><li>Employees are expected to use their best endeavor to conduct, improve, extend, develop, promote, protect and preserve the business, interests, reputation and goodwill of Landscapers INC at all times;</li><li>Employees are expected not to sleep whilst they are on duty.</li><li>Employees are expected not to make use of open flame cooking devices inside the cabs of Landscapers INC vehicles;</li><li>Vehicle drivers are expected to safely secure their vehicles at all times when their vehicles are left unattended or at overnight stops on route;</li><li>Employees are expected to abide by the bona fide work practices with regard to Landscapers INC, its clients and/or business associates;</li><li>Employees are expected to make use of their best endeavors to enhance, promote and maintain industrial peace at Landscapers INC work place;</li><li>Employees are expected to keep confidential and not to disclose any of Landscapers INC trade secrets, confidential documentation, technical know-how and drawings, trade agreements, systems, methods, computer software, processes, practices, client lists, programs, marketing, technological and/or financial or any confidential information or any other undertaking, institution, firm company or person with which Landscapers INC may be or may have been in technological and/or commercial co-operation or association;</li><li>Vehicle drivers are expected to declare to Landscapers INC all traffic fines with regard to company vehicles;</li><li>Employees are expected to wear Landscapers INC uniform whilst on duty;</li><li>Vehicle drives are expected to check the tyres, diesel and equipment on their vehicle.</li></ul>
<p>Code of Conduct Policy POL 006 Version: 00 Effective Date: 02-01-2026 Next Revision Date: 02-01-2027</p>
<p>Implementation This Policy applies to all employees of Landscapers INC. Breaches of the Policy may result in Landscapers INC Disciplinary Procedure being invoked, which may result in dismissal.</p>
<h3>RESPONSIBILITIES</h3>
<p>Landscapers INC will provide practical guidance and direction as required on such areas as gifts and entertainment and on other ethical considerations which arise routinely. RELATED LEGISLATION AND DOCUMENTS</p>
<p>Occupational Health &amp; Safety Act (85 OF 1993) Tobacco Products Control Act (83 of 1993) POPI Act RTA BCEA EEA FEEDBACK The Code of Conduct policy will be periodically reviewed to ensure continued relevance and adequacy. The Code of Conduct Policy shall be made available to all stakeholders on request and be prominently displayed within the organisation. The Code of Conduct Policy forms an integral part of the company’s core business processes and the HSE Management System. It is the responsibility of management and all employees to implement this policy effectively ranks equally with their respective responsibilities for services, maintenance, quality, sales, costs, profitability, and similar matters of vital importance to the success of the company.</p>
<h3>APPROVAL</h3>
<p>CEO Initial and Surname Signature Date J.E.NAIDOO 02 January 2026</p>', 'Code of Conduct Policy PURPOSE A Code of Conduct policy ensures that Landscapers INC complies with the Basic Conditions of Employment Act, Occupational Safety and Health Act and relevant legislation. SCOPE The Code of Conduct is an important part of an overall framework within which employees are expected to work. The purpose of the Code is to provide guidance to employees of Landscapers INC in performing their duties as employees. While the conduct of employees is primarily governed by the contract of employment, employees are required to comply with Landscapers INC wide range of policies and procedures. For the purposes of this Policy, employees are defined as individuals who are in the paid employment of the Landscapers INC whether full-time or part-time. POLICY STATEMENT General Conduct and Behaviour In the performance of their duty’s employees shall: Employees are expected to devote all their time, attention and ability to the business of and in all respects observe the lawful directions and requirements of Landscapers INC. No employee may be directly or indirectly employed by any other business concern or perform any work for his or her own profit without the written permission of Landscapers INC. Employees are expected to treat the property of Landscapers INC with due care, regard and respect. Company property must be used honestly and not to any employee''s benefit; Employees are expected not to be in any unauthorized possession of company property, nor make any unauthorized use of company property. An employee may not allow a third party to do the same. Employees are expected to make use of Landscapers INC grievance procedure to air and endeavor to settle any grievance, which may arise; Employees are expected to report all accidents or damage to Landscapers INC property or a third party or client''s property and/or to employees on Landscapers INC premises, no matter how small the in