insert into public.docs (id, title, category, content, text, locked, owner_role, doc_code, revision, effective_date, review_date, _src, _seed, data) values ('doc-procedure-1-f46f0e', 'Housekeeping at Work', 'procedure', '<p>Landscapers INC Housekeeping at Work</p>
<p>What is housekeeping?</p>
<p>When we think of “housekeeping” we tend to think of the common phrase: “A place for everything and everything in its place.” But housekeeping means more than this.</p>
<p>Good housekeeping means having no unnecessary items about and keeping all necessary items in their proper places.</p>
<p>What is so important about housekeeping?</p>
<p>Think about what could happen if a bunch of oily rags suddenly caught fire one night, or if, in an emergency, employees could not get out of the work area safely because aisles were cluttered. Imagine those same employees unable to get out altogether because of a blocked exit. Good housekeeping is an essential part of Landscapers INC health and safety program. What are the benefits of good housekeeping at work?</p>
<p>Good housekeeping at work benefits both employers and employees alike. Good housekeeping can:</p>
<ul><li>Eliminate clutter which is a common cause of accidents, such as slips, trips, and falls, and fires and explosions.</li><li>Reduce the chances of harmful materials entering the body (e.g., dust, vapors); improve productivity (the right tools and materials for the job will be easy to find); improve your company’s image, good housekeeping reflects a well-run business. An orderly workplace will impress all who enter it – employees, visitors, customers, etc.</li><li>Help your company to keep its inventory to a minimum (good housekeeping makes it easier to keep an accurate count of inventories); help your company to make the best use of its space.</li><li>Make the workplace neat, comfortable, and pleasant – not a dangerous eyesore.</li></ul>
<p>Keeping work areas organized and clean can help make work safer, healthier, and easier Housekeeping at Work</p>
<p>What are some signs of poor housekeeping?</p>
<p>There are many signs of poor housekeeping. You may recognize some of these in your own workplace:</p>
<ul><li>Cluttered and poorly arranged work areas.</li><li>Untidy or dangerous storage of materials (for example, materials stuffed in corners; overcrowded shelves).</li><li>Dusty, dirty floors and work surfaces.</li><li>items that are in excess or no longer needed.</li><li>Blocked or cluttered aisles and exits.</li><li>Tools and equipment left in work areas instead of being returned to proper storage places, broken containers and damaged materials.</li><li>Overflowing waste bins and containers.</li><li>Spills and leaks.</li></ul>
<p>How to improve housekeeping in your workplace?</p>
<p>Good housekeeping requires effort and teamwork, but it’s worth it. Set housekeeping standards. Make sure they are clear, objective, and attainable. Standards should make work easier, safer, and healthier. It is best to involve employees when setting standards. Measure how well the standards are met. (Remember: what gets measured gets done.) Use checklists to help you to systematically measure housekeeping. Provide positive feedback. Let employees know how well they are doing and how to improve. Encourage housekeeping as a way of life – not just a special activity when visitors are coming.</p>
<h3>LI-PRO-001</h3>
<p>Effective Date: 0 2 - 0 1 - 2 026 Revision Date: 02-01-2027</p>
<p>Revision 00</p>
<h3>LI-PRO-001</h3>
<p>Revision 00 Effective Date: 02-01-2025 Next Revision Date: 02-01-2026</p>
<p>Whether your workplace is an office, plant, store, or warehouse, here are some recommended housekeeping practices:</p>
<ul><li>Follow safe work procedures and the requirements of the law.</li><li>Keep work areas clean.</li><li>Keep aisles clear.</li><li>Keep exits and entrances clear.</li><li>Keep floors clean, dry and in good condition.</li><li>Vacuum or wet sweep dusty areas frequently.</li><li>Stack and store items safety.</li><li>Store all work materials (for example, paper, products, flammable liquids, etc.) in approved, clearly labelled containers in designated storage areas only.</li><li>Use proper waste containers.</li><li>Keep sprinklers, fire alarms and fire extinguishers clear.</li><li>Clean up spills and leaks of any type quickly and properly.</li><li>Clean and store tools, items and equipment properly. Fix or report broken or damaged tools, equipment, etc. Keep lighting sources clean and clear.</li></ul>', 'Landscapers INC Housekeeping at Work What is housekeeping? When we think of “housekeeping” we tend to think of the common phrase: “A place for everything and everything in its place.” But housekeeping means more than this. Good housekeeping means having no unnecessary items about and keeping all necessary items in their proper places. What is so important about housekeeping? Think about what could happen if a bunch of oily rags suddenly caught fire one night, or if, in an emergency, employees could not get out of the work area safely because aisles were cluttered. Imagine those same employees unable to get out altogether because of a blocked exit. Good housekeeping is an essential part of Landscapers INC health and safety program. What are the benefits of good housekeeping at work? Good housekeeping at work benefits both employers and employees alike. Good housekeeping can: Eliminate clutter which is a common cause of accidents, such as slips, trips, and falls, and fires and explosions. Reduce the chances of harmful materials entering the body (e.g., dust, vapors); improve productivity (the right tools and materials for the job will be easy to find); improve your company’s image, good housekeeping reflects a well-run business. An orderly workplace will impress all who enter it – employees, visitors, customers, etc. Help your company to keep its inventory to a minimum (good housekeeping makes it easier to keep an accurate count of inventories); help your company to make the best use of its space. Make the workplace neat, comfortable, and pleasant – not a dangerous eyesore. Keeping work areas organized and clean can help make work safer, healthier, and easier Housekeeping at Work What are some signs of poor housekeeping? There are many signs of poor housekeeping. You may recognize some of these in your own workplace: Cluttered and poorly arranged work areas. Untidy or dangerous storage of materials (for example, materials stuffed in corners; overcrowded shelves). Dusty, dirty floors and work surfaces. items that are in excess or no longer needed. Blocked or cluttered aisles and exits. Tools and equipment left in work areas instead of being returned to proper storage places, broken containers and damaged materials. Overflowing waste bins and containers. Spills and leaks. How to improve housekeeping in your workplace? Good housekeeping requires effort and teamwork, but it’s worth it. Set housekeeping standards. Make sure they are clear, objective, and attainable. Standards should make work easier, safer, and healthier. It is best to involve employees when setting standards. Measure how well the standards are met. (Remember: what gets measured gets done.) Use checklists to help you to systematically measure housekeeping. Provide positive feedback. Let employees know how well they are doing and how to improve. Encourage housekeeping as a way of life – not just a special activity when visitors are coming. LI-PRO-001 Effective Date: 0 2 - 0 1 - 2 026 Revision Date: 02-01-2027 Revision 00 LI-PRO-001 Revision 00 Effective Date: 02-01-2025 Next Revision Date: 02-01-2026 Whether your workplace is an office, plant, store, or warehouse, here are some recommended housekeeping practices: Follow safe work procedures and the requirements of the law. Keep work areas clean. Keep aisles clear. Keep exits and entrances clear. Keep floors clean, dry and in good condition. Vacuum or wet sweep dusty areas frequently. Stack and store items safety. Store all work materials (for example, paper, products, flammable liquids, etc.) in approved, clearly labelled containers in designated storage areas only. Use proper waste containers. Keep sprinklers, fire alarms and fire extinguishers clear. Clean up spills and leaks of any type quickly and properly. Clean and store tools, items and equipment properly. Fix or report broken or damaged tools, equipment, etc. Keep lighting sources clean and clear.', true, 'manager', 'PRO 001', null, null, '2026-01-02', 'x/H&S-20260923T091746Z-1-001/H&S/Procedures/1.Housekeeping at Work.pdf', true, '{}'::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, category = excluded.category, content = excluded.content, text = excluded.text, locked = excluded.locked, owner_role = excluded.owner_role, doc_code = excluded.doc_code, revision = excluded.revision, effective_date = excluded.effective_date, review_date = excluded.review_date, _src = excluded._src, _seed = excluded._seed, data = excluded.data where public.docs._seed and public.docs.updated_by is not distinct from public.docs.created_by;
insert into public.docs (id, title, category, content, text, locked, owner_role, doc_code, revision, effective_date, review_date, _src, _seed, data) values ('doc-procedure-10-bbd7c7', 'Offsite Emergency Procedure', 'procedure', '<h3>LI-PRO-010</h3>
<p>Revision 00 Effective Date: 02-01-2026 Next Revision Date: 02-01-2027</p>
<p>1.</p>
<h3>SUMMARY</h3>
<p>Landscapers INC Offsite Emergency Procedure</p>
<p>1.1 The purpose of this procedure is to provide assurance that any response to road accidents, incidents, customer site incidents, whether they are Fire, Medical or Hijacking 1.2 More specifically:</p>
<ul><li>To provide a communication system to facilitate rapid and effective response to off-site road transport emergencies to minimize delays and to ensure that the best possible plan of action is implemented.</li><li>To identify personnel and confirm everyone’s responsibilities</li><li>To determine post incident responsibilities for investigating and reporting 1.3 This procedure applies to all responsive activities performed by Landscapers Inc to prevent, reduce, contain, clean up, rehabilitate, or investigate accidents, incidents in accordance with both legislative and the customer’s requirements.</li></ul>
<p>2.</p>
<h3>TERMS AND DEFINITIONS</h3>
<p>An emergency: Is defined as an event where there is either a major transport operational problem (for example, a fire,hijacking or serious traffic incident), or where there is a loss of life or considerable environmental damage and there is a possibility of media coverage. The situation can be on a public road, private area, customer site.</p>
<p>3.</p>
<h3>PROCEDURE OVERVIEW</h3>
<p>4.1. Drivers involved in the accident/incident, reports to Landscapers INC operations. 4.2. Such reports are normally made by telephone or radio (if applicable). Calls must be made to the most senior operations official on duty who in turn should establish a contact number with the driver or relevant local authority and record all the relevant details of the incident. 4.3. The completed form should be copied and handed to the person responsible for the accident investigation. The original must be immediately sent to the Operations Manager.</p>
<h3>LI-PRO-010</h3>
<p>Revision 00 Effective Date: 02-01-2026 Next Revision Date: 02-01-2027</p>
<p>4.4. The Accident report form will be completed later by the safety officer.</p>
<h3>LI-PRO-010</h3>
<p>Revision 00 Effective Date: 02-01-2026 Next Revision Date: 02-01-2027</p>
<p>5.</p>
<h3>MEDICAL EMERGENCY</h3>
<p>Driver or Gardening team In the event of a serious or life-threatening condition such as:</p>
<ul><li>not breathing</li><li>seizure</li><li>profuse bleeding</li><li>stuporous state</li><li>head, neck, or back injury</li><li>suspected drug overdose</li><li>alcohol overdose</li><li>serious fall</li><li>diabetic incident</li><li>chest pain</li><li>shortness of breath .</li></ul>
<p>The National number is 10177</p>
<p>6.</p>
<h3>HIJACKING EMERGENCY</h3>
<p>Driver or gardening team A driver who experiences a hijacking is instructed to take the following measures to attempt to prevent injury or loss of life:</p>
<ul><li>DO exactly as told by the hijacker(s).</li><li>DO NOT resist. Surrender your vehicle and move away (quickly) if allowed to do so.</li><li>DO NOT reach for your valuables if allowed to leave. Leave everything behind and move away quickly.</li><li>DO NOT show signs of aggression. Do your best to appear calm. Your life is at stake.</li><li>DO NOT make eye contact, which may be perceived as a threat and retaliate aggressively.</li><li>KEEP your hands still and visible, giving the hijacker assurance of your passiveness.</li><li>DO NOT speak too fast or make sudden movements.</li><li>DO NOT lose your temper, threaten, or challenge the hijacker.</li><li>DO gather as much information as possible without appearing to pose a threat:</li><li>How many individuals? Height? Distinguishing features? Clothing?</li><li>Firearms / weapons? Make? Size?</li><li>Direction? Which direction did they head?</li><li>Language? English? Non-English? Accent?</li><li>URGENT:</li></ul>
<p>o Contact 10177 as soon as safely possible to do so; o Contact your controller immediately after calling 10177; o Find a safe place until help arrives.</p>
<p>Controller:</p>
<ul><li>URGENT:</li></ul>
<p>o Assist the driver in any manner possible; o Ensure that a 10177 call has been placed; o Notify Management (MD, Operations Manager) of situation asap; o Contact Tracker or Car Track;</p>
<ul><li>Non-URGENT:</li></ul>
<p>Inform Finance Dept of possible claim.</p>
<h3>LI-PRO-010</h3>
<p>Revision 00 Effective Date: 02-01-2026 Next Revision Date: 02-01-2027</p>
<h3>9</h3>
<p>DISCIPLINARY ACTION / REMEDIAL ACTION.</p>
<p>Once the Operations Manager, General Manager, Safety Officer, or Emergency Co-ordinator have completed his investigation of the incident they have to decide what the probable cause of the incident was. Should the information at his disposal indicate that it might have been the result of either employee error or negligence; disciplinary action has to be initiated. In the case of a major road accident, or where the Safety Officer is of the opinion that the facts indicate severe disciplinary action, a disciplinary enquiry should be convened in terms of the company’s disciplinary procedure. The Nominated Service Provider/ HR would be responsible for chairing the enquiry. In other cases, the Operations Manager will be responsible for the disciplinary action in terms of the Landscapers inc disciplinary procedure. With minor incidents a disciplinary counselling session should be sufficient for dealing with the matter.</p>
<p>In all instances the investigation might indicate that some remedial action/preventative Action Request e.g., training of the employee and amendment to Landscapers inc operations procedure is required. The Operations Manager and Controller will be responsible for the implementation of such remedial action.</p>
<h3>10</h3>
<h3>RESPONSIBILITIES</h3>
<p>Compliance, monitoring, and review</p>
<p>This procedure in particular outlines the responsibility of the following Landscapers inc officials with regards to Accident / Fire / Medical response, investigation, and reporting.</p>
<ul><li>Driver</li><li>Gardening team</li><li>Operations Manager</li><li>Maintenance Manager</li><li>Finance Dept Reporting No additional reporting is required. Records management Staff must maintain all records relevant to administering this policy and procedure in Landscapers inc recordkeeping system.</li></ul>
<h3>11</h3>
<h3>RELATED LEGISLATION AND DOCUMENTS</h3>
<p>National Road Traffic Act 93 of 1996 National Environmental Management Act 107 of 1998 Occupational Health and Safety Act 85 of 1993 Compensation for Occupational Injuries and Diseases Act 30 of 1993.</p>', 'LI-PRO-010 Revision 00 Effective Date: 02-01-2026 Next Revision Date: 02-01-2027 1. SUMMARY Landscapers INC Offsite Emergency Procedure 1.1 The purpose of this procedure is to provide assurance that any response to road accidents, incidents, customer site incidents, whether they are Fire, Medical or Hijacking 1.2 More specifically: To provide a communication system to facilitate rapid and effective response to off-site road transport emergencies to minimize delays and to ensure that the best possible plan of action is implemented. To identify personnel and confirm everyone’s responsibilities To determine post incident responsibilities for investigating and reporting 1.3 This procedure applies to all responsive activities performed by Landscapers Inc to prevent, reduce, contain, clean up, rehabilitate, or investigate accidents, incidents in accordance with both legislative and the customer’s requirements. 2. TERMS AND DEFINITIONS An emergency: Is defined as an event where there is either a major transport operational problem (for example, a fire,hijacking or serious traffic incident), or where there is a loss of life or considerable environmental damage and there is a possibility of media coverage. The situation can be on a public road, private area, customer site. 3. PROCEDURE OVERVIEW 4.1. Drivers involved in the accident/incident, reports to Landscapers INC operations. 4.2. Such reports are normally made by telephone or radio (if applicable). Calls must be made to the most senior operations official on duty who in turn should establish a contact number with the driver or relevant local authority and record all the relevant details of the incident. 4.3. The completed form should be copied and handed to the person responsible for the accident investigation. The original must be immediately sent to the Operations Manager. LI-PRO-010 Revision 00 Effective Date: 02-01-2026 Next Revision Date: 02-01-2027 4.4. The Accident report form will be completed later by the safety officer. LI-PRO-010 Revision 00 Effective Date: 02-01-2026 Next Revision Date: 02-01-2027 5. MEDICAL EMERGENCY Driver or Gardening team In the event of a serious or life-threatening condition such as: not breathing seizure profuse bleeding stuporous state head, neck, or back injury suspected drug overdose alcohol overdose serious fall diabetic incident chest pain shortness of breath . The National number is 10177 6. HIJACKING EMERGENCY Driver or gardening team A driver who experiences a hijacking is instructed to take the following measures to attempt to prevent injury or loss of life: DO exactly as told by the hijacker(s). DO NOT resist. Surrender your vehicle and move away (quickly) if allowed to do so. DO NOT reach for your valuables if allowed to leave. Leave everything behind and move away quickly. DO NOT show signs of aggression. Do your best to appear calm. Your life is at stake. DO NOT make eye contact, which may be perceived as a threat and retaliate aggressively. KEEP your hands still and visible, giving the hijacker assurance of your passiveness. DO NOT speak too fast or make sudden movements. DO NOT lose your temper, threaten, or challenge the hijacker. DO gather as much information as possible without appearing to pose a threat: How many individuals? Height? Distinguishing features? Clothing? Firearms / weapons? Make? Size? Direction? Which direction did they head? Language? English? Non-English? Accent? URGENT: o Contact 10177 as soon as safely possible to do so; o Contact your controller immediately after calling 10177; o Find a safe place until help arrives. Controller: URGENT: o Assist the driver in any manner possible; o Ensure that a 10177 call has been placed; o Notify Management (MD, Operations Manager) of situation asap; o Contact Tracker or Car Track; Non-URGENT: Inform Finance Dept of possible claim. LI-PRO-010 Revision 00 Effective Date: 02-01-2026 Next Revision Date: 02-01-2027 9 DISCIPLINARY ACTION / REMEDIAL ACTION. Once the Operations Manager, General Manager, Safety Officer, or Emergency Co-ordinator have completed his investigation of the incident they have to decide what the probable cause of the incident was. Should the information at his disposal indicate that it might have been the result of either employee error or negligence; disciplinary action has to be initiated. In the case of a major road accident, or where the Safety Officer is of the opinion that the facts indicate severe disciplinary action, a disciplinary enquiry should be convened in terms of the company’s disciplinary procedure. The Nominated Service Provider/ HR would be responsible for chairing the enquiry. In other cases, the Operations Manager will be responsible for the disciplinary action in terms of the Landscapers inc disciplinary procedure. With minor incidents a disciplinary counselling session should be sufficient for dealing with the matter. In all instances the investigation might indicate that some remedial action/preventative Action Request e.g., training of the employee and amendment to Landscapers inc operations procedure is required. The Operations Manager and Controller will be responsible for the implementation of such remedial action. 10 RESPONSIBILITIES Compliance, monitoring, and review This procedure in particular outlines the responsibility of the following Landscapers inc officials with regards to Accident / Fire / Medical response, investigation, and reporting. Driver Gardening team Operations Manager Maintenance Manager Finance Dept Reporting No additional reporting is required. Records management Staff must maintain all records relevant to administering this policy and procedure in Landscapers inc recordkeeping system. 11 RELATED LEGISLATION AND DOCUMENTS National Road Traffic Act 93 of 1996 National Environmental Management Act 107 of 1998 Occupational Health and Safety Act 85 of 1993 Compensation for Occupational Injuries and Diseases Act 30 of 1993.', true, 'manager', 'PRO 010', null, '2026-01-02', '2027-01-02', 'x/H&S-20260923T091746Z-1-001/H&S/Procedures/10.Offsite Emergency Procedure.pdf', true, '{}'::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, category = excluded.category, content = excluded.content, text = excluded.text, locked = excluded.locked, owner_role = excluded.owner_role, doc_code = excluded.doc_code, revision = excluded.revision, effective_date = excluded.effective_date, review_date = excluded.review_date, _src = excluded._src, _seed = excluded._seed, data = excluded.data where public.docs._seed and public.docs.updated_by is not distinct from public.docs.created_by;
insert into public.docs (id, title, category, content, text, locked, owner_role, doc_code, revision, effective_date, review_date, _src, _seed, data) values ('doc-procedure-2-bd8948', 'Spill Response and Clean-Up Procedure', 'procedure', '<h3>LI-PRO-002</h3>
<p>Revision 00 Effective Date: 02-01-2026 Next Revision Date: 02-01-2027</p>
<p>Landscapers INC Spill Response and Clean-Up Procedure</p>
<h3>1</h3>
<h3>PURPOSE</h3>
<p>1.1 The purpose of this procedure is to describe the proper means for cleaning up spills and disposal of any material which could pollute the state’s waterways if allowed to enter the Landscapers INC client sites storm sewer system.</p>
<h3>2</h3>
<h3>SCOPE</h3>
<p>2.1 This SOP specifically applies to spills caused by leaking equipment, broken or open containers. Some examples of spills include Petro Chemicals, Pesticides, diesel fuel, fertilizers, motor oil, and transmission fluid. These types of illicit discharges may result in significant fines from regulatory agencies.</p>
<h3>3</h3>
<h3>PROCEDURE</h3>
<p>First Responders First Responders will typically be a driver or staff or member of public who notices a spill. As soon as a spill is discovered, the initial action should be to protect personal safety and to prevent the pollutant from entering nearby drainage ditches or stormwater drop inlets. The person observing the spill should take immediate action to prevent further spillage and to confine the spilled material. The general instructions to contain a spill are:</p>
<ul><li>Notify the on-duty Supervisor as soon as the spill is discovered.</li><li>If possible, to do so safely, stop the cause of the spill.</li><li>This may include securing fluid/fuel caps or attempting to plug or cover punctures or gashes in containers. Do not attempt to stop the spill if the situation creates any degree of personal danger.</li></ul>
<p>Supervisor’s Response</p>
<ul><li>The on-duty Supervisor should respond to the spill scene to contain the fluids using spill absorbent.</li><li>If the spill is along a route, the Supervisor should follow the route to ensure there are no</li><li>additional spills.</li><li>The on-duty Supervisor should return to any treated spill locations to clean up the spill absorbent</li><li>and ensure that all the original spills have been contained.</li></ul>
<p>The on-duty Supervisor should immediately contact the Operations Manager and give the following information:</p>
<ul><li>Location of spillage</li><li>Type of material (i.e. diesel, pesticide, fertilizer etc.)</li><li>Estimated quantity and/or extent of spillage</li><li>A brief description of measures that have been taken to confine the spilled material and prevent further spillage.</li></ul>
<h3>LI-PRO-002</h3>
<p>Revision 00 Effective Date: 02-01-2026 Next Revision Date: 02-01-2027</p>
<h3>4</h3>
<h3>RESPONSIBILITIES</h3>
<p>Compliance, monitoring and review First responder Supervisor/Controller</p>
<ul><li>aligns with relevant legislation, government policy and/or Landscapers INC requirements/strategies/values</li><li>is implemented and monitored</li><li>is reviewed to evaluate its continuing effectiveness Reporting Reporting of all incidents is a requirement.</li></ul>
<p>Records management Staff must maintain all records relevant to administering this policy and procedure in a recognized Landscapers INC recordkeeping system.</p>
<h3>5</h3>
<h3>RELATED LEGISLATION AND DOCUMENTS</h3>
<p>National Road Traffic Act 93 of 1996 National Environmental Management Act 107 of 1998 Occupational Health and Safety Act 85 of 1993 Compensation for Occupational Injuries and Diseases Act 30 of 1993. Incident reporting document.</p>
<p>Emergency Team Organization.</p>', 'LI-PRO-002 Revision 00 Effective Date: 02-01-2026 Next Revision Date: 02-01-2027 Landscapers INC Spill Response and Clean-Up Procedure 1 PURPOSE 1.1 The purpose of this procedure is to describe the proper means for cleaning up spills and disposal of any material which could pollute the state’s waterways if allowed to enter the Landscapers INC client sites storm sewer system. 2 SCOPE 2.1 This SOP specifically applies to spills caused by leaking equipment, broken or open containers. Some examples of spills include Petro Chemicals, Pesticides, diesel fuel, fertilizers, motor oil, and transmission fluid. These types of illicit discharges may result in significant fines from regulatory agencies. 3 PROCEDURE First Responders First Responders will typically be a driver or staff or member of public who notices a spill. As soon as a spill is discovered, the initial action should be to protect personal safety and to prevent the pollutant from entering nearby drainage ditches or stormwater drop inlets. The person observing the spill should take immediate action to prevent further spillage and to confine the spilled material. The general instructions to contain a spill are: Notify the on-duty Supervisor as soon as the spill is discovered. If possible, to do so safely, stop the cause of the spill. This may include securing fluid/fuel caps or attempting to plug or cover punctures or gashes in containers. Do not attempt to stop the spill if the situation creates any degree of personal danger. Supervisor’s Response The on-duty Supervisor should respond to the spill scene to contain the fluids using spill absorbent. If the spill is along a route, the Supervisor should follow the route to ensure there are no additional spills. The on-duty Supervisor should return to any treated spill locations to clean up the spill absorbent and ensure that all the original spills have been contained. The on-duty Supervisor should immediately contact the Operations Manager and give the following information: Location of spillage Type of material (i.e. diesel, pesticide, fertilizer etc.) Estimated quantity and/or extent of spillage A brief description of measures that have been taken to confine the spilled material and prevent further spillage. LI-PRO-002 Revision 00 Effective Date: 02-01-2026 Next Revision Date: 02-01-2027 4 RESPONSIBILITIES Compliance, monitoring and review First responder Supervisor/Controller aligns with relevant legislation, government policy and/or Landscapers INC requirements/strategies/values is implemented and monitored is reviewed to evaluate its continuing effectiveness Reporting Reporting of all incidents is a requirement. Records management Staff must maintain all records relevant to administering this policy and procedure in a recognized Landscapers INC recordkeeping system. 5 RELATED LEGISLATION AND DOCUMENTS National Road Traffic Act 93 of 1996 National Environmental Management Act 107 of 1998 Occupational Health and Safety Act 85 of 1993 Compensation for Occupational Injuries and Diseases Act 30 of 1993. Incident reporting document. Emergency Team Organization.', true, 'manager', 'PRO 002', null, '2026-01-02', '2027-01-02', 'x/H&S-20260923T091746Z-1-001/H&S/Procedures/2.Spill Response and Clean-Up Procedure.pdf', true, '{}'::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, category = excluded.category, content = excluded.content, text = excluded.text, locked = excluded.locked, owner_role = excluded.owner_role, doc_code = excluded.doc_code, revision = excluded.revision, effective_date = excluded.effective_date, review_date = excluded.review_date, _src = excluded._src, _seed = excluded._seed, data = excluded.data where public.docs._seed and public.docs.updated_by is not distinct from public.docs.created_by;
insert into public.docs (id, title, category, content, text, locked, owner_role, doc_code, revision, effective_date, review_date, _src, _seed, data) values ('doc-procedure-3-158fc1', 'Grievance Procedure', 'procedure', '<h3>LI-PRO-003</h3>
<p>Revision 00 Effective Date: 02-01-2026 Next Revision Date: 02-01-2027</p>
<p>Introduction Landscapers INC Grievance Procedure</p>
<p>The objective of the Grievance Procedure is to provide a formal channel for the upward communication of grievances and to settle them as soon as possible in the interests of sound labour relations at Landscapers INC. Grievance is defined as an individual employee''s or group of employees'' dissatisfactions pertaining to:</p>
<ul><li>an inter-personal problem with another employee at work; or</li><li>his or their immediate work situation at the Company.</li></ul>
<p>Guarantee An employee can submit a grievance without fear that he will be victimized or discriminated against by management. Procedure to Be Followed</p>
<p>1.0 No employee or his employee representative shall leave his normal place of work or engage in any grievance discussion without the prior permission of his manager. Such permission shall not be unreasonably withheld.</p>
<p>2.0 Employee representatives may only act within their own Company. In the case of a preferred employee representative being absent, an alternative representative should be approached by an employee for assistance.</p>
<p>Steps in the Grievance Procedure Step 1 – Supervisor/Person Responsible</p>
<p>1.1.</p>
<p>In step 1, the employee must discuss his grievance with the Supervisor/person responsible. 1.2.</p>
<p>The Supervisor/person responsible will endeavor to solve the problem within two (2) working days and inform the employee.</p>
<p>1.3.</p>
<p>Should the employee not be satisfied with the outcome, he may proceed to Step 2. 1.4.</p>
<p>Should the grievance concern the Supervisor/person responsible, the employee may proceed immediately to Step 2.</p>
<p>Step 2 - Manager</p>
<p>2.1.</p>
<p>The employee must complete a grievance form with all the relevant details. He may be assisted by his employee representative to do this. The form is handed in to the Manager. 2.2.</p>
<p>The Manager will endeavour to solve the problem within two (2) working days and inform the employee.</p>
<p>2.3.</p>
<p>Should the employee not be satisfied with the outcome, he may proceed to Step 3.</p>
<p>Step 3 – Manager / Director</p>
<p>3.1.</p>
<p>The matter is referred to the Manager / Director by handing him the grievance form, together with any other further relevant written information.</p>
<p>3.2.</p>
<p>The Manager / Director shall hold an enquiry into the grievance, attended by the employee, his representative (if required) and any other persons who, in the opinion of the Manager / Director, should attend.</p>
<h3>LI-PRO-003</h3>
<p>Revision 00 Effective Date: 02-01-2026 Next Revision Date: 02-01-2027</p>
<p>3.3.</p>
<p>The Manager / Director must give a decision as soon as possible about which decision shall be final. GRIEVANCE FORM - STAGE ONE Date:</p>
<p>Name of Employee:</p>
<p>Company number:</p>
<p>Nature of Grievance:</p>
<p>Action Proposed by Assistant Manager / Manager:</p>
<p>Signed :</p>
<p>Date:</p>
<p>(Supervisor/Responsible Person/ Manager) I accept the proposed action / I wish to proceed to Stage Two Signed :</p>
<p>Signed:</p>
<p>(Employee / Witness)</p>
<p>(Employee Representative) Date:</p>
<p>Date:</p>', 'LI-PRO-003 Revision 00 Effective Date: 02-01-2026 Next Revision Date: 02-01-2027 Introduction Landscapers INC Grievance Procedure The objective of the Grievance Procedure is to provide a formal channel for the upward communication of grievances and to settle them as soon as possible in the interests of sound labour relations at Landscapers INC. Grievance is defined as an individual employee''s or group of employees'' dissatisfactions pertaining to: an inter-personal problem with another employee at work; or his or their immediate work situation at the Company. Guarantee An employee can submit a grievance without fear that he will be victimized or discriminated against by management. Procedure to Be Followed 1.0 No employee or his employee representative shall leave his normal place of work or engage in any grievance discussion without the prior permission of his manager. Such permission shall not be unreasonably withheld. 2.0 Employee representatives may only act within their own Company. In the case of a preferred employee representative being absent, an alternative representative should be approached by an employee for assistance. Steps in the Grievance Procedure Step 1 – Supervisor/Person Responsible 1.1. In step 1, the employee must discuss his grievance with the Supervisor/person responsible. 1.2. The Supervisor/person responsible will endeavor to solve the problem within two (2) working days and inform the employee. 1.3. Should the employee not be satisfied with the outcome, he may proceed to Step 2. 1.4. Should the grievance concern the Supervisor/person responsible, the employee may proceed immediately to Step 2. Step 2 - Manager 2.1. The employee must complete a grievance form with all the relevant details. He may be assisted by his employee representative to do this. The form is handed in to the Manager. 2.2. The Manager will endeavour to solve the problem within two (2) working days and inform the employee. 2.3. Should the employee not be satisfied with the outcome, he may proceed to Step 3. Step 3 – Manager / Director 3.1. The matter is referred to the Manager / Director by handing him the grievance form, together with any other further relevant written information. 3.2. The Manager / Director shall hold an enquiry into the grievance, attended by the employee, his representative (if required) and any other persons who, in the opinion of the Manager / Director, should attend. LI-PRO-003 Revision 00 Effective Date: 02-01-2026 Next Revision Date: 02-01-2027 3.3. The Manager / Director must give a decision as soon as possible about which decision shall be final. GRIEVANCE FORM - STAGE ONE Date: Name of Employee: Company number: Nature of Grievance: Action Proposed by Assistant Manager / Manager: Signed : Date: (Supervisor/Responsible Person/ Manager) I accept the proposed action / I wish to proceed to Stage Two Signed : Signed: (Employee / Witness) (Employee Representative) Date: Date:', true, 'manager', 'PRO 003', null, '2026-01-02', '2027-01-02', 'x/H&S-20260923T091746Z-1-001/H&S/Procedures/3.Grievance Procedure.pdf', true, '{}'::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, category = excluded.category, content = excluded.content, text = excluded.text, locked = excluded.locked, owner_role = excluded.owner_role, doc_code = excluded.doc_code, revision = excluded.revision, effective_date = excluded.effective_date, review_date = excluded.review_date, _src = excluded._src, _seed = excluded._seed, data = excluded.data where public.docs._seed and public.docs.updated_by is not distinct from public.docs.created_by;
insert into public.docs (id, title, category, content, text, locked, owner_role, doc_code, revision, effective_date, review_date, _src, _seed, data) values ('doc-procedure-4-bd488c', 'Power Equipment Operations Procedure', 'procedure', '<h3>LI-PRO-004</h3>
<p>Revision 00 Effective Date: 02-01-2026 Next Revision Date: 02-01-2027</p>
<p>Landscapers INC Power Equipment Operations Procedure 1. General safety - Safe Operations:</p>
<ul><li>Read All Instructions - failure to follow the safety rules listed below and other basic safety precautions may result in serious personal injury.</li></ul>
<h3>2. Work Area</h3>
<ul><li>Keep children away</li><li>Do not let clients contact tool or extension cord. All clients shall be kept away from work area.</li><li>Do not use power tools in damp or wet locations.</li><li>Keep work areas well lit.</li><li>Do not expose power tools to rain.</li><li>Do not use the tool in the presence of flammable fluids or gases. 3. Personal safety</li><li>Now your power tool - Read and understand the owner''s manual and labels affixed to the tool. Learn its application and limitations as well as the specific potential hazards peculiar to this tool.</li><li>Don''t overreach - always Keep proper footing and balance.</li><li>Watch what you are doing.</li><li>Dress properly -do not wear loose clothing or jewelry; they can be caught in moving parts. Rubber gloves and non-skid footwear is recommended when working outdoors. Wear protective hair covering to contain long hair.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Disconnect tools from power source when not in use, before servicing, when changing wheels etc.</li><li>Keep guards in place, in working order, and in proper adjustment and alignment.</li><li>Remove adjusting keys and wrenches when not in use, before servicing, and when changing wheels.</li><li>Ensure the switch is in the "off" position before plugging in tool.</li><li>Never stand on tool or its stand.</li><li>Check damaged parts for damaged parts before operating the tool.</li><li>Only trained repairmen should attempt all repairs, electrical or mechanical.</li><li>Don''t leave tool until it comes to a complete stop.</li><li>Do not operate electric tools in gaseous or explosive atmosphere.</li><li>Keep handles dry, clean, and free from oil and grease.</li><li>Before connecting the tool to a power source (receptacle, outlet), be sure voltage supplied is the same as that specified on the nameplate of the tool</li><li>Never place your fingers in a position where they could contact cutting tool parts. LI-PRO-004 Revision 00 Effective Date: 02-01-2026 Next Revision Date: 02-01-2027</li><li>Turn the motor switch off and unplug from the power source when not in operation. RESPONSIBILITIES POSITION TITLE ROLE DESCRIPTION OF TASK Safety Team Develop and amend this document Review and amend this document Landscapers INC employees and contractors Adherence All employees and contractors engaged in work shall adhere to the guidelines in this procedure Human Resources Monitoring Monitor and report on employee excessive hours Departmental managers Ensure compliance Ensure compliance with procedure requirements</li></ul>', 'LI-PRO-004 Revision 00 Effective Date: 02-01-2026 Next Revision Date: 02-01-2027 Landscapers INC Power Equipment Operations Procedure 1. General safety - Safe Operations: Read All Instructions - failure to follow the safety rules listed below and other basic safety precautions may result in serious personal injury. 2. Work Area Keep children away Do not let clients contact tool or extension cord. All clients shall be kept away from work area. Do not use power tools in damp or wet locations. Keep work areas well lit. Do not expose power tools to rain. Do not use the tool in the presence of flammable fluids or gases. 3. Personal safety Now your power tool - Read and understand the owner''s manual and labels affixed to the tool. Learn its application and limitations as well as the specific potential hazards peculiar to this tool. Don''t overreach - always Keep proper footing and balance. Watch what you are doing. Dress properly -do not wear loose clothing or jewelry; they can be caught in moving parts. Rubber gloves and non-skid footwear is recommended when working outdoors. Wear protective hair covering to contain long hair. Disconnect tools from power source when not in use, before servicing, when changing wheels etc. Keep guards in place, in working order, and in proper adjustment and alignment. Remove adjusting keys and wrenches when not in use, before servicing, and when changing wheels. Ensure the switch is in the "off" position before plugging in tool. Never stand on tool or its stand. Check damaged parts for damaged parts before operating the tool. Only trained repairmen should attempt all repairs, electrical or mechanical. Don''t leave tool until it comes to a complete stop. Do not operate electric tools in gaseous or explosive atmosphere. Keep handles dry, clean, and free from oil and grease. Before connecting the tool to a power source (receptacle, outlet), be sure voltage supplied is the same as that specified on the nameplate of the tool Never place your fingers in a position where they could contact cutting tool parts. LI-PRO-004 Revision 00 Effective Date: 02-01-2026 Next Revision Date: 02-01-2027 Turn the motor switch off and unplug from the power source when not in operation. RESPONSIBILITIES POSITION TITLE ROLE DESCRIPTION OF TASK Safety Team Develop and amend this document Review and amend this document Landscapers INC employees and contractors Adherence All employees and contractors engaged in work shall adhere to the guidelines in this procedure Human Resources Monitoring Monitor and report on employee excessive hours Departmental managers Ensure compliance Ensure compliance with procedure requirements', true, 'manager', 'PRO 004', null, '2026-01-02', '2027-01-02', 'x/H&S-20260923T091746Z-1-001/H&S/Procedures/4.Power Equipment Operations Procedure.pdf', true, '{}'::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, category = excluded.category, content = excluded.content, text = excluded.text, locked = excluded.locked, owner_role = excluded.owner_role, doc_code = excluded.doc_code, revision = excluded.revision, effective_date = excluded.effective_date, review_date = excluded.review_date, _src = excluded._src, _seed = excluded._seed, data = excluded.data where public.docs._seed and public.docs.updated_by is not distinct from public.docs.created_by;
insert into public.docs (id, title, category, content, text, locked, owner_role, doc_code, revision, effective_date, review_date, _src, _seed, data) values ('doc-procedure-5-16f805', 'Electrical Safety Procedure', 'procedure', '<p>Landscapers INC Electrical Safety Procedure LI-PRO-005 Revision 00 Effective Date: 02-01-2026 Next Revision Date: 02-01-2027</p>
<h3>RESPONSIBILITIES</h3>
<h3>POSITION TITLE</h3>
<h3>ROLE</h3>
<h3>DESCRIPTION OF TASK</h3>
<p>Safety Team Develop and amend this document Review and amend this document Landscapers INC employees and contractors Adherence All employees and contractors engaged in work shall adhere to the guidelines in this procedure Human Resources Monitoring Monitor and report on employee excessive hours Departmental managers Ensure compliance Ensure compliance to procedure requirements</p>
<p>1.</p>
<p>Unauthorized people shall not make or attempt to make repairs to electrical equipment. 2.</p>
<p>All unsafe cords, tools, and other equipment shall be reported immediately. 3.</p>
<p>Avoid stepping on live lines of lighting units or trouble lights when lines are lying on floors. 4.</p>
<p>Defective electrical cords or lines shall be replaced or repaired if possible. 5.</p>
<p>When working with energized lines, personnel wearing rings or other jewelry shall either remove jewelry or wear insulated gloves.</p>
<p>6.</p>
<p>Electric outlets shall not be overloaded.</p>
<p>7.</p>
<p>Outlets providing power to portable electric tools shall be equipped with ground fault interrupt when working in damp or wet conditions.</p>', 'Landscapers INC Electrical Safety Procedure LI-PRO-005 Revision 00 Effective Date: 02-01-2026 Next Revision Date: 02-01-2027 RESPONSIBILITIES POSITION TITLE ROLE DESCRIPTION OF TASK Safety Team Develop and amend this document Review and amend this document Landscapers INC employees and contractors Adherence All employees and contractors engaged in work shall adhere to the guidelines in this procedure Human Resources Monitoring Monitor and report on employee excessive hours Departmental managers Ensure compliance Ensure compliance to procedure requirements 1. Unauthorized people shall not make or attempt to make repairs to electrical equipment. 2. All unsafe cords, tools, and other equipment shall be reported immediately. 3. Avoid stepping on live lines of lighting units or trouble lights when lines are lying on floors. 4. Defective electrical cords or lines shall be replaced or repaired if possible. 5. When working with energized lines, personnel wearing rings or other jewelry shall either remove jewelry or wear insulated gloves. 6. Electric outlets shall not be overloaded. 7. Outlets providing power to portable electric tools shall be equipped with ground fault interrupt when working in damp or wet conditions.', true, 'manager', 'PRO 005', null, '2026-01-02', '2027-01-02', 'x/H&S-20260923T091746Z-1-001/H&S/Procedures/5.Electrical Safety Procedure.pdf', true, '{}'::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, category = excluded.category, content = excluded.content, text = excluded.text, locked = excluded.locked, owner_role = excluded.owner_role, doc_code = excluded.doc_code, revision = excluded.revision, effective_date = excluded.effective_date, review_date = excluded.review_date, _src = excluded._src, _seed = excluded._seed, data = excluded.data where public.docs._seed and public.docs.updated_by is not distinct from public.docs.created_by;
insert into public.docs (id, title, category, content, text, locked, owner_role, doc_code, revision, effective_date, review_date, _src, _seed, data) values ('doc-procedure-6-95cd57', 'Electrical Power Tools Procedure', 'procedure', '<h3>LI-PRO-006</h3>
<p>Revision 00 Effective Date: 02-01-2026 Next Revision Date: 02-01-2027</p>
<p>Landscapers INC Electrical Power Tools Procedure</p>
<p>When working with electric tools, basic safety precautions must always be followed to reduce the risk of fire, electric shock, and personal injury including the following:</p>
<ul><li>Employees will be properly and thoroughly trained before attempting to do any work. Read all instructions thoroughly and learn the applications, operating limitations, and potential hazards of each tool before attempting to operate it.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Avoid dangerous environment. Do not expose power tools to rain. Do not use power tools in damp or wet locations. Keep area well lit. Avoid chemical or corrosive environment. Do not use tools in presence of flammable liquids or gases.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Guard against electric shock. Prevent body contact with ground surfaces. For example: pipes, radiators, ranges, refrigerator enclosures.</li><li>Do not let clients contact tools or extension cord. All clients should be kept away from work area.</li><li>Store idle tools. When not in use, tools will be stored in dry, and high or locked-up place – out of reach of children if the possibility of exposure is there.</li><li>Do not force the tool. It will do the job better and safer at the rate for which it was intended.</li><li>Use right tool. Do not force small tool or attachments to do the job of a heavy-duty tool. Don’t use tool for purpose not intended - for example - do not use a circular saw for cutting tree limbs or logs.</li><li>Dress properly. Do not wear loose clothing or jewelry. Loose clothing, drawstrings and jewelry can be caught in moving parts. Rubber gloves and non-skid footwear are recommended when working outdoors. Wear protective hair covering to contain long hair.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Use safety glasses. Wear safety glasses or goggles while operating power tools. Also face or dust mask if operation creates dust. All persons in the area where power tools are being operated must also wear safety glasses and face or dust mask.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Do not abuse cord. Never carry tool by cord or yank it to disconnect from receptacle. Keep cord from heat, oil, and sharp edges. Have damaged or worn power cord and strain reliever replaced immediately. Do not attempt to repair power cord.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Do not overreach. Keep proper footing and balance at all times.</li><li>Maintain tools with care. Keep tools sharp and clean for better and safer performance. Follow instructions for lubricating and changing accessories. Inspect tool cords periodically and if damaged, have repaired by authorized service facility. Inspect extension cords periodically and replace if damaged. Have all worn, broken, or lost parts replaced immediately. Keep handles dry, clean, and free from oil and grease.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Disconnect tools when not in use, before servicing, and when changing accessories such as blades, bits, cutters, and etc.</li><li>Remove adjusting keys and wrenches. Form habit of checking to see that keys and adjusting wrenches are removed from the tool before turning it on.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Avoid unintentional starting. Do not carry a plugged-in tool with finger on switch. Be sure switch is off when plugging in. Keep hands, body, and clothing clear of blades, bits, cutters, etc. when plugging in the tool.</li></ul>
<h3>LI-PRO-006</h3>
<p>Revision 00 Effective Date: 02-01-2026 Next Revision Date: 02-01-2027</p>
<ul><li>Outdoor use extension cords. When a tool is used outdoors, use only extension cords marked “Suitable for use with outdoor appliances - store indoors when not in use.”</li><li>Stay alert. Watch what you are doing. Use common sense. Do not operate a tool when you are tired or while under the influence of medication, alcohol, or drugs.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Check damaged parts. Check for alignment of moving parts, binding of moving parts, breakage of parts, mounting, and any other conditions that may affect its operation. A guard or other part that is damaged must be properly repaired or replaced by an authorized service centre unless otherwise indicated elsewhere. Have defective switches replaced by authorized service centre. Do not used tool if switch does not turn it on and off.</li><li>Wear ear protection to safeguard against possible hearing loss. RESPONSIBILITIES POSITION TITLE ROLE DESCRIPTION OF TASK Safety Team Develop and amend this document Review and amend this document Landscapers INC employees and contractors Adherence All employees and contractors engaged in work shall adhere to the guidelines in this procedure Human Resources Monitoring Monitor and report on employee excessive hours Departmental managers Ensure compliance Ensure compliance to procedure requirements</li></ul>', 'LI-PRO-006 Revision 00 Effective Date: 02-01-2026 Next Revision Date: 02-01-2027 Landscapers INC Electrical Power Tools Procedure When working with electric tools, basic safety precautions must always be followed to reduce the risk of fire, electric shock, and personal injury including the following: Employees will be properly and thoroughly trained before attempting to do any work. Read all instructions thoroughly and learn the applications, operating limitations, and potential hazards of each tool before attempting to operate it. Avoid dangerous environment. Do not expose power tools to rain. Do not use power tools in damp or wet locations. Keep area well lit. Avoid chemical or corrosive environment. Do not use tools in presence of flammable liquids or gases. Guard against electric shock. Prevent body contact with ground surfaces. For example: pipes, radiators, ranges, refrigerator enclosures. Do not let clients contact tools or extension cord. All clients should be kept away from work area. Store idle tools. When not in use, tools will be stored in dry, and high or locked-up place – out of reach of children if the possibility of exposure is there. Do not force the tool. It will do the job better and safer at the rate for which it was intended. Use right tool. Do not force small tool or attachments to do the job of a heavy-duty tool. Don’t use tool for purpose not intended - for example - do not use a circular saw for cutting tree limbs or logs. Dress properly. Do not wear loose clothing or jewelry. Loose clothing, drawstrings and jewelry can be caught in moving parts. Rubber gloves and non-skid footwear are recommended when working outdoors. Wear protective hair covering to contain long hair. Use safety glasses. Wear safety glasses or goggles while operating power tools. Also face or dust mask if operation creates dust. All persons in the area where power tools are being operated must also wear safety glasses and face or dust mask. Do not abuse cord. Never carry tool by cord or yank it to disconnect from receptacle. Keep cord from heat, oil, and sharp edges. Have damaged or worn power cord and strain reliever replaced immediately. Do not attempt to repair power cord. Do not overreach. Keep proper footing and balance at all times. Maintain tools with care. Keep tools sharp and clean for better and safer performance. Follow instructions for lubricating and changing accessories. Inspect tool cords periodically and if damaged, have repaired by authorized service facility. Inspect extension cords periodically and replace if damaged. Have all worn, broken, or lost parts replaced immediately. Keep handles dry, clean, and free from oil and grease. Disconnect tools when not in use, before servicing, and when changing accessories such as blades, bits, cutters, and etc. Remove adjusting keys and wrenches. Form habit of checking to see that keys and adjusting wrenches are removed from the tool before turning it on. Avoid unintentional starting. Do not carry a plugged-in tool with finger on switch. Be sure switch is off when plugging in. Keep hands, body, and clothing clear of blades, bits, cutters, etc. when plugging in the tool. LI-PRO-006 Revision 00 Effective Date: 02-01-2026 Next Revision Date: 02-01-2027 Outdoor use extension cords. When a tool is used outdoors, use only extension cords marked “Suitable for use with outdoor appliances - store indoors when not in use.” Stay alert. Watch what you are doing. Use common sense. Do not operate a tool when you are tired or while under the influence of medication, alcohol, or drugs. Check damaged parts. Check for alignment of moving parts, binding of moving parts, breakage of parts, mounting, and any other conditions that may affect its operation. A guard or other part that is damaged must be properly repaired or replaced by an authorized service centre unless otherwise indicated elsewhere. Have defective switches replaced by authorized service centre. Do not used tool if switch does not turn it on and off. Wear ear protection to safeguard against possible hearing loss. RESPONSIBILITIES POSITION TITLE ROLE DESCRIPTION OF TASK Safety Team Develop and amend this document Review and amend this document Landscapers INC employees and contractors Adherence All employees and contractors engaged in work shall adhere to the guidelines in this procedure Human Resources Monitoring Monitor and report on employee excessive hours Departmental managers Ensure compliance Ensure compliance to procedure requirements', true, 'manager', 'PRO 006', null, '2026-01-02', '2027-01-02', 'x/H&S-20260923T091746Z-1-001/H&S/Procedures/6.Electrical Power Tools Procedure.pdf', true, '{}'::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, category = excluded.category, content = excluded.content, text = excluded.text, locked = excluded.locked, owner_role = excluded.owner_role, doc_code = excluded.doc_code, revision = excluded.revision, effective_date = excluded.effective_date, review_date = excluded.review_date, _src = excluded._src, _seed = excluded._seed, data = excluded.data where public.docs._seed and public.docs.updated_by is not distinct from public.docs.created_by;
insert into public.docs (id, title, category, content, text, locked, owner_role, doc_code, revision, effective_date, review_date, _src, _seed, data) values ('doc-procedure-7-4b04ec', 'Extension Cords Procedure', 'procedure', '<p>Landscapers INC Extension Cords Procedure LI-PRO-007 Revision 00 Effective Date: 02-01-2026 Next Revision Date: 02-01-2027</p>
<h3>RESPONSIBILITIES</h3>
<h3>POSITION TITLE</h3>
<h3>ROLE</h3>
<h3>DESCRIPTION OF TASK</h3>
<p>Safety team Develop and amend this document Review and amend this document Landscapers INC employees and contractors Adherence All employees and contractors engaged in work shall adhere to the guidelines in this procedure Human Resources Monitoring Monitor and report on employee excessive hours Departmental managers Ensure compliance Ensure compliance to procedure requirements</p>
<p>1. Use extension cords only when necessary and only on a temporary basis.</p>
<p>2. Use cords with polarized plugs or grounded three-pronged plugs. 3. High wattage appliances need special, heavy-duty extension cords. 4. Extension cords used outside should be specifically designed for outdoor use.</p>
<p>5. Always insert plugs fully so that no parts of the prongs are exposed.</p>
<p>6. Never cover cords with rugs or other objects because trapped heat may result in a fire. 7. Do not overload cords with too many tools.</p>
<p>8. If a cord feels hot to the touch, stop using it and throw it away. 9. Do not drive over or roll equipment over a cord.</p>', 'Landscapers INC Extension Cords Procedure LI-PRO-007 Revision 00 Effective Date: 02-01-2026 Next Revision Date: 02-01-2027 RESPONSIBILITIES POSITION TITLE ROLE DESCRIPTION OF TASK Safety team Develop and amend this document Review and amend this document Landscapers INC employees and contractors Adherence All employees and contractors engaged in work shall adhere to the guidelines in this procedure Human Resources Monitoring Monitor and report on employee excessive hours Departmental managers Ensure compliance Ensure compliance to procedure requirements 1. Use extension cords only when necessary and only on a temporary basis. 2. Use cords with polarized plugs or grounded three-pronged plugs. 3. High wattage appliances need special, heavy-duty extension cords. 4. Extension cords used outside should be specifically designed for outdoor use. 5. Always insert plugs fully so that no parts of the prongs are exposed. 6. Never cover cords with rugs or other objects because trapped heat may result in a fire. 7. Do not overload cords with too many tools. 8. If a cord feels hot to the touch, stop using it and throw it away. 9. Do not drive over or roll equipment over a cord.', true, 'manager', 'PRO 007', null, '2026-01-02', '2027-01-02', 'x/H&S-20260923T091746Z-1-001/H&S/Procedures/7.Extension Cords Procedure.pdf', true, '{}'::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, category = excluded.category, content = excluded.content, text = excluded.text, locked = excluded.locked, owner_role = excluded.owner_role, doc_code = excluded.doc_code, revision = excluded.revision, effective_date = excluded.effective_date, review_date = excluded.review_date, _src = excluded._src, _seed = excluded._seed, data = excluded.data where public.docs._seed and public.docs.updated_by is not distinct from public.docs.created_by;
insert into public.docs (id, title, category, content, text, locked, owner_role, doc_code, revision, effective_date, review_date, _src, _seed, data) values ('doc-procedure-8-d327bf', 'Hand and Power Tools Procedure', 'procedure', '<h3>LI-PRO-008</h3>
<p>Revision 00 Effective Date: 02-01-2026 Next Revision Date: 02-01-2027</p>
<h3>RESPONSIBILITIES</h3>
<p>General Landscapers INC Hand and Power Tools Procedure 1.</p>
<p>All tools, regardless of ownership, shall be of an approved type and maintained in good Condition. (Tools are subject to inspection at any time. A supervisor has the authority and responsibility to condemn unsafe tools, regardless of ownership).</p>
<p>2.</p>
<p>Unsafe tools shall be tagged with an unsafe tag to prevent their use.</p>
<p>3.</p>
<p>Employees shall always use the proper tool for the job to be performed. Makeshift and substitute tools shall not be used.</p>
<p>4.</p>
<p>Hammers with metal handles, screwdrivers with metal continuing through the handle, and metallic measuring tapes shall not be used on or near energized electrical circuit or equipment.</p>
<p>5.</p>
<p>Tools shall not be thrown from place to place or from person to person; tools that must be raised or lowered from one elevation to another shall be placed in tool buckets or rigidly attached to hand lines. 6.</p>
<p>Tools shall never be placed unsecured on elevated places.</p>
<p>7.</p>
<p>Rotating tools such as brush cutter, chainsaws and hedge trimmers that become stuck or broken will be repaired, or replaced before further use.</p>
<p>8.</p>
<p>Tools shall be used only for the purposes for which they have been approved. 9.</p>
<p>Tools with sharp edges shall be stored and handled so that they will not cause injury or damage. They shall not be carried in pockets unless suitable protectors are in use to protect the edge. POSITION TITLE ROLE DESCRIPTION OF TASK Safety Team Develop and amend this document Review and amend this document Landscapers INC employees and contractors Adherence All employees and contractors engaged in work shall adhere to the guidelines in this procedure Human Resources Monitoring Monitor and report on employee excessive hours Departmental managers Ensure compliance Ensure compliance to procedure requirements</p>
<h3>LI-PRO-008</h3>
<p>Revision 00 Effective Date: 02-01-2026 Next Revision Date: 02-01-2027</p>
<p>10.</p>
<p>Wooden handles that are loose, cracked, or splintered shall be replaced. The handle shall not be taped or lashed with wire.</p>
<p>11.</p>
<p>Tools shall not be left lying around where they may cause a person to trip or stumble.</p>
<p>12. When working on or above open grating, a canvas or other suitable covering shall be used to cover the grating to prevent tools or parts from dropping to a lower level where others are present, or the danger area shall be barricaded or guarded.</p>
<p>Portable Electric Tools</p>
<p>1.</p>
<p>All powered tools shall be examined prior to use to insure general serviceability and the presence of all applicable safety devices.</p>
<p>2.</p>
<p>Powered tools shall be used only within their design and shall be operated in accordance with the instructions of the manufacturer.</p>
<p>3.</p>
<p>All tools shall be kept in good repair and shall be disconnected from the power source while Repairs or adjustments are being made.</p>
<p>4.</p>
<p>Electrical tools shall not be used where there is hazard of flammable vapours, gases, or dusts.</p>', 'LI-PRO-008 Revision 00 Effective Date: 02-01-2026 Next Revision Date: 02-01-2027 RESPONSIBILITIES General Landscapers INC Hand and Power Tools Procedure 1. All tools, regardless of ownership, shall be of an approved type and maintained in good Condition. (Tools are subject to inspection at any time. A supervisor has the authority and responsibility to condemn unsafe tools, regardless of ownership). 2. Unsafe tools shall be tagged with an unsafe tag to prevent their use. 3. Employees shall always use the proper tool for the job to be performed. Makeshift and substitute tools shall not be used. 4. Hammers with metal handles, screwdrivers with metal continuing through the handle, and metallic measuring tapes shall not be used on or near energized electrical circuit or equipment. 5. Tools shall not be thrown from place to place or from person to person; tools that must be raised or lowered from one elevation to another shall be placed in tool buckets or rigidly attached to hand lines. 6. Tools shall never be placed unsecured on elevated places. 7. Rotating tools such as brush cutter, chainsaws and hedge trimmers that become stuck or broken will be repaired, or replaced before further use. 8. Tools shall be used only for the purposes for which they have been approved. 9. Tools with sharp edges shall be stored and handled so that they will not cause injury or damage. They shall not be carried in pockets unless suitable protectors are in use to protect the edge. POSITION TITLE ROLE DESCRIPTION OF TASK Safety Team Develop and amend this document Review and amend this document Landscapers INC employees and contractors Adherence All employees and contractors engaged in work shall adhere to the guidelines in this procedure Human Resources Monitoring Monitor and report on employee excessive hours Departmental managers Ensure compliance Ensure compliance to procedure requirements LI-PRO-008 Revision 00 Effective Date: 02-01-2026 Next Revision Date: 02-01-2027 10. Wooden handles that are loose, cracked, or splintered shall be replaced. The handle shall not be taped or lashed with wire. 11. Tools shall not be left lying around where they may cause a person to trip or stumble. 12. When working on or above open grating, a canvas or other suitable covering shall be used to cover the grating to prevent tools or parts from dropping to a lower level where others are present, or the danger area shall be barricaded or guarded. Portable Electric Tools 1. All powered tools shall be examined prior to use to insure general serviceability and the presence of all applicable safety devices. 2. Powered tools shall be used only within their design and shall be operated in accordance with the instructions of the manufacturer. 3. All tools shall be kept in good repair and shall be disconnected from the power source while Repairs or adjustments are being made. 4. Electrical tools shall not be used where there is hazard of flammable vapours, gases, or dusts.', true, 'manager', 'PRO 008', null, '2026-01-02', '2027-01-02', 'x/H&S-20260923T091746Z-1-001/H&S/Procedures/8.Hand and Power Tools Procedure.pdf', true, '{}'::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, category = excluded.category, content = excluded.content, text = excluded.text, locked = excluded.locked, owner_role = excluded.owner_role, doc_code = excluded.doc_code, revision = excluded.revision, effective_date = excluded.effective_date, review_date = excluded.review_date, _src = excluded._src, _seed = excluded._seed, data = excluded.data where public.docs._seed and public.docs.updated_by is not distinct from public.docs.created_by;
insert into public.docs (id, title, category, content, text, locked, owner_role, doc_code, revision, effective_date, review_date, _src, _seed, data) values ('doc-procedure-9-729963', 'Machine Guarding Procedure', 'procedure', '<h3>LI-PRO-009</h3>
<p>Revision 00 Effective Date: 02-01-2026 Next Revision Date: 02-01-2027</p>
<h3>RESPONSIBILITIES</h3>
<p>Landscapers INC Machine Guarding Procedure</p>
<h3>POSITION TITLE</h3>
<h3>ROLE</h3>
<h3>DESCRIPTION OF TASK</h3>
<p>Safety Team Develop and amend this document Review and amend this document Landscapers INC employees and contractors Adherence All employees and contractors engaged in work shall adhere to the guidelines in this procedure Human Resources Monitoring Monitor and report on employee excessive hours Departmental managers Ensure compliance Ensure compliance to procedure requirements Do not operate any machinery without complete and clear instructions from your supervisor. It is his/her responsibility to make certain that each employee knows and understands the proper operation of the machine as well as any potential hazards involved. If you have any questions or concerns about the hazards or operation of any machine, do not run it before your supervisor has answered your questions. Since machine-related accidents are usually profoundly serious, any written rules or supervisor’s directions, Which are not explicitly followed, will result in strong disciplinary actions.</p>
<ul><li>No machine should be oiled, cleaned, or adjusted while it is in operation, unless specific provisions have been made for this purpose.</li><li>Safety devices should not be by-passed, blocked, or tied down.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Guards are placed at all hazardous points on the machine. They must be in place when the machinery is in operation. When this is not the case, a supervisor signed Danger Tag with printed operating instructions must be posted on the unguarded area and on the safety switch. The department head and safety officer must be duly informed.</li></ul>
<ul><li>If, of necessity, a guard must be removed and the machine power operated, keep a safe distance.</li></ul>
<p>Never attempt a repair or adjustment in an area that cannot be reached safely.</p>
<ul><li>Do not make any alterations on your own. Tell your supervisor if you feel a guard needs changing.</li><li>You may make only those operating adjustments stated in the machine operation instructions.</li></ul>
<h3>LI-PRO-009</h3>
<p>Revision 00 Effective Date: 02-01-2026 Next Revision Date: 02-01-2027</p>
<ul><li>When you leave your machine. No machine should be left running unattended unless specifically designed for that purpose.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Wait for the machine to stop! Do not try to slow down or brake a moving machine by hand or with a makeshift device. If there is a problem, notify your supervisor. He/she will inform the proper authorities.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Before clearing a jam is sure to turn the safety switch OFF unless there are other written procedures from the Safety Department.</li><li>Make sure everyone is clear of the machine before you start it.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Adjusting tools or keys should not be left in places where they can fall, slide, or be thrown into the machine when it is started.</li><li>Only use those machines and equipment for which you have been trained and authorized.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Do not wear jewelry, gloves, neckties, long sleeves, or loose clothing around machines. Hair should not be loose or straggly. Revolving shafts can catch onto these. Extreme caution is always necessary around running machinery.</li><li>Machines should always be clean and free of rags, tools, or other devices.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Use the proper brush, hook, or tool to remove residue such as chips or shavings. Never use your hands or an air hose.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Do not use pins, projecting set screws, etc. on rotating parts unless properly guarded.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Report all hazards to your supervisor.</li></ul>', 'LI-PRO-009 Revision 00 Effective Date: 02-01-2026 Next Revision Date: 02-01-2027 RESPONSIBILITIES Landscapers INC Machine Guarding Procedure POSITION TITLE ROLE DESCRIPTION OF TASK Safety Team Develop and amend this document Review and amend this document Landscapers INC employees and contractors Adherence All employees and contractors engaged in work shall adhere to the guidelines in this procedure Human Resources Monitoring Monitor and report on employee excessive hours Departmental managers Ensure compliance Ensure compliance to procedure requirements Do not operate any machinery without complete and clear instructions from your supervisor. It is his/her responsibility to make certain that each employee knows and understands the proper operation of the machine as well as any potential hazards involved. If you have any questions or concerns about the hazards or operation of any machine, do not run it before your supervisor has answered your questions. Since machine-related accidents are usually profoundly serious, any written rules or supervisor’s directions, Which are not explicitly followed, will result in strong disciplinary actions. No machine should be oiled, cleaned, or adjusted while it is in operation, unless specific provisions have been made for this purpose. Safety devices should not be by-passed, blocked, or tied down. Guards are placed at all hazardous points on the machine. They must be in place when the machinery is in operation. When this is not the case, a supervisor signed Danger Tag with printed operating instructions must be posted on the unguarded area and on the safety switch. The department head and safety officer must be duly informed. If, of necessity, a guard must be removed and the machine power operated, keep a safe distance. Never attempt a repair or adjustment in an area that cannot be reached safely. Do not make any alterations on your own. Tell your supervisor if you feel a guard needs changing. You may make only those operating adjustments stated in the machine operation instructions. LI-PRO-009 Revision 00 Effective Date: 02-01-2026 Next Revision Date: 02-01-2027 When you leave your machine. No machine should be left running unattended unless specifically designed for that purpose. Wait for the machine to stop! Do not try to slow down or brake a moving machine by hand or with a makeshift device. If there is a problem, notify your supervisor. He/she will inform the proper authorities. Before clearing a jam is sure to turn the safety switch OFF unless there are other written procedures from the Safety Department. Make sure everyone is clear of the machine before you start it. Adjusting tools or keys should not be left in places where they can fall, slide, or be thrown into the machine when it is started. Only use those machines and equipment for which you have been trained and authorized. Do not wear jewelry, gloves, neckties, long sleeves, or loose clothing around machines. Hair should not be loose or straggly. Revolving shafts can catch onto these. Extreme caution is always necessary around running machinery. Machines should always be clean and free of rags, tools, or other devices. Use the proper brush, hook, or tool to remove residue such as chips or shavings. Never use your hands or an air hose. Do not use pins, projecting set screws, etc. on rotating parts unless properly guarded. Report all hazards to your supervisor.', true, 'manager', 'PRO 009', null, '2026-01-02', '2027-01-02', 'x/H&S-20260923T091746Z-1-001/H&S/Procedures/9.Machine Guarding Procedure.pdf', true, '{}'::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, category = excluded.category, content = excluded.content, text = excluded.text, locked = excluded.locked, owner_role = excluded.owner_role, doc_code = excluded.doc_code, revision = excluded.revision, effective_date = excluded.effective_date, review_date = excluded.review_date, _src = excluded._src, _seed = excluded._seed, data = excluded.data where public.docs._seed and public.docs.updated_by is not distinct from public.docs.created_by;
insert into public.docs (id, title, category, content, text, locked, owner_role, doc_code, _src, _seed, data) values ('doc-sop-asset-management-sop-2c9d13', 'Asset Management SOP', 'sop', '<h2>Asset Management Standard Operating Procedure (SOP)</h2>
<h3>1. Purpose</h3>
<p>The purpose of this Standard Operating Procedure (SOP) is to establish a consistent process for the acquisition, registration, allocation, use, inspection, maintenance, and disposal of all company assets.</p>
<p>Effective asset management ensures company assets remain operational, are properly maintained, and achieve their maximum useful life while supporting safe and efficient business operations.</p>
<h2>2. Scope</h2>
<p>This SOP applies to all company-owned assets, including but not limited to:</p>
<p>Company vehicles</p>
<p>Landscaping equipment</p>
<p>Power tools</p>
<p>Hand tools</p>
<p>Machinery</p>
<p>Office equipment</p>
<p>Computers and electronic devices</p>
<p>Furniture</p>
<p>Safety equipment</p>
<p>Any other company-owned assets</p>
<h2>3. Responsibilities</h2>
<h4>Chief Executive Officer (CEO)</h4>
<p>Approve major asset purchases.</p>
<p>Approve disposal of significant assets.</p>
<p>Review asset replacement planning.</p>
<h4>General Manager</h4>
<p>Ensure assets are effectively managed.</p>
<p>Review asset utilisation.</p>
<p>Approve maintenance expenditure within authorised limits.</p>
<h4>Operations Manager</h4>
<p>Maintain the Asset Register.</p>
<p>Allocate company assets.</p>
<p>Conduct inspections.</p>
<p>Schedule preventative maintenance.</p>
<p>Arrange repairs and servicing.</p>
<p>Report damaged or missing assets.</p>
<h4>Employees</h4>
<p>Use company assets responsibly.</p>
<p>Conduct daily inspections where required.</p>
<p>Report damage, faults, or loss immediately.</p>
<p>Keep assets clean and properly stored.</p>
<h2>4. Asset Categories</h2>
<p>Company assets are categorised as:</p>
<p>Vehicles</p>
<p>Equipment</p>
<p>Tools</p>
<p>Machinery</p>
<p>Office Assets</p>
<p>Information Technology</p>
<p>Safety Equipment</p>
<p>Furniture &amp; Fixtures</p>
<h2>5. Asset Registration</h2>
<p>All newly acquired assets must be recorded in the Asset Register before being placed into service.</p>
<p>The Asset Register should include:</p>
<p>Asset Number</p>
<p>Asset Description</p>
<p>Asset Category</p>
<p>Manufacturer</p>
<p>Model</p>
<p>Serial Number</p>
<p>Purchase Date</p>
<p>Purchase Cost</p>
<p>Supplier</p>
<p>Assigned Location</p>
<p>Assigned Employee (where applicable)</p>
<p>Current Condition</p>
<p>Warranty Details</p>
<p>Maintenance Schedule</p>
<p>Disposal Date (if applicable)</p>
<p>No asset may be used unless it has been recorded in the Asset Register.</p>
<h2>6. Asset Allocation</h2>
<p>Before issuing an asset:</p>
<p>Confirm the asset is operational.</p>
<p>Record the employee or department responsible.</p>
<p>Explain the correct use of the asset.</p>
<p>Confirm the employee understands their responsibilities.</p>
<p>Employees remain responsible for company assets allocated to them until they are returned.</p>
<h2>7. Daily Inspections</h2>
<p>Assets used daily must be inspected before use.</p>
<p>Inspection should include:</p>
<p>General condition</p>
<p>Cleanliness</p>
<p>Visible damage</p>
<p>Safety features</p>
<p>Operational performance</p>
<p>Fuel, oil, coolant or battery levels where applicable</p>
<p>Faulty or unsafe assets must not be used.</p>
<h2>8. Preventative Maintenance</h2>
<p>The Operations Manager is responsible for ensuring preventative maintenance is completed according to manufacturer recommendations and company maintenance schedules.</p>
<p>Maintenance may include:</p>
<p>Vehicle servicing</p>
<p>Equipment servicing</p>
<p>Blade sharpening</p>
<p>Oil changes</p>
<p>Lubrication</p>
<p>Battery replacement</p>
<p>Cleaning</p>
<p>Calibration where applicable</p>
<p>All maintenance must be recorded in the Maintenance Register.</p>
<h2>9. Repairs</h2>
<p>If an asset becomes damaged or faulty:</p>
<p>Remove the asset from service if unsafe.</p>
<p>Report the fault immediately.</p>
<p>Record the issue in the Maintenance Register.</p>
<p>Arrange repair or replacement.</p>
<p>Update the Asset Register once repairs have been completed.</p>
<p>Employees may not repair company assets unless authorised.</p>
<h2>10. Asset Storage</h2>
<p>After use:</p>
<p>Clean all equipment.</p>
<p>Return assets to their designated storage location.</p>
<p>Secure valuable assets.</p>
<p>Charge batteries where required.</p>
<p>Lock vehicles and equipment storage areas.</p>
<p>Proper storage helps maximise the lifespan of company assets.</p>
<h2>11. Asset Disposal</h2>
<p>Assets may be disposed of when they:</p>
<p>Become uneconomical to repair.</p>
<p>Reach the end of their useful life.</p>
<p>Become obsolete.</p>
<p>Are replaced by newer equipment.</p>
<p>Before disposal:</p>
<p>Obtain management approval.</p>
<p>Remove the asset from the Asset Register.</p>
<p>Record the disposal method and date.</p>
<h2>12. Records</h2>
<p>The following records form part of the Asset Management System:</p>
<p>Asset Register</p>
<p>Maintenance Register</p>
<p>Service Records</p>
<p>Warranty Documents</p>
<p>Purchase Invoices</p>
<h2>13. Key Performance Indicators (KPIs)</h2>
<p>Management should monitor:</p>
<p>Asset availability</p>
<p>Equipment downtime</p>
<p>Preventative maintenance compliance</p>
<p>Vehicle servicing compliance</p>
<p>Asset losses</p>
<p>Asset damage incidents</p>
<p>Repair costs</p>
<p>Asset utilisation</p>
<h2>14. Review</h2>
<p>This SOP will be reviewed periodically to ensure company assets are effectively managed, maintenance programs remain current, and assets continue to support safe, reliable, and efficient business operations.</p>
<p>(images embedded: 0)</p>', 'Asset Management Standard Operating Procedure (SOP) 1. Purpose The purpose of this Standard Operating Procedure (SOP) is to establish a consistent process for the acquisition, registration, allocation, use, inspection, maintenance, and disposal of all company assets. Effective asset management ensures company assets remain operational, are properly maintained, and achieve their maximum useful life while supporting safe and efficient business operations. 2. Scope This SOP applies to all company-owned assets, including but not limited to: Company vehicles Landscaping equipment Power tools Hand tools Machinery Office equipment Computers and electronic devices Furniture Safety equipment Any other company-owned assets 3. Responsibilities Chief Executive Officer (CEO) Approve major asset purchases. Approve disposal of significant assets. Review asset replacement planning. General Manager Ensure assets are effectively managed. Review asset utilisation. Approve maintenance expenditure within authorised limits. Operations Manager Maintain the Asset Register. Allocate company assets. Conduct inspections. Schedule preventative maintenance. Arrange repairs and servicing. Report damaged or missing assets. Employees Use company assets responsibly. Conduct daily inspections where required. Report damage, faults, or loss immediately. Keep assets clean and properly stored. 4. Asset Categories Company assets are categorised as: Vehicles Equipment Tools Machinery Office Assets Information Technology Safety Equipment Furniture & Fixtures 5. Asset Registration All newly acquired assets must be recorded in the Asset Register before being placed into service. The Asset Register should include: Asset Number Asset Description Asset Category Manufacturer Model Serial Number Purchase Date Purchase Cost Supplier Assigned Location Assigned Employee (where applicable) Current Condition Warranty Details Maintenance Schedule Disposal Date (if applicable) No asset may be used unless it has been recorded in the Asset Register. 6. Asset Allocation Before issuing an asset: Confirm the asset is operational. Record the employee or department responsible. Explain the correct use of the asset. Confirm the employee understands their responsibilities. Employees remain responsible for company assets allocated to them until they are returned. 7. Daily Inspections Assets used daily must be inspected before use. Inspection should include: General condition Cleanliness Visible damage Safety features Operational performance Fuel, oil, coolant or battery levels where applicable Faulty or unsafe assets must not be used. 8. Preventative Maintenance The Operations Manager is responsible for ensuring preventative maintenance is completed according to manufacturer recommendations and company maintenance schedules. Maintenance may include: Vehicle servicing Equipment servicing Blade sharpening Oil changes Lubrication Battery replacement Cleaning Calibration where applicable All maintenance must be recorded in the Maintenance Register. 9. Repairs If an asset becomes damaged or faulty: Remove the asset from service if unsafe. Report the fault immediately. Record the issue in the Maintenance Register. Arrange repair or replacement. Update the Asset Register once repairs have been completed. Employees may not repair company assets unless authorised. 10. Asset Storage After use: Clean all equipment. Return assets to their designated storage location. Secure valuable assets. Charge batteries where required. Lock vehicles and equipment storage areas. Proper storage helps maximise the lifespan of company assets. 11. Asset Disposal Assets may be disposed of when they: Become uneconomical to repair. Reach the end of their useful life. Become obsolete. Are replaced by newer equipment. Before disposal: Obtain management approval. Remove the asset from the Asset Register. Record the disposal method and date. 12. Records The following records form part of the Asset Management System: Asset Register Maintenance Register Service Records Warranty Documents Purchase Invoices 13. Key Performance Indicators (KPIs) Management should monitor: Asset availability Equipment downtime Preventative maintenance compliance Vehicle servicing compliance Asset losses Asset damage incidents Repair costs Asset utilisation 14. Review This SOP will be reviewed periodically to ensure company assets are effectively managed, maintenance programs remain current, and assets continue to support safe, reliable, and efficient business operations. (images embedded: 0)', true, 'manager', null, 'x/SOPS-20260923T091755Z-1-001/SOPS/Asset Management Standard Operating Procedure (SOP).docx', true, '{}'::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, category = excluded.category, content = excluded.content, text = excluded.text, locked = excluded.locked, owner_role = excluded.owner_role, doc_code = excluded.doc_code, _src = excluded._src, _seed = excluded._seed, data = excluded.data where public.docs._seed and public.docs.updated_by is not distinct from public.docs.created_by;
insert into public.docs (id, title, category, content, text, locked, owner_role, doc_code, _src, _seed, data) values ('doc-sop-equipment-management-sop-71a248', 'Equipment Management SOP', 'sop', '<p>Equipment Management Standard Operating Procedure (SOP)</p>
<p>1. Purpose</p>
<p>To ensure all company equipment is used safely, maintained correctly, and remains in good working condition.</p>
<p>2. Scope</p>
<p>Applies to all employees who use, transport or maintain company equipment.</p>
<p>3. Responsibilities</p>
<p>Operations Manager</p>
<p>* Maintain the Equipment Register.</p>
<p>* Schedule maintenance.</p>
<p>* Replace damaged equipment when approved.</p>
<p>Team Supervisor</p>
<p>* Inspect equipment before and after each job.</p>
<p>* Report defects immediately.</p>
<p>Employees</p>
<p>* Use equipment correctly.</p>
<p>* Clean equipment after use.</p>
<p>* Report faults immediately.</p>
<p>4. Procedure</p>
<p>Before Each Job</p>
<p>* Inspect equipment.</p>
<p>* Confirm equipment is operational.</p>
<p>* Load required equipment.</p>
<p>* Record missing or damaged items.</p>
<p>During the Job</p>
<p>* Operate equipment safely.</p>
<p>* Prevent misuse.</p>
<p>* Protect equipment from unnecessary damage.</p>
<p>After Each Job</p>
<p>* Clean equipment.</p>
<p>* Inspect for damage.</p>
<p>* Return equipment to storage.</p>
<p>* Report faults requiring repair.</p>
<p>5. Records</p>
<p>* Equipment Register</p>
<p>* Maintenance Register</p>
<p>* Incident Register</p>
<p>6. KPIs</p>
<p>* Equipment availability</p>
<p>* Equipment downtime</p>
<p>* Damage incidents</p>
<p>* Preventative maintenance compliance</p>
<p>⸻</p>
<p>(images embedded: 0)</p>', 'Equipment Management Standard Operating Procedure (SOP) 1. Purpose To ensure all company equipment is used safely, maintained correctly, and remains in good working condition. 2. Scope Applies to all employees who use, transport or maintain company equipment. 3. Responsibilities Operations Manager * Maintain the Equipment Register. * Schedule maintenance. * Replace damaged equipment when approved. Team Supervisor * Inspect equipment before and after each job. * Report defects immediately. Employees * Use equipment correctly. * Clean equipment after use. * Report faults immediately. 4. Procedure Before Each Job * Inspect equipment. * Confirm equipment is operational. * Load required equipment. * Record missing or damaged items. During the Job * Operate equipment safely. * Prevent misuse. * Protect equipment from unnecessary damage. After Each Job * Clean equipment. * Inspect for damage. * Return equipment to storage. * Report faults requiring repair. 5. Records * Equipment Register * Maintenance Register * Incident Register 6. KPIs * Equipment availability * Equipment downtime * Damage incidents * Preventative maintenance compliance ⸻ (images embedded: 0)', true, 'manager', null, 'x/SOPS-20260923T091755Z-1-001/SOPS/Equipment Management Standard Operating Procedure (SOP).docx', true, '{}'::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, category = excluded.category, content = excluded.content, text = excluded.text, locked = excluded.locked, owner_role = excluded.owner_role, doc_code = excluded.doc_code, _src = excluded._src, _seed = excluded._seed, data = excluded.data where public.docs._seed and public.docs.updated_by is not distinct from public.docs.created_by;
insert into public.docs (id, title, category, content, text, locked, owner_role, doc_code, _src, _seed, data) values ('doc-sop-health-safety-sop-bddcc0', 'Health & Safety SOP', 'sop', '<p>Health &amp; Safety Standard Operating Procedure (SOP)</p>
<p>1. Purpose</p>
<p>To provide a safe working environment for employees, clients and the public.</p>
<p>2. Responsibilities</p>
<p>All employees must:</p>
<p>* Wear required PPE.</p>
<p>* Follow safe work practices.</p>
<p>* Report hazards.</p>
<p>* Stop unsafe work.</p>
<p>Operations Manager:</p>
<p>* Conduct safety inspections.</p>
<p>* Ensure staff training.</p>
<p>* Maintain safety equipment.</p>
<p>3. Procedure</p>
<p>Before Work</p>
<p>* Conduct a site risk assessment.</p>
<p>* Wear required PPE.</p>
<p>* Inspect equipment.</p>
<p>During Work</p>
<p>* Maintain safe work areas.</p>
<p>* Use equipment correctly.</p>
<p>* Follow company procedures.</p>
<p>After Work</p>
<p>* Leave the site safe.</p>
<p>* Remove hazards.</p>
<p>* Report incidents.</p>
<p>4. Emergency Response</p>
<p>In an emergency:</p>
<p>* Stop work.</p>
<p>* Protect people.</p>
<p>* Contact emergency services if required.</p>
<p>* Notify management.</p>
<p>* Record the incident.</p>
<p>5. KPIs</p>
<p>* Lost-time injuries</p>
<p>* Safety inspections completed</p>
<p>* PPE compliance</p>
<p>* Near-miss reporting</p>
<p>(images embedded: 0)</p>', 'Health & Safety Standard Operating Procedure (SOP) 1. Purpose To provide a safe working environment for employees, clients and the public. 2. Responsibilities All employees must: * Wear required PPE. * Follow safe work practices. * Report hazards. * Stop unsafe work. Operations Manager: * Conduct safety inspections. * Ensure staff training. * Maintain safety equipment. 3. Procedure Before Work * Conduct a site risk assessment. * Wear required PPE. * Inspect equipment. During Work * Maintain safe work areas. * Use equipment correctly. * Follow company procedures. After Work * Leave the site safe. * Remove hazards. * Report incidents. 4. Emergency Response In an emergency: * Stop work. * Protect people. * Contact emergency services if required. * Notify management. * Record the incident. 5. KPIs * Lost-time injuries * Safety inspections completed * PPE compliance * Near-miss reporting (images embedded: 0)', true, 'manager', null, 'x/SOPS-20260923T091755Z-1-001/SOPS/Health & Safety Standard Operating Procedure (SOP).docx', true, '{}'::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, category = excluded.category, content = excluded.content, text = excluded.text, locked = excluded.locked, owner_role = excluded.owner_role, doc_code = excluded.doc_code, _src = excluded._src, _seed = excluded._seed, data = excluded.data where public.docs._seed and public.docs.updated_by is not distinct from public.docs.created_by;
insert into public.docs (id, title, category, content, text, locked, owner_role, doc_code, _src, _seed, data) values ('doc-sop-incident-reporting-sop-4ece0c', 'Incident Reporting SOP', 'sop', '<h2>Incident Reporting Standard Operating Procedure (SOP)</h2>
<h3>1. Purpose</h3>
<p>The purpose of this SOP is to establish a consistent process for reporting, investigating, and resolving workplace incidents. Prompt and accurate reporting helps protect employees, clients, company assets, and the reputation of the business while supporting continuous improvement.</p>
<h2>2. Scope</h2>
<p>This SOP applies to all employees, contractors, and managers involved in any incident that occurs during company operations, whether on company premises, at a client site, or while travelling for company business.</p>
<h2>3. Responsibilities</h2>
<h4>General Manager</h4>
<p>Ensure all incidents are investigated.</p>
<p>Review investigation findings.</p>
<p>Approve corrective actions where required.</p>
<p>Monitor incident trends and implement improvements.</p>
<h4>Operations Manager</h4>
<p>Respond to reported incidents.</p>
<p>Secure the affected area if necessary.</p>
<p>Conduct or assist with investigations.</p>
<p>Update the Incident Register.</p>
<p>Implement corrective actions.</p>
<h4>Team Supervisor</h4>
<p>Report incidents immediately.</p>
<p>Assist injured persons where appropriate.</p>
<p>Gather initial information.</p>
<p>Preserve evidence if required.</p>
<p>Cooperate during investigations.</p>
<h4>Employees</h4>
<p>Report all incidents immediately.</p>
<p>Stop unsafe work where necessary.</p>
<p>Cooperate with investigations.</p>
<p>Follow corrective actions implemented by management.</p>
<h2>4. Reportable Incidents</h2>
<p>The following must be reported immediately:</p>
<p>Employee injuries</p>
<p>Vehicle accidents</p>
<p>Equipment damage or failure</p>
<p>Damage to client property</p>
<p>Client complaints relating to operations</p>
<p>Environmental incidents</p>
<p>Safety hazards</p>
<p>Near misses</p>
<p>Theft or vandalism</p>
<p>Any event that may expose the company to legal or financial risk</p>
<h2>5. Procedure</h2>
<h3>Step 1 – Respond Immediately</h3>
<p>When an incident occurs:</p>
<p>Stop work if it is unsafe to continue.</p>
<p>Protect people from further harm.</p>
<p>Provide first aid if required.</p>
<p>Contact emergency services if necessary.</p>
<h3>Step 2 – Notify Management</h3>
<p>The employee or Team Supervisor must immediately notify the Operations Manager.</p>
<p>Where the incident is serious, the General Manager must also be informed without delay.</p>
<h3>Step 3 – Secure the Area</h3>
<p>Where appropriate:</p>
<p>Prevent further access to the area.</p>
<p>Protect company property.</p>
<p>Preserve evidence for investigation.</p>
<p>Do not disturb the scene unless necessary for safety.</p>
<h3>Step 4 – Record the Incident</h3>
<p>The Operations Manager must record:</p>
<p>Date and time</p>
<p>Location</p>
<p>People involved</p>
<p>Description of the incident</p>
<p>Injuries or damage sustained</p>
<p>Immediate actions taken</p>
<p>Witness details</p>
<p>Photos (where applicable)</p>
<p>The incident must then be entered into the Incident Register.</p>
<h3>Step 5 – Investigate</h3>
<p>Management must determine:</p>
<p>What happened</p>
<p>Why it happened</p>
<p>Contributing factors</p>
<p>Whether procedures were followed</p>
<p>Corrective actions required</p>
<p>The objective is to prevent recurrence, not to assign blame.</p>
<h3>Step 6 – Corrective Actions</h3>
<p>Where required:</p>
<p>Repair damaged equipment.</p>
<p>Replace unsafe equipment.</p>
<p>Provide additional staff training.</p>
<p>Update procedures if necessary.</p>
<p>Improve safety controls.</p>
<p>Monitor implementation of corrective actions.</p>
<h3>Step 7 – Close the Incident</h3>
<p>An incident may only be closed once:</p>
<p>The investigation has been completed.</p>
<p>Corrective actions have been implemented.</p>
<p>All records have been updated.</p>
<p>Management has reviewed the outcome.</p>
<h2>6. Records</h2>
<p>The following documents must be updated where applicable:</p>
<p>Incident Register</p>
<p>Maintenance Register</p>
<p>Vehicle Register</p>
<p>Equipment Register</p>
<p>Job Register</p>
<p>Investigation Report</p>
<p>Operations Dashboard</p>
<h2>7. Key Performance Indicators (KPIs)</h2>
<p>The company aims to achieve:</p>
<p>Timely reporting of all incidents.</p>
<p>Prompt completion of investigations.</p>
<p>Reduction in repeat incidents.</p>
<p>Reduction in workplace injuries.</p>
<p>Reduction in equipment damage.</p>
<p>Continuous improvement in operational safety.</p>
<h2>8. Review</h2>
<p>This SOP will be reviewed periodically to ensure the company’s incident management process remains effective, compliant, and aligned with operational best practices.</p>
<p>(images embedded: 0)</p>', 'Incident Reporting Standard Operating Procedure (SOP) 1. Purpose The purpose of this SOP is to establish a consistent process for reporting, investigating, and resolving workplace incidents. Prompt and accurate reporting helps protect employees, clients, company assets, and the reputation of the business while supporting continuous improvement. 2. Scope This SOP applies to all employees, contractors, and managers involved in any incident that occurs during company operations, whether on company premises, at a client site, or while travelling for company business. 3. Responsibilities General Manager Ensure all incidents are investigated. Review investigation findings. Approve corrective actions where required. Monitor incident trends and implement improvements. Operations Manager Respond to reported incidents. Secure the affected area if necessary. Conduct or assist with investigations. Update the Incident Register. Implement corrective actions. Team Supervisor Report incidents immediately. Assist injured persons where appropriate. Gather initial information. Preserve evidence if required. Cooperate during investigations. Employees Report all incidents immediately. Stop unsafe work where necessary. Cooperate with investigations. Follow corrective actions implemented by management. 4. Reportable Incidents The following must be reported immediately: Employee injuries Vehicle accidents Equipment damage or failure Damage to client property Client complaints relating to operations Environmental incidents Safety hazards Near misses Theft or vandalism Any event that may expose the company to legal or financial risk 5. Procedure Step 1 – Respond Immediately When an incident occurs: Stop work if it is unsafe to continue. Protect people from further harm. Provide first aid if required. Contact emergency services if necessary. Step 2 – Notify Management The employee or Team Supervisor must immediately notify the Operations Manager. Where the incident is serious, the General Manager must also be informed without delay. Step 3 – Secure the Area Where appropriate: Prevent further access to the area. Protect company property. Preserve evidence for investigation. Do not disturb the scene unless necessary for safety. Step 4 – Record the Incident The Operations Manager must record: Date and time Location People involved Description of the incident Injuries or damage sustained Immediate actions taken Witness details Photos (where applicable) The incident must then be entered into the Incident Register. Step 5 – Investigate Management must determine: What happened Why it happened Contributing factors Whether procedures were followed Corrective actions required The objective is to prevent recurrence, not to assign blame. Step 6 – Corrective Actions Where required: Repair damaged equipment. Replace unsafe equipment. Provide additional staff training. Update procedures if necessary. Improve safety controls. Monitor implementation of corrective actions. Step 7 – Close the Incident An incident may only be closed once: The investigation has been completed. Corrective actions have been implemented. All records have been updated. Management has reviewed the outcome. 6. Records The following documents must be updated where applicable: Incident Register Maintenance Register Vehicle Register Equipment Register Job Register Investigation Report Operations Dashboard 7. Key Performance Indicators (KPIs) The company aims to achieve: Timely reporting of all incidents. Prompt completion of investigations. Reduction in repeat incidents. Reduction in workplace injuries. Reduction in equipment damage. Continuous improvement in operational safety. 8. Review This SOP will be reviewed periodically to ensure the company’s incident management process remains effective, compliant, and aligned with operational best practices. (images embedded: 0)', true, 'manager', null, 'x/SOPS-20260923T091755Z-1-001/SOPS/Incident Reporting Standard Operating Procedure (SOP).docx', true, '{}'::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, category = excluded.category, content = excluded.content, text = excluded.text, locked = excluded.locked, owner_role = excluded.owner_role, doc_code = excluded.doc_code, _src = excluded._src, _seed = excluded._seed, data = excluded.data where public.docs._seed and public.docs.updated_by is not distinct from public.docs.created_by;
insert into public.docs (id, title, category, content, text, locked, owner_role, doc_code, _src, _seed, data) values ('doc-sop-job-execution-sop-60a8b2', 'Job Execution SOP', 'sop', '<h2>Job Execution Standard Operating Procedure (SOP)</h2>
<h3>1. Purpose</h3>
<p>The purpose of this SOP is to establish a consistent process for executing all jobs safely, efficiently, and professionally. It ensures every job is completed to the company’s quality standards while delivering an excellent customer experience.</p>
<h2>2. Scope</h2>
<p>This SOP applies to all operational employees, supervisors, and managers involved in delivering services from the time the team arrives on site until the job is completed and closed.</p>
<h2>3. Responsibilities</h2>
<h4>General Manager</h4>
<p>Monitor overall operational performance.</p>
<p>Resolve operational issues beyond the authority of the Operations Manager.</p>
<p>Review job performance and identify opportunities for improvement.</p>
<h4>Operations Manager</h4>
<p>Ensure all jobs are properly prepared before departure.</p>
<p>Allocate teams, vehicles, equipment, and materials.</p>
<p>Monitor job progress.</p>
<p>Support operational staff where required.</p>
<p>Ensure company procedures are followed.</p>
<h4>Team Supervisor</h4>
<p>Lead the team on site.</p>
<p>Conduct the pre-job briefing.</p>
<p>Ensure safety requirements are followed.</p>
<p>Monitor workmanship throughout the job.</p>
<p>Communicate with the client when required.</p>
<p>Complete the final quality inspection.</p>
<h4>Operational Employees</h4>
<p>Perform work according to company standards.</p>
<p>Follow all safety procedures.</p>
<p>Wear required PPE.</p>
<p>Use company equipment responsibly.</p>
<p>Report hazards, incidents, and equipment faults immediately.</p>
<p>Represent the company professionally.</p>
<h2>4. Procedure</h2>
<h3>Step 1 – Arrival on Site</h3>
<p>Upon arrival, the Team Supervisor must:</p>
<p>Park vehicles safely.</p>
<p>Introduce the team to the client where appropriate.</p>
<p>Confirm the work to be completed.</p>
<p>Identify any site-specific hazards.</p>
<p>Ensure the work area is safe before commencing.</p>
<h3>Step 2 – Team Briefing</h3>
<p>Before work begins, the Team Supervisor must brief the team on:</p>
<p>Scope of work.</p>
<p>Individual responsibilities.</p>
<p>Safety requirements.</p>
<p>Client expectations.</p>
<p>Special instructions.</p>
<p>Estimated completion time.</p>
<h3>Step 3 – Equipment and Safety Checks</h3>
<p>Before commencing work:</p>
<p>Inspect all equipment.</p>
<p>Confirm PPE is being worn correctly.</p>
<p>Ensure all required materials are available.</p>
<p>Remove any faulty equipment from service.</p>
<p>Verify vehicles and equipment are positioned safely.</p>
<h3>Step 4 – Execute the Work</h3>
<p>The operational team must:</p>
<p>Complete the work according to company standards.</p>
<p>Follow approved work methods.</p>
<p>Protect the client’s property.</p>
<p>Maintain a clean and organised work area.</p>
<p>Work efficiently without compromising quality or safety.</p>
<p>Immediately report any unexpected issues.</p>
<h3>Step 5 – Client Communication</h3>
<p>During the job:</p>
<p>Keep the client informed of progress where appropriate.</p>
<p>Communicate any delays or unforeseen issues.</p>
<p>Obtain approval before performing additional work outside the agreed scope.</p>
<p>Maintain a professional and courteous attitude at all times.</p>
<h3>Step 6 – Monitor Quality</h3>
<p>Throughout the job, the Team Supervisor must:</p>
<p>Inspect completed sections of work.</p>
<p>Correct defects immediately.</p>
<p>Ensure work continues to meet company standards.</p>
<p>Provide guidance to team members where required.</p>
<h3>Step 7 – Site Clean-Up</h3>
<p>Once the work is complete:</p>
<p>Remove all waste and debris.</p>
<p>Clean the work area.</p>
<p>Return equipment to the vehicle.</p>
<p>Ensure no company tools or materials remain on site.</p>
<p>Leave the client’s property in a clean and professional condition.</p>
<h3>Step 8 – Final Quality Inspection</h3>
<p>Before leaving site, the Team Supervisor must confirm:</p>
<p>All agreed work has been completed.</p>
<p>Company quality standards have been met.</p>
<p>The site is clean and safe.</p>
<p>Equipment has been packed away.</p>
<p>Any outstanding issues have been resolved or communicated.</p>
<h3>Step 9 – Job Completion</h3>
<p>Before departing:</p>
<p>Thank the client where applicable.</p>
<p>Obtain client sign-off if required.</p>
<p>Record any additional work completed.</p>
<p>Report damaged equipment or incidents.</p>
<p>Notify the Operations Manager that the job has been completed.</p>
<h3>Step 10 – Update Operational Records</h3>
<p>Following completion of the job:</p>
<p>Update the Job Register.</p>
<p>Complete the Quality Control Checklist.</p>
<p>Record maintenance requirements if identified.</p>
<p>Submit job photos where applicable.</p>
<p>Notify Administration that the job is ready for invoicing.</p>
<h2>5. Records</h2>
<p>The following documents must be updated where applicable:</p>
<p>Job Register</p>
<p>Daily Schedule</p>
<p>Quality Control Checklist</p>
<p>Equipment Register</p>
<p>Vehicle Register</p>
<p>Maintenance Register</p>
<p>Incident Register</p>
<h2>6. Key Performance Indicators (KPIs)</h2>
<p>The Operations Team should aim to achieve:</p>
<p>Jobs completed safely.</p>
<p>Jobs completed on schedule.</p>
<p>High-quality workmanship.</p>
<p>Minimal rework.</p>
<p>High client satisfaction.</p>
<p>Zero preventable incidents.</p>
<p>Professional client interactions.</p>
<p>Proper care of company assets.</p>
<h2>7. Review</h2>
<p>This SOP will be reviewed periodically to ensure operational efficiency, consistent service delivery, and continuous improvement as the business grows.</p>
<p>(images embedded: 0)</p>', 'Job Execution Standard Operating Procedure (SOP) 1. Purpose The purpose of this SOP is to establish a consistent process for executing all jobs safely, efficiently, and professionally. It ensures every job is completed to the company’s quality standards while delivering an excellent customer experience. 2. Scope This SOP applies to all operational employees, supervisors, and managers involved in delivering services from the time the team arrives on site until the job is completed and closed. 3. Responsibilities General Manager Monitor overall operational performance. Resolve operational issues beyond the authority of the Operations Manager. Review job performance and identify opportunities for improvement. Operations Manager Ensure all jobs are properly prepared before departure. Allocate teams, vehicles, equipment, and materials. Monitor job progress. Support operational staff where required. Ensure company procedures are followed. Team Supervisor Lead the team on site. Conduct the pre-job briefing. Ensure safety requirements are followed. Monitor workmanship throughout the job. Communicate with the client when required. Complete the final quality inspection. Operational Employees Perform work according to company standards. Follow all safety procedures. Wear required PPE. Use company equipment responsibly. Report hazards, incidents, and equipment faults immediately. Represent the company professionally. 4. Procedure Step 1 – Arrival on Site Upon arrival, the Team Supervisor must: Park vehicles safely. Introduce the team to the client where appropriate. Confirm the work to be completed. Identify any site-specific hazards. Ensure the work area is safe before commencing. Step 2 – Team Briefing Before work begins, the Team Supervisor must brief the team on: Scope of work. Individual responsibilities. Safety requirements. Client expectations. Special instructions. Estimated completion time. Step 3 – Equipment and Safety Checks Before commencing work: Inspect all equipment. Confirm PPE is being worn correctly. Ensure all required materials are available. Remove any faulty equipment from service. Verify vehicles and equipment are positioned safely. Step 4 – Execute the Work The operational team must: Complete the work according to company standards. Follow approved work methods. Protect the client’s property. Maintain a clean and organised work area. Work efficiently without compromising quality or safety. Immediately report any unexpected issues. Step 5 – Client Communication During the job: Keep the client informed of progress where appropriate. Communicate any delays or unforeseen issues. Obtain approval before performing additional work outside the agreed scope. Maintain a professional and courteous attitude at all times. Step 6 – Monitor Quality Throughout the job, the Team Supervisor must: Inspect completed sections of work. Correct defects immediately. Ensure work continues to meet company standards. Provide guidance to team members where required. Step 7 – Site Clean-Up Once the work is complete: Remove all waste and debris. Clean the work area. Return equipment to the vehicle. Ensure no company tools or materials remain on site. Leave the client’s property in a clean and professional condition. Step 8 – Final Quality Inspection Before leaving site, the Team Supervisor must confirm: All agreed work has been completed. Company quality standards have been met. The site is clean and safe. Equipment has been packed away. Any outstanding issues have been resolved or communicated. Step 9 – Job Completion Before departing: Thank the client where applicable. Obtain client sign-off if required. Record any additional work completed. Report damaged equipment or incidents. Notify the Operations Manager that the job has been completed. Step 10 – Update Operational Records Following completion of the job: Update the Job Register. Complete the Quality Control Checklist. Record maintenance requirements if identified. Submit job photos where applicable. Notify Administration that the job is ready for invoicing. 5. Records The following documents must be updated where applicable: Job Register Daily Schedule Quality Control Checklist Equipment Register Vehicle Register Maintenance Register Incident Register 6. Key Performance Indicators (KPIs) The Operations Team should aim to achieve: Jobs completed safely. Jobs completed on schedule. High-quality workmanship. Minimal rework. High client satisfaction. Zero preventable incidents. Professional client interactions. Proper care of company assets. 7. Review This SOP will be reviewed periodically to ensure operational efficiency, consistent service delivery, and continuous improvement as the business grows. (images embedded: 0)', true, 'manager', null, 'x/SOPS-20260923T091755Z-1-001/SOPS/Job Execution Standard Operating Procedure (SOP).docx', true, '{}'::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, category = excluded.category, content = excluded.content, text = excluded.text, locked = excluded.locked, owner_role = excluded.owner_role, doc_code = excluded.doc_code, _src = excluded._src, _seed = excluded._seed, data = excluded.data where public.docs._seed and public.docs.updated_by is not distinct from public.docs.created_by;
insert into public.docs (id, title, category, content, text, locked, owner_role, doc_code, _src, _seed, data) values ('doc-sop-job-scheduling-sop-dc0dc2', 'Job Scheduling SOP', 'sop', '<h2>Job Scheduling Standard Operating Procedure (SOP)</h2>
<h3>1. Purpose</h3>
<p>The purpose of this SOP is to establish a consistent process for scheduling jobs, allocating resources, and ensuring every job is properly prepared before work begins.</p>
<p>Effective scheduling ensures that staff, vehicles, equipment, and materials are available while delivering jobs on time and maintaining high customer satisfaction.</p>
<h2>2. Scope</h2>
<p>This procedure applies to all jobs undertaken by the company from the moment a quotation has been accepted until the job is ready for execution.</p>
<h2>3. Responsibility</h2>
<h4>Sales Representative</h4>
<p>Notify the Operations Manager once a quotation has been accepted.</p>
<p>Ensure all client information is complete.</p>
<h4>Operations Manager</h4>
<p>Schedule all jobs.</p>
<p>Allocate teams.</p>
<p>Allocate vehicles.</p>
<p>Allocate equipment.</p>
<p>Confirm material requirements.</p>
<p>Update the Job Register.</p>
<p>Communicate the schedule to the operational team.</p>
<h4>General Manager</h4>
<p>Resolve scheduling conflicts.</p>
<p>Approve major schedule changes when required.</p>
<h2>4. Procedure</h2>
<h3>Step 1 – Confirm Job Details</h3>
<p>Before scheduling, confirm:</p>
<p>Client name</p>
<p>Contact details</p>
<p>Service required</p>
<p>Site address</p>
<p>Date required</p>
<p>Estimated duration</p>
<p>Special client requirements</p>
<h3>Step 2 – Create the Job</h3>
<p>Enter the following information into the Job Register:</p>
<p>Job Number</p>
<p>Client Name</p>
<p>Property Address</p>
<p>Service Type</p>
<p>Scheduled Date</p>
<p>Scheduled Start Time</p>
<p>Estimated Completion Time</p>
<p>Assigned Team</p>
<p>Assigned Vehicle</p>
<p>Assigned Supervisor</p>
<p>Job Status</p>
<h3>Step 3 – Allocate Resources</h3>
<p>Assign:</p>
<p>Operational team</p>
<p>Supervisor</p>
<p>Company vehicle</p>
<p>Required equipment</p>
<p>Required materials</p>
<p>Ensure all required resources are available before confirming the booking.</p>
<h3>Step 4 – Check Availability</h3>
<p>Confirm:</p>
<p>Staff availability</p>
<p>Vehicle availability</p>
<p>Equipment availability</p>
<p>Material availability</p>
<p>If any conflicts exist, resolve them before finalising the schedule.</p>
<h3>Step 5 – Update the Schedule</h3>
<p>Update the company’s Daily Schedule with:</p>
<p>Job order</p>
<p>Arrival times</p>
<p>Team allocation</p>
<p>Vehicle allocation</p>
<p>Estimated completion times</p>
<h3>Step 6 – Notify the Team</h3>
<p>Before the job begins, communicate:</p>
<p>Client name</p>
<p>Site location</p>
<p>Arrival time</p>
<p>Scope of work</p>
<p>Special instructions</p>
<p>Equipment required</p>
<h3>Step 7 – Prepare for the Job</h3>
<p>Ensure:</p>
<p>Equipment is available and operational.</p>
<p>Vehicles are ready.</p>
<p>Materials are loaded.</p>
<p>PPE is available.</p>
<p>Job documentation is complete.</p>
<h2>5. Schedule Changes</h2>
<p>If changes occur:</p>
<p>Update the Job Register immediately.</p>
<p>Inform the client if required.</p>
<p>Notify the operational team.</p>
<p>Reallocate resources where necessary.</p>
<h2>6. Records</h2>
<p>The following documents must be updated:</p>
<p>Job Register</p>
<p>Daily Schedule</p>
<p>Equipment Register (if applicable)</p>
<p>Vehicle Register (if applicable)</p>
<h2>7. Key Performance Indicators (KPIs)</h2>
<p>The Operations Manager should aim to achieve:</p>
<p>Jobs scheduled accurately.</p>
<p>Minimal scheduling conflicts.</p>
<p>On-time job starts.</p>
<p>Efficient use of staff and vehicles.</p>
<p>Low equipment downtime.</p>
<p>High client satisfaction.</p>
<h2>8. Review</h2>
<p>This SOP will be reviewed periodically to improve scheduling efficiency and support the company’s operational growth.</p>
<p>(images embedded: 0)</p>', 'Job Scheduling Standard Operating Procedure (SOP) 1. Purpose The purpose of this SOP is to establish a consistent process for scheduling jobs, allocating resources, and ensuring every job is properly prepared before work begins. Effective scheduling ensures that staff, vehicles, equipment, and materials are available while delivering jobs on time and maintaining high customer satisfaction. 2. Scope This procedure applies to all jobs undertaken by the company from the moment a quotation has been accepted until the job is ready for execution. 3. Responsibility Sales Representative Notify the Operations Manager once a quotation has been accepted. Ensure all client information is complete. Operations Manager Schedule all jobs. Allocate teams. Allocate vehicles. Allocate equipment. Confirm material requirements. Update the Job Register. Communicate the schedule to the operational team. General Manager Resolve scheduling conflicts. Approve major schedule changes when required. 4. Procedure Step 1 – Confirm Job Details Before scheduling, confirm: Client name Contact details Service required Site address Date required Estimated duration Special client requirements Step 2 – Create the Job Enter the following information into the Job Register: Job Number Client Name Property Address Service Type Scheduled Date Scheduled Start Time Estimated Completion Time Assigned Team Assigned Vehicle Assigned Supervisor Job Status Step 3 – Allocate Resources Assign: Operational team Supervisor Company vehicle Required equipment Required materials Ensure all required resources are available before confirming the booking. Step 4 – Check Availability Confirm: Staff availability Vehicle availability Equipment availability Material availability If any conflicts exist, resolve them before finalising the schedule. Step 5 – Update the Schedule Update the company’s Daily Schedule with: Job order Arrival times Team allocation Vehicle allocation Estimated completion times Step 6 – Notify the Team Before the job begins, communicate: Client name Site location Arrival time Scope of work Special instructions Equipment required Step 7 – Prepare for the Job Ensure: Equipment is available and operational. Vehicles are ready. Materials are loaded. PPE is available. Job documentation is complete. 5. Schedule Changes If changes occur: Update the Job Register immediately. Inform the client if required. Notify the operational team. Reallocate resources where necessary. 6. Records The following documents must be updated: Job Register Daily Schedule Equipment Register (if applicable) Vehicle Register (if applicable) 7. Key Performance Indicators (KPIs) The Operations Manager should aim to achieve: Jobs scheduled accurately. Minimal scheduling conflicts. On-time job starts. Efficient use of staff and vehicles. Low equipment downtime. High client satisfaction. 8. Review This SOP will be reviewed periodically to improve scheduling efficiency and support the company’s operational growth. (images embedded: 0)', true, 'manager', null, 'x/SOPS-20260923T091755Z-1-001/SOPS/Job Scheduling Standard Operating Procedure (SOP).docx', true, '{}'::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, category = excluded.category, content = excluded.content, text = excluded.text, locked = excluded.locked, owner_role = excluded.owner_role, doc_code = excluded.doc_code, _src = excluded._src, _seed = excluded._seed, data = excluded.data where public.docs._seed and public.docs.updated_by is not distinct from public.docs.created_by;
insert into public.docs (id, title, category, content, text, locked, owner_role, doc_code, _src, _seed, data) values ('doc-sop-quality-control-sop-3a57e0', 'Quality Control SOP', 'sop', '<h2>Quality Control Standard Operating Procedure (SOP)</h2>
<h3>1. Purpose</h3>
<p>The purpose of this SOP is to establish a consistent quality control process for all services provided by the company. It ensures that every job is inspected before completion, meets the company’s quality standards, and delivers an exceptional customer experience.</p>
<h2>2. Scope</h2>
<p>This SOP applies to all operational employees, supervisors, and managers responsible for completing and inspecting work before a job is officially closed.</p>
<h2>3. Responsibilities</h2>
<h4>Operations Manager</h4>
<p>Ensure quality inspections are completed.</p>
<p>Investigate recurring quality issues.</p>
<p>Implement corrective actions where required.</p>
<p>Review quality performance.</p>
<h4>Team Supervisor</h4>
<p>Conduct the final quality inspection.</p>
<p>Ensure all work meets company standards.</p>
<p>Correct any defects before leaving site.</p>
<p>Complete the Quality Control Checklist.</p>
<h4>Operational Employees</h4>
<p>Complete work according to company standards.</p>
<p>Immediately correct any defects identified.</p>
<p>Assist with final inspections when required.</p>
<h2>4. Quality Standards</h2>
<p>Every completed job must meet the following standards:</p>
<p>Work is completed as quoted.</p>
<p>Company workmanship standards are achieved.</p>
<p>Client property is respected.</p>
<p>Work area is left clean and tidy.</p>
<p>Equipment has been used correctly.</p>
<p>No avoidable damage has occurred.</p>
<p>Safety standards have been maintained.</p>
<p>Client expectations have been met.</p>
<h2>5. Quality Inspection Procedure</h2>
<h3>Step 1 – Self-Inspection</h3>
<p>Before requesting a final inspection, the operational team must:</p>
<p>Review the completed work.</p>
<p>Correct any obvious defects.</p>
<p>Remove waste and debris.</p>
<p>Clean equipment where required.</p>
<h3>Step 2 – Supervisor Inspection</h3>
<p>The Team Supervisor must inspect:</p>
<p>Overall workmanship.</p>
<p>Completion of the agreed scope.</p>
<p>Cleanliness of the site.</p>
<p>Safety of the completed work.</p>
<p>Client-specific requirements.</p>
<p>Any additional work completed.</p>
<h3>Step 3 – Corrective Action</h3>
<p>If defects are identified:</p>
<p>Record the issue.</p>
<p>Correct the defect immediately where possible.</p>
<p>Re-inspect the affected area.</p>
<p>Confirm that the required standard has been achieved.</p>
<p>No job may be signed off until all identified defects have been resolved or an agreed action plan has been communicated to the client.</p>
<h3>Step 4 – Client Walkthrough (Where Applicable)</h3>
<p>Where appropriate:</p>
<p>Walk the client through the completed work.</p>
<p>Explain what has been completed.</p>
<p>Address any concerns.</p>
<p>Obtain client acknowledgement or sign-off if required.</p>
<h3>Step 5 – Final Approval</h3>
<p>The job may only be marked as complete once:</p>
<p>The work meets company standards.</p>
<p>All defects have been corrected.</p>
<p>The site is clean.</p>
<p>Equipment has been packed away.</p>
<p>The Quality Control Checklist has been completed.</p>
<h2>6. Quality Control Checklist</h2>
<p>Before leaving site, confirm:</p>
<p>Scope of work completed.</p>
<p>Workmanship meets company standards.</p>
<p>Site left clean and tidy.</p>
<p>Equipment removed.</p>
<p>Waste disposed of correctly.</p>
<p>Client concerns addressed.</p>
<p>Photos taken (where applicable).</p>
<p>Job ready for invoicing.</p>
<h2>7. Non-Conformance Procedure</h2>
<p>Where work does not meet company standards:</p>
<p>Record the issue.</p>
<p>Notify the Operations Manager.</p>
<p>Determine the cause.</p>
<p>Correct the work.</p>
<p>Review whether additional training or process improvements are required.</p>
<p>Recurring quality issues should be discussed during management meetings to prevent future occurrences.</p>
<h2>8. Records</h2>
<p>The following documents may be updated:</p>
<p>Quality Control Checklist</p>
<p>Job Register</p>
<p>Incident Register (if applicable)</p>
<p>Client Feedback Register (if used)</p>
<p>Operations Dashboard</p>
<h2>9. Key Performance Indicators (KPIs)</h2>
<p>The Operations Team should aim to achieve:</p>
<p>High first-time quality.</p>
<p>Minimal rework.</p>
<p>Low client complaint rates.</p>
<p>High client satisfaction.</p>
<p>Consistent compliance with company standards.</p>
<p>Completion of all quality inspections.</p>
<h2>10. Review</h2>
<p>This SOP will be reviewed periodically to ensure quality standards continue to improve and remain aligned with the company’s commitment to professional service delivery and customer satisfaction.</p>
<p>(images embedded: 0)</p>', 'Quality Control Standard Operating Procedure (SOP) 1. Purpose The purpose of this SOP is to establish a consistent quality control process for all services provided by the company. It ensures that every job is inspected before completion, meets the company’s quality standards, and delivers an exceptional customer experience. 2. Scope This SOP applies to all operational employees, supervisors, and managers responsible for completing and inspecting work before a job is officially closed. 3. Responsibilities Operations Manager Ensure quality inspections are completed. Investigate recurring quality issues. Implement corrective actions where required. Review quality performance. Team Supervisor Conduct the final quality inspection. Ensure all work meets company standards. Correct any defects before leaving site. Complete the Quality Control Checklist. Operational Employees Complete work according to company standards. Immediately correct any defects identified. Assist with final inspections when required. 4. Quality Standards Every completed job must meet the following standards: Work is completed as quoted. Company workmanship standards are achieved. Client property is respected. Work area is left clean and tidy. Equipment has been used correctly. No avoidable damage has occurred. Safety standards have been maintained. Client expectations have been met. 5. Quality Inspection Procedure Step 1 – Self-Inspection Before requesting a final inspection, the operational team must: Review the completed work. Correct any obvious defects. Remove waste and debris. Clean equipment where required. Step 2 – Supervisor Inspection The Team Supervisor must inspect: Overall workmanship. Completion of the agreed scope. Cleanliness of the site. Safety of the completed work. Client-specific requirements. Any additional work completed. Step 3 – Corrective Action If defects are identified: Record the issue. Correct the defect immediately where possible. Re-inspect the affected area. Confirm that the required standard has been achieved. No job may be signed off until all identified defects have been resolved or an agreed action plan has been communicated to the client. Step 4 – Client Walkthrough (Where Applicable) Where appropriate: Walk the client through the completed work. Explain what has been completed. Address any concerns. Obtain client acknowledgement or sign-off if required. Step 5 – Final Approval The job may only be marked as complete once: The work meets company standards. All defects have been corrected. The site is clean. Equipment has been packed away. The Quality Control Checklist has been completed. 6. Quality Control Checklist Before leaving site, confirm: Scope of work completed. Workmanship meets company standards. Site left clean and tidy. Equipment removed. Waste disposed of correctly. Client concerns addressed. Photos taken (where applicable). Job ready for invoicing. 7. Non-Conformance Procedure Where work does not meet company standards: Record the issue. Notify the Operations Manager. Determine the cause. Correct the work. Review whether additional training or process improvements are required. Recurring quality issues should be discussed during management meetings to prevent future occurrences. 8. Records The following documents may be updated: Quality Control Checklist Job Register Incident Register (if applicable) Client Feedback Register (if used) Operations Dashboard 9. Key Performance Indicators (KPIs) The Operations Team should aim to achieve: High first-time quality. Minimal rework. Low client complaint rates. High client satisfaction. Consistent compliance with company standards. Completion of all quality inspections. 10. Review This SOP will be reviewed periodically to ensure quality standards continue to improve and remain aligned with the company’s commitment to professional service delivery and customer satisfaction. (images embedded: 0)', true, 'manager', null, 'x/SOPS-20260923T091755Z-1-001/SOPS/Quality Control Standard Operating Procedure (SOP).docx', true, '{}'::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, category = excluded.category, content = excluded.content, text = excluded.text, locked = excluded.locked, owner_role = excluded.owner_role, doc_code = excluded.doc_code, _src = excluded._src, _seed = excluded._seed, data = excluded.data where public.docs._seed and public.docs.updated_by is not distinct from public.docs.created_by;
