insert into public.docs (id, title, category, content, text, locked, owner_role, _src, _seed, data) values ('doc-strategy-leadership-development-programm-d5d0bc', 'Leadership Development Programme — General Manager Strategic Development Plan (Anthony Guruvadu)', 'plan', '<h2>Leadership Development Programme</h2>
<p>General Manager Strategic Development Plan</p>
<h2>Strategic Intent</h2>
<p>This document outlines a comprehensive framework designed to cultivate executive-level leadership capabilities and operational excellence.</p>
<p>The objective of this programme is to transition management personnel into proficient, professional leaders capable of autonomous business oversight while upholding the core values and institutional standards of Landscapers Inc.</p>
<h2>Programme Overview</h2>
<p>Name: Anthony Guruvadu</p>
<p>Current Role: General Manager</p>
<p>Business Unit: Landscapers Inc.</p>
<p>Programme Duration: 12 Months</p>
<p>Review Cycle: Weekly coaching sessions supplemented by quarterly performance evaluations.</p>
<p>Commencement Date: 01 August 2026</p>
<p>Completion Date: 01 July 2027</p>
<h2>Executive Leadership Philosophy</h2>
<p>A General Manager’s efficacy is determined not by individual effort, but by organizational outcomes.</p>
<p>Leadership success is appraised based on:</p>
<p>The performance and development of the team.</p>
<p>Client satisfaction and relationship longevity.</p>
<p>The sustained profitability of the business unit.</p>
<p>The integrity and quality of executive leadership.</p>
<p>Strategic problem-solving and decision-making capabilities.</p>
<h2>Baseline Executive Assessment</h2>
<p>Strengths</p>
<p>Organizational loyalty and commitment.</p>
<p>High standards of integrity and transparency.</p>
<p>Proactive ownership of responsibilities.</p>
<p>Exceptional professional work ethic.</p>
<p>Operational, hands-on management approach.</p>
<p>Competent administrative oversight.</p>
<p>Fundamental financial literacy.</p>
<p>Aptitude for continuous professional development.</p>
<p>Development Areas</p>
<p>Leadership presence</p>
<p>Communication skills</p>
<p>Approachability</p>
<p>Daily planning and organisation</p>
<p>Client relationship management</p>
<p>Professional presentation</p>
<p>Team accountability</p>
<p>Time management</p>
<h2>Development Roadmap</h2>
<p>Quarter 1 – Build Structure</p>
<p>Objectives</p>
<p>Create and follow a daily schedule.</p>
<p>Conduct daily morning team briefings.</p>
<p>Improve communication with staff.</p>
<p>Maintain professional appearance every day.</p>
<p>Begin weekly CEO coaching meetings.</p>
<p>Success Measures</p>
<p>Daily planner completed.</p>
<p>Team briefings held consistently.</p>
<p>Weekly priorities achieved.</p>
<p>Improved staff communication.</p>
<p>Professional standard maintained.</p>
<p>Quarter 2 – Lead People</p>
<p>Objectives</p>
<p>Delegates work effectively.</p>
<p>Hold staff accountable.</p>
<p>Conduct one-on-one conversations with employees.</p>
<p>Resolve conflict professionally.</p>
<p>Build confidence when leading the team.</p>
<p>Success Measures</p>
<p>Less CEO involvement in staff issues.</p>
<p>Improved staff discipline.</p>
<p>Clear delegation.</p>
<p>Better morale.</p>
<p>Quarter 3 – Build Client Relationships</p>
<p>Objectives</p>
<p>Visit key clients regularly.</p>
<p>Handle complaints independently.</p>
<p>Improve communication with customers.</p>
<p>Identify opportunities for additional work.</p>
<p>Success Measures</p>
<p>Positive client feedback.</p>
<p>Higher client retention.</p>
<p>Increased upselling.</p>
<p>Reduced customer complaints.</p>
<h2>Programme Success Criteria</h2>
<p>Objectives</p>
<p>Understand monthly financial reports.</p>
<p>Improve profitability.</p>
<p>Identify cost savings.</p>
<p>Present monthly business review to the CEO.</p>
<p>Operate independently.</p>
<p>Success Measures</p>
<p>Monthly reporting completed.</p>
<p>Improved margins.</p>
<p>Independent decision-making.</p>
<p>CEO involvement reduced.</p>
<p>Weekly Coaching Meeting (45 Minutes- 1 hour)</p>
<p>1. Wins</p>
<p>What went well this week?</p>
<p>2. Challenges</p>
<p>What was difficult?</p>
<p>3. KPIs</p>
<p>Revenue</p>
<p>Jobs completed</p>
<p>Staff attendance</p>
<p>Customer issues</p>
<p>Quotes outstanding</p>
<p>Safety incidents</p>
<p>4. Leadership Development</p>
<p>What leadership skill was practised this week?</p>
<p>5. CEO Feedback</p>
<p>One thing done well.</p>
<p>One thing to improve.</p>
<p>6. Next Week</p>
<p>Three priorities.</p>
<h2>Leadership Competency Scorecard</h2>
<p>&lt;TABLE&gt;</p>
<p>Competency | Current | Target | Q1 | Q2 | Q3 | Q4</p>
<p>Leadership Presence | 2.5 | 5.0 |  |  |  |</p>
<p>Communication Skills | 2.0 | 4.0 |  |  |  |</p>
<p>Planning &amp; Organisation | 1.0 | 4.0 |  |  |  |</p>
<p>Client Relationships | 2.0 | 4.0 |  |  |  |</p>
<p>Financial Understanding | 3.0 | 4.0 |  |  |  |</p>
<p>Administration | 4.0 | 4.0 |  |  |  |</p>
<p>Professional Presentation | 2.0 | 5.0 |  |  |  |</p>
<p>Problem Solving | 3.0 | 4.0 |  |  |  |</p>
<p>Accountability | 2.0 | 4.0 |  |  |  |</p>
<p>&lt;/TABLE&gt;</p>
<h2>Executive Commitments</h2>
<p>As CEO, I commit to:</p>
<p>Coach rather than criticise.</p>
<p>Give honest, timely feedback.</p>
<p>Provide opportunities to lead.</p>
<p>Hold consistent weekly meetings.</p>
<p>Set clear expectations.</p>
<p>Recognise progress and celebrate wins.</p>
<p>Develop leaders, not followers.</p>
<h2>Candidate Commitments</h2>
<p>As General Manager, I commit to:</p>
<p>Be coachable.</p>
<p>Lead by example.</p>
<p>Continue learning.</p>
<p>Be organised.</p>
<p>Communicate openly.</p>
<p>Take ownership of results.</p>
<p>Represent the business professionally.</p>
<p>Definition of Success</p>
<p>By the end of this programme, the General Manager should be capable of:</p>
<p>Leading the team confidently.</p>
<p>Managing operations and sales department effectively.</p>
<p>Building strong client relationships.</p>
<p>Running weekly meetings.</p>
<p>Managing staff performance.</p>
<p>Understanding business financials.</p>
<p>Making sound operational decisions.</p>
<p>Representing landscapers inc with professionalism and integrity.</p>
<p>The ultimate goal is to create a leader who strengthens the business every day.</p>
<p>(images embedded: 0)</p>', 'Leadership Development Programme General Manager Strategic Development Plan Strategic Intent This document outlines a comprehensive framework designed to cultivate executive-level leadership capabilities and operational excellence. The objective of this programme is to transition management personnel into proficient, professional leaders capable of autonomous business oversight while upholding the core values and institutional standards of Landscapers Inc. Programme Overview Name: Anthony Guruvadu Current Role: General Manager Business Unit: Landscapers Inc. Programme Duration: 12 Months Review Cycle: Weekly coaching sessions supplemented by quarterly performance evaluations. Commencement Date: 01 August 2026 Completion Date: 01 July 2027 Executive Leadership Philosophy A General Manager’s efficacy is determined not by individual effort, but by organizational outcomes. Leadership success is appraised based on: The performance and development of the team. Client satisfaction and relationship longevity. The sustained profitability of the business unit. The integrity and quality of executive leadership. Strategic problem-solving and decision-making capabilities. Baseline Executive Assessment Strengths Organizational loyalty and commitment. High standards of integrity and transparency. Proactive ownership of responsibilities. Exceptional professional work ethic. Operational, hands-on management approach. Competent administrative oversight. Fundamental financial literacy. Aptitude for continuous professional development. Development Areas Leadership presence Communication skills Approachability Daily planning and organisation Client relationship management Professional presentation Team accountability Time management Development Roadmap Quarter 1 – Build Structure Objectives Create and follow a daily schedule. Conduct daily morning team briefings. Improve communication with staff. Maintain professional appearance every day. Begin weekly CEO coaching meetings. Success Measures Daily planner completed. Team briefings held consistently. Weekly priorities achieved. Improved staff communication. Professional standard maintained. Quarter 2 – Lead People Objectives Delegates work effectively. Hold staff accountable. Conduct one-on-one conversations with employees. Resolve conflict professionally. Build confidence when leading the team. Success Measures Less CEO involvement in staff issues. Improved staff discipline. Clear delegation. Better morale. Quarter 3 – Build Client Relationships Objectives Visit key clients regularly. Handle complaints independently. Improve communication with customers. Identify opportunities for additional work. Success Measures Positive client feedback. Higher client retention. Increased upselling. Reduced customer complaints. Programme Success Criteria Objectives Understand monthly financial reports. Improve profitability. Identify cost savings. Present monthly business review to the CEO. Operate independently. Success Measures Monthly reporting completed. Improved margins. Independent decision-making. CEO involvement reduced. Weekly Coaching Meeting (45 Minutes- 1 hour) 1. Wins What went well this week? 2. Challenges What was difficult? 3. KPIs Revenue Jobs completed Staff attendance Customer issues Quotes outstanding Safety incidents 4. Leadership Development What leadership skill was practised this week? 5. CEO Feedback One thing done well. One thing to improve. 6. Next Week Three priorities. Leadership Competency Scorecard <TABLE> Competency | Current | Target | Q1 | Q2 | Q3 | Q4 Leadership Presence | 2.5 | 5.0 | | | | Communication Skills | 2.0 | 4.0 | | | | Planning & Organisation | 1.0 | 4.0 | | | | Client Relationships | 2.0 | 4.0 | | | | Financial Understanding | 3.0 | 4.0 | | | | Administration | 4.0 | 4.0 | | | | Professional Presentation | 2.0 | 5.0 | | | | Problem Solving | 3.0 | 4.0 | | | | Accountability | 2.0 | 4.0 | | | | </TABLE> Executive Commitments As CEO, I commit to: Coach rather than criticise. Give honest, timely feedback. Provide opportunities to lead. Hold consistent weekly meetings. Set clear expectations. Recognise progress and celebrate wins. Develop leaders, not followers. Candidate Commitments As General Manager, I commit to: Be coachable. Lead by example. Continue learning. Be organised. Communicate openly. Take ownership of results. Represent the business professionally. Definition of Success By the end of this programme, the General Manager should be capable of: Leading the team confidently. Managing operations and sales department effectively. Building strong client relationships. Running weekly meetings. Managing staff performance. Understanding business financials. Making sound operational decisions. Representing landscapers inc with professionalism and integrity. The ultimate goal is to create a leader who strengthens the business every day. (images embedded: 0)', true, 'manager', 'x/General Manager-20260923T091726Z-1-001/General Manager/Landscapers inc Leadership Development Programme Anthony.docx', true, '{}'::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, category = excluded.category, content = excluded.content, text = excluded.text, locked = excluded.locked, owner_role = excluded.owner_role, _src = excluded._src, _seed = excluded._seed, data = excluded.data where public.docs._seed and public.docs.updated_by is not distinct from public.docs.created_by;
insert into public.docs (id, title, category, content, text, locked, owner_role, _src, _seed, data) values ('doc-strategy-operations-department-code-of-c-77400a', 'Operations Department Code of Conduct', 'policy', '<h2>Operations Department Code of Conduct</h2>
<h3>Purpose</h3>
<p>The Operations Department is committed to delivering safe, efficient and high-quality services while maintaining professionalism, accountability and respect in every aspect of our work.</p>
<h3>1. Professionalism</h3>
<p>Conduct yourself professionally at all times.</p>
<p>Treat clients, colleagues and suppliers with respect.</p>
<p>Represent the company positively on and off client sites.</p>
<p>Maintain a positive attitude and strong work ethic.</p>
<h3>2. Integrity</h3>
<p>Be honest in all reporting and communication.</p>
<p>Never falsify records, timesheets or company documents.</p>
<p>Report mistakes immediately so they can be resolved.</p>
<p>Protect company assets and confidential information.</p>
<h3>3. Punctuality &amp; Attendance</h3>
<p>Arrive at work on time and ready to begin the day.</p>
<p>Notify your manager as early as possible if you are unable to attend work.</p>
<p>Complete assigned tasks within agreed timeframes.</p>
<p>Attend all required meetings and briefings.</p>
<h3>4. Health &amp; Safety</h3>
<p>Follow all health and safety procedures.</p>
<p>Wear required Personal Protective Equipment (PPE) at all times.</p>
<p>Never perform unsafe work.</p>
<p>Report hazards, incidents and accidents immediately.</p>
<h3>5. Quality Standards</h3>
<p>Complete every job to the company’s quality standards.</p>
<p>Follow approved Standard Operating Procedures (SOPs).</p>
<p>Never leave a job unfinished without approval.</p>
<p>Take pride in workmanship and attention to detail.</p>
<h3>6. Teamwork</h3>
<p>Support fellow team members.</p>
<p>Communicate clearly and respectfully.</p>
<p>Resolve disagreements professionally.</p>
<p>Share knowledge and assist when required.</p>
<h3>7. Client Service</h3>
<p>Treat every client courteously and professionally.</p>
<p>Keep clients informed of progress where appropriate.</p>
<p>Respond to concerns respectfully and promptly.</p>
<p>Protect client property and maintain clean work areas.</p>
<h3>8. Company Property</h3>
<p>Use company vehicles, equipment and tools responsibly.</p>
<p>Report damaged or missing equipment immediately.</p>
<p>Keep vehicles and equipment clean and well maintained.</p>
<p>Do not use company assets for unauthorised personal purposes.</p>
<h3>9. Communication</h3>
<p>Follow the company’s communication procedures.</p>
<p>Report operational issues promptly.</p>
<p>Keep managers informed of delays or risks.</p>
<p>Maintain accurate operational records.</p>
<h3>10. Confidentiality</h3>
<p>Protect company information.</p>
<p>Do not disclose confidential business or client information without authorisation.</p>
<p>Respect the privacy of colleagues and clients.</p>
<h3>11. Accountability</h3>
<p>Every employee is responsible for:</p>
<p>Completing assigned duties.</p>
<p>Following company policies and SOPs.</p>
<p>Maintaining high performance standards.</p>
<p>Taking ownership of their work.</p>
<p>Continuously improving operational efficiency.</p>
<h3>12. Unacceptable Conduct</h3>
<p>The following may result in disciplinary action:</p>
<p>Dishonesty or fraud</p>
<p>Theft or misuse of company property</p>
<p>Harassment, bullying or discrimination</p>
<p>Substance abuse while on duty</p>
<p>Repeated lateness or absenteeism</p>
<p>Failure to follow safety procedures</p>
<p>Gross negligence</p>
<p>Insubordination</p>
<p>Unprofessional behaviour towards clients or colleagues</p>
<p>Deliberate damage to company assets</p>
<h3>Commitment</h3>
<p>Every member of the Operations Department is expected to uphold these standards and contribute to a safe, efficient, professional and customer-focused workplace. Compliance with this Code of Conduct is a condition of employment and forms part of the company’s operational standards.</p>
<p>(images embedded: 0)</p>', 'Operations Department Code of Conduct Purpose The Operations Department is committed to delivering safe, efficient and high-quality services while maintaining professionalism, accountability and respect in every aspect of our work. 1. Professionalism Conduct yourself professionally at all times. Treat clients, colleagues and suppliers with respect. Represent the company positively on and off client sites. Maintain a positive attitude and strong work ethic. 2. Integrity Be honest in all reporting and communication. Never falsify records, timesheets or company documents. Report mistakes immediately so they can be resolved. Protect company assets and confidential information. 3. Punctuality & Attendance Arrive at work on time and ready to begin the day. Notify your manager as early as possible if you are unable to attend work. Complete assigned tasks within agreed timeframes. Attend all required meetings and briefings. 4. Health & Safety Follow all health and safety procedures. Wear required Personal Protective Equipment (PPE) at all times. Never perform unsafe work. Report hazards, incidents and accidents immediately. 5. Quality Standards Complete every job to the company’s quality standards. Follow approved Standard Operating Procedures (SOPs). Never leave a job unfinished without approval. Take pride in workmanship and attention to detail. 6. Teamwork Support fellow team members. Communicate clearly and respectfully. Resolve disagreements professionally. Share knowledge and assist when required. 7. Client Service Treat every client courteously and professionally. Keep clients informed of progress where appropriate. Respond to concerns respectfully and promptly. Protect client property and maintain clean work areas. 8. Company Property Use company vehicles, equipment and tools responsibly. Report damaged or missing equipment immediately. Keep vehicles and equipment clean and well maintained. Do not use company assets for unauthorised personal purposes. 9. Communication Follow the company’s communication procedures. Report operational issues promptly. Keep managers informed of delays or risks. Maintain accurate operational records. 10. Confidentiality Protect company information. Do not disclose confidential business or client information without authorisation. Respect the privacy of colleagues and clients. 11. Accountability Every employee is responsible for: Completing assigned duties. Following company policies and SOPs. Maintaining high performance standards. Taking ownership of their work. Continuously improving operational efficiency. 12. Unacceptable Conduct The following may result in disciplinary action: Dishonesty or fraud Theft or misuse of company property Harassment, bullying or discrimination Substance abuse while on duty Repeated lateness or absenteeism Failure to follow safety procedures Gross negligence Insubordination Unprofessional behaviour towards clients or colleagues Deliberate damage to company assets Commitment Every member of the Operations Department is expected to uphold these standards and contribute to a safe, efficient, professional and customer-focused workplace. Compliance with this Code of Conduct is a condition of employment and forms part of the company’s operational standards. (images embedded: 0)', true, 'manager', 'x/OPERATIONS MANAGER-20260923T091749Z-1-001/OPERATIONS MANAGER/Operations Department Code of Conduct.docx', true, '{}'::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, category = excluded.category, content = excluded.content, text = excluded.text, locked = excluded.locked, owner_role = excluded.owner_role, _src = excluded._src, _seed = excluded._seed, data = excluded.data where public.docs._seed and public.docs.updated_by is not distinct from public.docs.created_by;
insert into public.docs (id, title, category, content, text, locked, owner_role, _src, _seed, data) values ('doc-strategy-operations-manager-job-descript-7a2cd1', 'Operations Manager Job Description', 'job_description', '<h2>Operations Manager Job Description</h2>
<h3>Position</h3>
<p>Operations Manager</p>
<h3>Reports To</h3>
<p>General Manager (or CEO, depending on company structure)</p>
<h3>Purpose of the Role</h3>
<p>The Operations Manager is responsible for planning, coordinating and overseeing all daily operations to ensure jobs are completed safely, efficiently, on time and to company standards. The role focuses on execution, productivity, quality control and resource management.</p>
<h3>Key Responsibilities</h3>
<h4>1. Daily Operations</h4>
<p>Plan and coordinate daily work schedules.</p>
<p>Allocate staff, vehicles and equipment to jobs.</p>
<p>Ensure all jobs start and finish on time.</p>
<p>Resolve operational issues throughout the day.</p>
<p>Monitor progress of all active jobs.</p>
<h4>2. Staff Management</h4>
<p>Supervise operational employees.</p>
<p>Conduct daily morning briefings.</p>
<p>Ensure staff follow company SOPs.</p>
<p>Monitor attendance and productivity.</p>
<p>Coach and develop team members.</p>
<p>Address operational performance issues.</p>
<h4>3. Quality Control</h4>
<p>Inspect completed work.</p>
<p>Ensure company quality standards are met.</p>
<p>Resolve client complaints relating to operations.</p>
<p>Implement corrective actions where required.</p>
<h4>4. Health &amp; Safety</h4>
<p>Enforce all health and safety procedures.</p>
<p>Ensure staff use PPE correctly.</p>
<p>Report incidents and accidents.</p>
<p>Maintain a safe working environment.</p>
<p>Conduct regular safety inspections.</p>
<h4>5. Equipment &amp; Vehicles</h4>
<p>Manage company vehicles and equipment.</p>
<p>Ensure routine maintenance is completed.</p>
<p>Report breakdowns immediately.</p>
<p>Keep maintenance records up to date.</p>
<p>Minimise equipment downtime.</p>
<h4>6. Inventory &amp; Supplies</h4>
<p>Monitor stock levels.</p>
<p>Order operational materials when required.</p>
<p>Prevent unnecessary wastage.</p>
<p>Ensure teams have the equipment needed for each job.</p>
<h4>7. Client Service</h4>
<p>Liaise with clients during projects.</p>
<p>Resolve operational concerns professionally.</p>
<p>Ensure teams represent the company well.</p>
<p>Maintain high customer satisfaction.</p>
<h4>8. Reporting</h4>
<p>Submit:</p>
<p>Daily Operations Report</p>
<p>Weekly Operations Report</p>
<p>Maintenance Report</p>
<p>Incident Reports</p>
<p>Staff Attendance Report</p>
<h3>Key Performance Indicators (KPIs)</h3>
<p>Jobs completed on schedule</p>
<p>Labour productivity</p>
<p>Quality inspection score</p>
<p>Client satisfaction</p>
<p>Equipment downtime</p>
<p>Vehicle maintenance compliance</p>
<p>Safety incidents</p>
<p>Team attendance</p>
<p>Budget adherence</p>
<p>Operational efficiency</p>
<h3>Required Skills</h3>
<p>Strong leadership</p>
<p>Planning and organisation</p>
<p>Problem-solving</p>
<p>Decision-making</p>
<p>Communication</p>
<p>Time management</p>
<p>Conflict resolution</p>
<p>Computer literacy</p>
<p>Ability to work under pressure</p>
<h3>Authority</h3>
<p>The Operations Manager may:</p>
<p>Allocate staff and resources.</p>
<p>Schedule operational work.</p>
<p>Recommend disciplinary action.</p>
<p>Stop unsafe work.</p>
<p>Approve operational purchases within authorised limits.</p>
<p>Make day-to-day operational decisions.</p>
<h3>Success Looks Like</h3>
<p>A successful Operations Manager delivers jobs on time, maintains high quality standards, keeps staff productive and safe, ensures equipment is well maintained, and enables the General Manager and CEO to focus on strategic growth rather than day-to-day execution.</p>
<p>(images embedded: 0)</p>', 'Operations Manager Job Description Position Operations Manager Reports To General Manager (or CEO, depending on company structure) Purpose of the Role The Operations Manager is responsible for planning, coordinating and overseeing all daily operations to ensure jobs are completed safely, efficiently, on time and to company standards. The role focuses on execution, productivity, quality control and resource management. Key Responsibilities 1. Daily Operations Plan and coordinate daily work schedules. Allocate staff, vehicles and equipment to jobs. Ensure all jobs start and finish on time. Resolve operational issues throughout the day. Monitor progress of all active jobs. 2. Staff Management Supervise operational employees. Conduct daily morning briefings. Ensure staff follow company SOPs. Monitor attendance and productivity. Coach and develop team members. Address operational performance issues. 3. Quality Control Inspect completed work. Ensure company quality standards are met. Resolve client complaints relating to operations. Implement corrective actions where required. 4. Health & Safety Enforce all health and safety procedures. Ensure staff use PPE correctly. Report incidents and accidents. Maintain a safe working environment. Conduct regular safety inspections. 5. Equipment & Vehicles Manage company vehicles and equipment. Ensure routine maintenance is completed. Report breakdowns immediately. Keep maintenance records up to date. Minimise equipment downtime. 6. Inventory & Supplies Monitor stock levels. Order operational materials when required. Prevent unnecessary wastage. Ensure teams have the equipment needed for each job. 7. Client Service Liaise with clients during projects. Resolve operational concerns professionally. Ensure teams represent the company well. Maintain high customer satisfaction. 8. Reporting Submit: Daily Operations Report Weekly Operations Report Maintenance Report Incident Reports Staff Attendance Report Key Performance Indicators (KPIs) Jobs completed on schedule Labour productivity Quality inspection score Client satisfaction Equipment downtime Vehicle maintenance compliance Safety incidents Team attendance Budget adherence Operational efficiency Required Skills Strong leadership Planning and organisation Problem-solving Decision-making Communication Time management Conflict resolution Computer literacy Ability to work under pressure Authority The Operations Manager may: Allocate staff and resources. Schedule operational work. Recommend disciplinary action. Stop unsafe work. Approve operational purchases within authorised limits. Make day-to-day operational decisions. Success Looks Like A successful Operations Manager delivers jobs on time, maintains high quality standards, keeps staff productive and safe, ensures equipment is well maintained, and enables the General Manager and CEO to focus on strategic growth rather than day-to-day execution. (images embedded: 0)', true, 'manager', 'x/OPERATIONS MANAGER-20260923T091749Z-1-001/OPERATIONS MANAGER/Operations Manager Job Description.docx', true, '{}'::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, category = excluded.category, content = excluded.content, text = excluded.text, locked = excluded.locked, owner_role = excluded.owner_role, _src = excluded._src, _seed = excluded._seed, data = excluded.data where public.docs._seed and public.docs.updated_by is not distinct from public.docs.created_by;
insert into public.docs (id, title, category, content, text, locked, owner_role, _src, _seed, data) values ('doc-strategy-operations-registers-reference--0e73c7', 'Operations Registers (reference manual for the 5 core operations registers)', 'procedure', '<h2>Operations Registers</h2>
<h3>Purpose</h3>
<p>The Operations Registers form part of the company’s Operations Management System. They provide a centralised method for recording, monitoring, and managing operational activities, ensuring accurate record-keeping, accountability, and informed decision-making.</p>
<p>All registers must be maintained accurately and updated promptly by the responsible person.</p>
<h2>1. Job Register</h2>
<h3>Purpose</h3>
<p>The Job Register is the master record of all jobs undertaken by the company. It tracks each job from quotation acceptance through to completion and invoicing.</p>
<h4>Responsibility</h4>
<p>Operations Manager</p>
<h4>Information Recorded</h4>
<p>Job Number</p>
<p>Quote Number</p>
<p>Client Name</p>
<p>Contact Details</p>
<p>Property Address</p>
<p>Service Type</p>
<p>Job Description</p>
<p>Date Booked</p>
<p>Scheduled Date</p>
<p>Team Assigned</p>
<p>Supervisor</p>
<p>Vehicle Assigned</p>
<p>Job Status</p>
<p>Invoice Status</p>
<p>Completion Date</p>
<p>Notes</p>
<h4>Update Frequency</h4>
<p>Updated whenever a job is created, amended, completed, or cancelled.</p>
<h2>2. Daily Operations Schedule</h2>
<h3>Purpose</h3>
<p>The Daily Operations Schedule provides the operational team with a structured work plan for each day. It ensures that all jobs, staff, vehicles, and equipment are allocated efficiently.</p>
<h4>Responsibility</h4>
<p>Operations Manager</p>
<h4>Information Recorded</h4>
<p>Date</p>
<p>Team</p>
<p>Supervisor</p>
<p>Job Number</p>
<p>Client Name</p>
<p>Property Address</p>
<p>Arrival Time</p>
<p>Service Type</p>
<p>Vehicle Assigned</p>
<p>Equipment Required</p>
<p>Estimated Finish Time</p>
<p>Job Status</p>
<p>Notes</p>
<h4>Update Frequency</h4>
<p>Prepared daily and updated throughout the day as operational changes occur.</p>
<h2>3. Asset Register</h2>
<h3>Purpose</h3>
<p>The Asset Register provides a complete record of all company-owned assets and allows management to monitor ownership, allocation, condition, and replacement planning.</p>
<h4>Responsibility</h4>
<p>Operations Manager</p>
<h4>Information Recorded</h4>
<p>Asset ID</p>
<p>Asset Name</p>
<p>Category</p>
<p>Brand</p>
<p>Model</p>
<p>Serial Number</p>
<p>Purchase Date</p>
<p>Purchase Cost</p>
<p>Assigned Employee or Department</p>
<p>Location</p>
<p>Condition</p>
<p>Warranty Expiry</p>
<p>Status</p>
<p>Notes</p>
<h4>Update Frequency</h4>
<p>Updated whenever assets are purchased, allocated, repaired, relocated, or disposed of.</p>
<h2>4. Maintenance Register</h2>
<h3>Purpose</h3>
<p>The Maintenance Register records all inspections, servicing, repairs, and preventative maintenance performed on company assets.</p>
<h4>Responsibility</h4>
<p>Operations Manager</p>
<h4>Information Recorded</h4>
<p>Maintenance ID</p>
<p>Asset ID</p>
<p>Asset Name</p>
<p>Maintenance Type</p>
<p>Description</p>
<p>Date Reported</p>
<p>Date Completed</p>
<p>Performed By</p>
<p>Cost</p>
<p>Next Service Due</p>
<p>Status</p>
<p>Notes</p>
<h4>Update Frequency</h4>
<p>Updated whenever maintenance is scheduled, completed, or a fault is reported.</p>
<h2>5. Incident Register</h2>
<h3>Purpose</h3>
<p>The Incident Register records all operational incidents to ensure they are investigated, resolved, and used to improve safety and operational performance.</p>
<h4>Responsibility</h4>
<p>Operations Manager</p>
<h4>Information Recorded</h4>
<p>Incident Number</p>
<p>Date</p>
<p>Time</p>
<p>Location</p>
<p>Employee(s) Involved</p>
<p>Incident Type</p>
<p>Description</p>
<p>Immediate Action Taken</p>
<p>Investigation Status</p>
<p>Corrective Action</p>
<p>Status</p>
<p>Notes</p>
<h4>Update Frequency</h4>
<p>Updated immediately after any reportable incident occurs.</p>
<h2>Register Management</h2>
<p>The Operations Manager is responsible for ensuring all registers remain accurate, complete, and up to date.</p>
<p>The General Manager should review the registers regularly to monitor operational performance, identify trends, and ensure compliance with company procedures.</p>
<p>The CEO may review these registers during operational meetings to assess business performance, identify risks, and make strategic decisions.</p>
<h2>Document Review</h2>
<p>This document will be reviewed periodically to ensure the company’s operational registers continue to support efficient operations, effective reporting, and sustainable business growth.</p>
<p>(images embedded: 0)</p>', 'Operations Registers Purpose The Operations Registers form part of the company’s Operations Management System. They provide a centralised method for recording, monitoring, and managing operational activities, ensuring accurate record-keeping, accountability, and informed decision-making. All registers must be maintained accurately and updated promptly by the responsible person. 1. Job Register Purpose The Job Register is the master record of all jobs undertaken by the company. It tracks each job from quotation acceptance through to completion and invoicing. Responsibility Operations Manager Information Recorded Job Number Quote Number Client Name Contact Details Property Address Service Type Job Description Date Booked Scheduled Date Team Assigned Supervisor Vehicle Assigned Job Status Invoice Status Completion Date Notes Update Frequency Updated whenever a job is created, amended, completed, or cancelled. 2. Daily Operations Schedule Purpose The Daily Operations Schedule provides the operational team with a structured work plan for each day. It ensures that all jobs, staff, vehicles, and equipment are allocated efficiently. Responsibility Operations Manager Information Recorded Date Team Supervisor Job Number Client Name Property Address Arrival Time Service Type Vehicle Assigned Equipment Required Estimated Finish Time Job Status Notes Update Frequency Prepared daily and updated throughout the day as operational changes occur. 3. Asset Register Purpose The Asset Register provides a complete record of all company-owned assets and allows management to monitor ownership, allocation, condition, and replacement planning. Responsibility Operations Manager Information Recorded Asset ID Asset Name Category Brand Model Serial Number Purchase Date Purchase Cost Assigned Employee or Department Location Condition Warranty Expiry Status Notes Update Frequency Updated whenever assets are purchased, allocated, repaired, relocated, or disposed of. 4. Maintenance Register Purpose The Maintenance Register records all inspections, servicing, repairs, and preventative maintenance performed on company assets. Responsibility Operations Manager Information Recorded Maintenance ID Asset ID Asset Name Maintenance Type Description Date Reported Date Completed Performed By Cost Next Service Due Status Notes Update Frequency Updated whenever maintenance is scheduled, completed, or a fault is reported. 5. Incident Register Purpose The Incident Register records all operational incidents to ensure they are investigated, resolved, and used to improve safety and operational performance. Responsibility Operations Manager Information Recorded Incident Number Date Time Location Employee(s) Involved Incident Type Description Immediate Action Taken Investigation Status Corrective Action Status Notes Update Frequency Updated immediately after any reportable incident occurs. Register Management The Operations Manager is responsible for ensuring all registers remain accurate, complete, and up to date. The General Manager should review the registers regularly to monitor operational performance, identify trends, and ensure compliance with company procedures. The CEO may review these registers during operational meetings to assess business performance, identify risks, and make strategic decisions. Document Review This document will be reviewed periodically to ensure the company’s operational registers continue to support efficient operations, effective reporting, and sustainable business growth. (images embedded: 0)', true, 'manager', 'x/OPERATIONS MANAGER-20260923T091749Z-1-001/OPERATIONS MANAGER/Operations Registers MANUEL.docx', true, '{}'::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, category = excluded.category, content = excluded.content, text = excluded.text, locked = excluded.locked, owner_role = excluded.owner_role, _src = excluded._src, _seed = excluded._seed, data = excluded.data where public.docs._seed and public.docs.updated_by is not distinct from public.docs.created_by;
insert into public.docs (id, title, category, content, text, locked, owner_role, _src, _seed, data) values ('doc-strategy-operations-workflow-7d8e64', 'Operations Workflow', 'procedure', '<h2>Operations Workflow</h2>
<h3>Purpose</h3>
<p>The Operations Workflow outlines the standard process followed for every job undertaken by the company. Its purpose is to ensure all work is planned, executed, monitored, and completed consistently while maintaining high standards of quality, safety, and customer service.</p>
<p>All operational employees and managers are required to follow this workflow unless otherwise approved by management.</p>
<h2>Step 1: Lead Generated</h2>
<p>Client enquiry is received.</p>
<p>Lead is recorded in the Lead Register or CRM.</p>
<p>The sales representative contacts the client.</p>
<p>Site visit is scheduled if required.</p>
<p>Responsibility: Sales</p>
<h2>Step 2: Quotation</h2>
<p>Site inspection is completed.</p>
<p>Scope of work is determined.</p>
<p>A quote is prepared and sent to the client.</p>
<p>The quote is recorded in the Quote Register.</p>
<p>Follow-up is conducted until the client makes a decision.</p>
<p>Responsibility: Sales</p>
<h2>Step 3: Quote Accepted</h2>
<p>Client accepts the quotation.</p>
<p>The job is approved for scheduling.</p>
<p>Relevant documentation is received if required.</p>
<p>Responsibility: Sales / General Manager</p>
<h2>Step 4: Job Planning</h2>
<p>Job is entered into the Job Register.</p>
<p>The job date is scheduled.</p>
<p>Team members are assigned.</p>
<p>Equipment requirements are identified.</p>
<p>Vehicles are allocated.</p>
<p>Materials and consumables are confirmed.</p>
<p>Responsibility: Operations Manager</p>
<h2>Step 5: Pre-Job Preparation</h2>
<p>Before leaving for site, the Operations Manager ensures:</p>
<p>The team is briefed.</p>
<p>Vehicle inspections are completed.</p>
<p>Equipment is inspected.</p>
<p>Materials are loaded.</p>
<p>Safety equipment is available.</p>
<p>Job documentation is ready.</p>
<p>Responsibility: Operations Manager</p>
<h2>Step 6: Job Execution</h2>
<p>The operational team carries out the work according to company standards.</p>
<p>During execution:</p>
<p>Safety procedures are followed.</p>
<p>Work quality is monitored.</p>
<p>Client communication is maintained where necessary.</p>
<p>Any issues are reported immediately.</p>
<p>Responsibility: Operations Team</p>
<h2>Step 7: Quality Control</h2>
<p>Once the work is completed:</p>
<p>Final inspection is performed.</p>
<p>Work is checked against company standards.</p>
<p>Photos are taken where applicable.</p>
<p>Any defects are corrected before the team leaves the site.</p>
<p>Responsibility: Operations Manager</p>
<h2>Step 8: Job Completion</h2>
<p>Once the client is satisfied:</p>
<p>The job is marked as complete.</p>
<p>Client sign-off is obtained where applicable.</p>
<p>Equipment is cleaned and returned.</p>
<p>Vehicles are checked.</p>
<p>Any incidents or damages are reported.</p>
<p>Responsibility: Operations Manager</p>
<h2>Step 9: Administration</h2>
<p>Following job completion:</p>
<p>Invoice is issued.</p>
<p>Client records are updated.</p>
<p>Job documents are filed.</p>
<p>Payment terms are confirmed.</p>
<p>Outstanding balances are monitored.</p>
<p>Responsibility: Administration / General Manager</p>
<h2>Step 10: Job Closure</h2>
<p>The job is officially closed once:</p>
<p>Payment has been received (where applicable).</p>
<p>Client feedback has been requested.</p>
<p>Operational records have been updated.</p>
<p>All documentation has been filed.</p>
<p>Lessons learned are communicated if improvements are identified.</p>
<p>Responsibility: General Manager</p>
<h2>Supporting Documents</h2>
<p>The following documents support this workflow:</p>
<p>CRM / Lead Register</p>
<p>Quote Register</p>
<p>Job Register</p>
<p>Daily Schedule</p>
<p>Equipment Register</p>
<p>Vehicle Register</p>
<p>Asset Register</p>
<p>Maintenance Register</p>
<p>Supplier Register</p>
<p>Incident Register</p>
<p>Quality Control Checklist</p>
<p>Operations Dashboard</p>
<h2>Key Principles</h2>
<p>Every job must be:</p>
<p>Planned before execution.</p>
<p>Performed safely.</p>
<p>Completed to company quality standards.</p>
<p>Properly documented.</p>
<p>Professionally communicated.</p>
<p>Closed with accurate records.</p>
<p>Following this workflow ensures operational consistency, accountability, and an excellent customer experience while supporting the long-term growth and scalability of the business.</p>
<p>(images embedded: 0)</p>', 'Operations Workflow Purpose The Operations Workflow outlines the standard process followed for every job undertaken by the company. Its purpose is to ensure all work is planned, executed, monitored, and completed consistently while maintaining high standards of quality, safety, and customer service. All operational employees and managers are required to follow this workflow unless otherwise approved by management. Step 1: Lead Generated Client enquiry is received. Lead is recorded in the Lead Register or CRM. The sales representative contacts the client. Site visit is scheduled if required. Responsibility: Sales Step 2: Quotation Site inspection is completed. Scope of work is determined. A quote is prepared and sent to the client. The quote is recorded in the Quote Register. Follow-up is conducted until the client makes a decision. Responsibility: Sales Step 3: Quote Accepted Client accepts the quotation. The job is approved for scheduling. Relevant documentation is received if required. Responsibility: Sales / General Manager Step 4: Job Planning Job is entered into the Job Register. The job date is scheduled. Team members are assigned. Equipment requirements are identified. Vehicles are allocated. Materials and consumables are confirmed. Responsibility: Operations Manager Step 5: Pre-Job Preparation Before leaving for site, the Operations Manager ensures: The team is briefed. Vehicle inspections are completed. Equipment is inspected. Materials are loaded. Safety equipment is available. Job documentation is ready. Responsibility: Operations Manager Step 6: Job Execution The operational team carries out the work according to company standards. During execution: Safety procedures are followed. Work quality is monitored. Client communication is maintained where necessary. Any issues are reported immediately. Responsibility: Operations Team Step 7: Quality Control Once the work is completed: Final inspection is performed. Work is checked against company standards. Photos are taken where applicable. Any defects are corrected before the team leaves the site. Responsibility: Operations Manager Step 8: Job Completion Once the client is satisfied: The job is marked as complete. Client sign-off is obtained where applicable. Equipment is cleaned and returned. Vehicles are checked. Any incidents or damages are reported. Responsibility: Operations Manager Step 9: Administration Following job completion: Invoice is issued. Client records are updated. Job documents are filed. Payment terms are confirmed. Outstanding balances are monitored. Responsibility: Administration / General Manager Step 10: Job Closure The job is officially closed once: Payment has been received (where applicable). Client feedback has been requested. Operational records have been updated. All documentation has been filed. Lessons learned are communicated if improvements are identified. Responsibility: General Manager Supporting Documents The following documents support this workflow: CRM / Lead Register Quote Register Job Register Daily Schedule Equipment Register Vehicle Register Asset Register Maintenance Register Supplier Register Incident Register Quality Control Checklist Operations Dashboard Key Principles Every job must be: Planned before execution. Performed safely. Completed to company quality standards. Properly documented. Professionally communicated. Closed with accurate records. Following this workflow ensures operational consistency, accountability, and an excellent customer experience while supporting the long-term growth and scalability of the business. (images embedded: 0)', true, 'manager', 'x/OPERATIONS MANAGER-20260923T091749Z-1-001/OPERATIONS MANAGER/OPERATIONS WORKFLOW.docx', true, '{}'::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, category = excluded.category, content = excluded.content, text = excluded.text, locked = excluded.locked, owner_role = excluded.owner_role, _src = excluded._src, _seed = excluded._seed, data = excluded.data where public.docs._seed and public.docs.updated_by is not distinct from public.docs.created_by;
insert into public.docs (id, title, category, content, text, locked, owner_role, _src, _seed, data) values ('doc-strategy-sales-code-of-conduct-97321e', 'Sales Code of Conduct', 'policy', '<h2>Tab 1</h2>
<h2>Landscapers Inc.</h2>
<h3>Sales Code of Conduct</h3>
<h4>Purpose</h4>
<p>The purpose of this Sales Code of Conduct is to define the professional standards expected of all sales employees. Every salesperson represents the reputation and values of Landscapers Inc. and is expected to conduct themselves with integrity, professionalism, and accountability at all times.</p>
<h3>1. Professionalism</h3>
<p>Maintain a professional appearance and wear the approved company uniform.</p>
<p>Be punctual for work, meetings, and client appointments.</p>
<p>Treat all clients, colleagues, and suppliers with respect.</p>
<p>Represent the company positively at all times.</p>
<h3>2. Client Service</h3>
<p>Respond to all enquiries promptly.</p>
<p>Listen carefully to the client’s requirements before offering solutions.</p>
<p>Communicate honestly and accurately.</p>
<p>Never make promises that cannot be fulfilled.</p>
<p>Keep clients informed throughout the sales process.</p>
<h3>3. Integrity</h3>
<p>Provide accurate quotations and information.</p>
<p>Never mislead clients regarding pricing, timelines, or services.</p>
<p>Maintain confidentiality of client information.</p>
<p>Avoid conflicts of interest.</p>
<h3>4. Sales Process</h3>
<p>Every salesperson is responsible for following the company’s sales process by:</p>
<p>Recording every enquiry in the Sales Pipeline.</p>
<p>Updating the status of every lead.</p>
<p>Issuing quotations using the approved quotation template.</p>
<p>Recording every quotation in the Quote Register.</p>
<p>Completing follow-ups on time.</p>
<p>Updating the CRM once a client has accepted a quotation.</p>
<p>Completing a proper handover to Operations.</p>
<h3>5. Communication</h3>
<p>Respond professionally to phone calls, emails, and WhatsApp messages.</p>
<p>Return missed calls as soon as reasonably possible.</p>
<p>Keep management informed of significant client issues.</p>
<p>Maintain respectful communication at all times.</p>
<h3>6. Accountability</h3>
<p>Salespeople are responsible for maintaining accurate and up-to-date records, including:</p>
<p>Sales Pipeline</p>
<p>Quote Register</p>
<p>CRM</p>
<p>Weekly KPI Tracker</p>
<p>Failure to keep company records updated may be addressed through the company’s performance management process.</p>
<h3>7. Company Property</h3>
<p>Employees must take reasonable care of company vehicles, phones, laptops, uniforms, and other company assets. Company property must be used for business purposes unless authorised otherwise.</p>
<h3>8. Confidentiality</h3>
<p>Client information, pricing, proposals, company documents, and business information are confidential and may not be shared with unauthorised persons.</p>
<h3>9. Unacceptable Conduct</h3>
<p>The following behaviours are not acceptable:</p>
<p>Falsifying sales information or records.</p>
<p>Failing to update company systems.</p>
<p>Misrepresenting company services.</p>
<p>Being disrespectful to clients or colleagues.</p>
<p>Sharing confidential information.</p>
<p>Accepting gifts or payments that create a conflict of interest.</p>
<p>Damaging the company’s reputation through unprofessional conduct.</p>
<h3>10. Acknowledgement</h3>
<p>I acknowledge that I have read and understood the Landscapers Inc. Sales Code of Conduct and agree to comply with its requirements.</p>
<p>Employee Name: _______________________</p>
<p>Employee Signature: ___________________</p>
<p>Date: ________________________________</p>
<p>Manager Signature: ____________________</p>
<p>Date: ________________________________</p>
<p>(images embedded: 0)</p>', 'Tab 1 Landscapers Inc. Sales Code of Conduct Purpose The purpose of this Sales Code of Conduct is to define the professional standards expected of all sales employees. Every salesperson represents the reputation and values of Landscapers Inc. and is expected to conduct themselves with integrity, professionalism, and accountability at all times. 1. Professionalism Maintain a professional appearance and wear the approved company uniform. Be punctual for work, meetings, and client appointments. Treat all clients, colleagues, and suppliers with respect. Represent the company positively at all times. 2. Client Service Respond to all enquiries promptly. Listen carefully to the client’s requirements before offering solutions. Communicate honestly and accurately. Never make promises that cannot be fulfilled. Keep clients informed throughout the sales process. 3. Integrity Provide accurate quotations and information. Never mislead clients regarding pricing, timelines, or services. Maintain confidentiality of client information. Avoid conflicts of interest. 4. Sales Process Every salesperson is responsible for following the company’s sales process by: Recording every enquiry in the Sales Pipeline. Updating the status of every lead. Issuing quotations using the approved quotation template. Recording every quotation in the Quote Register. Completing follow-ups on time. Updating the CRM once a client has accepted a quotation. Completing a proper handover to Operations. 5. Communication Respond professionally to phone calls, emails, and WhatsApp messages. Return missed calls as soon as reasonably possible. Keep management informed of significant client issues. Maintain respectful communication at all times. 6. Accountability Salespeople are responsible for maintaining accurate and up-to-date records, including: Sales Pipeline Quote Register CRM Weekly KPI Tracker Failure to keep company records updated may be addressed through the company’s performance management process. 7. Company Property Employees must take reasonable care of company vehicles, phones, laptops, uniforms, and other company assets. Company property must be used for business purposes unless authorised otherwise. 8. Confidentiality Client information, pricing, proposals, company documents, and business information are confidential and may not be shared with unauthorised persons. 9. Unacceptable Conduct The following behaviours are not acceptable: Falsifying sales information or records. Failing to update company systems. Misrepresenting company services. Being disrespectful to clients or colleagues. Sharing confidential information. Accepting gifts or payments that create a conflict of interest. Damaging the company’s reputation through unprofessional conduct. 10. Acknowledgement I acknowledge that I have read and understood the Landscapers Inc. Sales Code of Conduct and agree to comply with its requirements. Employee Name: _______________________ Employee Signature: ___________________ Date: ________________________________ Manager Signature: ____________________ Date: ________________________________ (images embedded: 0)', true, 'manager', 'x/SALES PORTFOLIO-20260923T091822Z-1-001/SALES PORTFOLIO/Sales Code of Conduct.docx', true, '{}'::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, category = excluded.category, content = excluded.content, text = excluded.text, locked = excluded.locked, owner_role = excluded.owner_role, _src = excluded._src, _seed = excluded._seed, data = excluded.data where public.docs._seed and public.docs.updated_by is not distinct from public.docs.created_by;
insert into public.docs (id, title, category, content, text, locked, owner_role, _src, _seed, data) values ('doc-strategy-sales-marketing-role-responsibi-e19b7c', 'Sales & Marketing Role & Responsibilities (Sales Role Profile)', 'job_description', '<p>SALES ROLE PROFILE</p>
<p>Sales &amp; Marketing Role &amp; Responsibilities</p>
<p>A clear accountability guide for turning enquiries into profitable, well-managed work.</p>
<p>&lt;TABLE&gt;</p>
<p>Reports to | General Manager</p>
<p>Core purpose | Generate, quote, follow up and win the right work</p>
<p>&lt;/TABLE&gt;</p>
<h2>Role Purpose</h2>
<p>The Sales &amp; Marketing role is responsible for turning enquiries into profitable work. This includes responding to prospects, understanding their needs, preparing quotations, following up consistently, and maintaining professional client communication until the job is handed over to Operations.</p>
<p>The role owns the sales process and visibility of opportunities. It does not own daily job delivery, team scheduling or site management.</p>
<h2>Core Responsibilities</h2>
<h3>1. Manage Enquiries and New Opportunities</h3>
<p>Respond to every new enquiry promptly and professionally.</p>
<p>Qualify the opportunity: understand the client’s needs, budget, timing, location and decision-maker.</p>
<p>Arrange site visits or gather the information needed to prepare an accurate quote.</p>
<h3>2. Prepare and Send Quotations</h3>
<p>Prepare clear, accurate quotes using the company’s approved templates and pricing guidance.</p>
<p>Ensure the client understands what is included, any exclusions, proposed timing and next steps.</p>
<p>Seek approval from the GM before offering unapproved discounts, special pricing or commitments outside normal terms.</p>
<h3>3. Follow Up and Convert Work</h3>
<p>Follow up every outstanding quote consistently until it is won, lost or no longer active.</p>
<p>Record client feedback, objections and reasons for lost work so the business can improve.</p>
<p>Focus on winning profitable work that the Operations team can deliver to the company’s standard.</p>
<h3>4. Manage Client Communication</h3>
<p>Keep prospective clients informed, responsive and treated professionally before the job begins.</p>
<p>Escalate urgent opportunities, major client concerns or unclear technical requirements to the GM.</p>
<p>Hand over confirmed work clearly to Operations, including client expectations, scope and commitments made.</p>
<h3>5. Maintain the Sales Workspace</h3>
<p>Save quotes, client documents, photos, proposals and relevant communication in the Sales folder on the Shared Drive.</p>
<p>Use agreed sales templates and keep active opportunities visible in the company’s agreed sales register or pipeline.</p>
<p>Ensure the GM can see the current status and next action for every meaningful opportunity.</p>
<h3>6. Support Basic Marketing Activity</h3>
<p>Assist with practical marketing activities assigned by the GM, including requesting reviews, updating portfolio material and sharing completed work.</p>
<p>Identify local opportunities, partnerships or follow-up campaigns that may generate suitable enquiries.</p>
<p>Keep marketing activity simple, consistent and connected to real sales outcomes.</p>
<h2>Daily and Weekly Rhythm</h2>
<p>The role should follow a consistent rhythm so opportunities do not get missed and the GM is never guessing about sales activity.</p>
<p>Daily: Respond to enquiries, progress quotes, follow up active opportunities, keep clients informed, and save work in the Sales folder.</p>
<p>Weekly: Review the sales pipeline with the GM: new enquiries, quotes sent, quotes won and lost, outstanding follow-ups, expected work and marketing activity.</p>
<p>Before promising work: Confirm capacity, timing and operational feasibility with the Operations Manager or GM before committing a client to a start date.</p>
<h2>Clear Boundaries and Working Relationships</h2>
<p>&lt;TABLE&gt;</p>
<p>Sales &amp; Marketing | General Manager | Operations Manager</p>
<p>Brings in, quotes and follows up work. | Sets sales expectations and manages performance. | Confirms delivery capacity and manages daily job execution.</p>
<p>Owns enquiries and pre-job client communication. | Approves exceptions, pricing boundaries and major commitments. | Receives a clear handover once work is confirmed.</p>
<p>Does not schedule teams or manage sites. | Coordinates sales, operations and administration. | Does not take ownership of lead generation or quote follow-up.</p>
<p>&lt;/TABLE&gt;</p>
<h2>Concluding Expectations</h2>
<p>Success in this role means no good enquiry is ignored, no quote is forgotten, and no client is left uncertain about the next step. The salesperson is expected to be organised, proactive and accountable for moving opportunities forward.</p>
<p>The goal is not only to send more quotes. It is to create a dependable sales process that brings in profitable work, gives the GM clear visibility, and allows Operations to deliver what has been promised.</p>
<p>Sales principle: Every enquiry deserves a response; every quote deserves a follow-up.</p>
<p>SALES ROLE PROFILE</p>
<p>Sales &amp; Marketing Role &amp; Responsibilities</p>
<p>(images embedded: 0)</p>', 'SALES ROLE PROFILE Sales & Marketing Role & Responsibilities A clear accountability guide for turning enquiries into profitable, well-managed work. <TABLE> Reports to | General Manager Core purpose | Generate, quote, follow up and win the right work </TABLE> Role Purpose The Sales & Marketing role is responsible for turning enquiries into profitable work. This includes responding to prospects, understanding their needs, preparing quotations, following up consistently, and maintaining professional client communication until the job is handed over to Operations. The role owns the sales process and visibility of opportunities. It does not own daily job delivery, team scheduling or site management. Core Responsibilities 1. Manage Enquiries and New Opportunities Respond to every new enquiry promptly and professionally. Qualify the opportunity: understand the client’s needs, budget, timing, location and decision-maker. Arrange site visits or gather the information needed to prepare an accurate quote. 2. Prepare and Send Quotations Prepare clear, accurate quotes using the company’s approved templates and pricing guidance. Ensure the client understands what is included, any exclusions, proposed timing and next steps. Seek approval from the GM before offering unapproved discounts, special pricing or commitments outside normal terms. 3. Follow Up and Convert Work Follow up every outstanding quote consistently until it is won, lost or no longer active. Record client feedback, objections and reasons for lost work so the business can improve. Focus on winning profitable work that the Operations team can deliver to the company’s standard. 4. Manage Client Communication Keep prospective clients informed, responsive and treated professionally before the job begins. Escalate urgent opportunities, major client concerns or unclear technical requirements to the GM. Hand over confirmed work clearly to Operations, including client expectations, scope and commitments made. 5. Maintain the Sales Workspace Save quotes, client documents, photos, proposals and relevant communication in the Sales folder on the Shared Drive. Use agreed sales templates and keep active opportunities visible in the company’s agreed sales register or pipeline. Ensure the GM can see the current status and next action for every meaningful opportunity. 6. Support Basic Marketing Activity Assist with practical marketing activities assigned by the GM, including requesting reviews, updating portfolio material and sharing completed work. Identify local opportunities, partnerships or follow-up campaigns that may generate suitable enquiries. Keep marketing activity simple, consistent and connected to real sales outcomes. Daily and Weekly Rhythm The role should follow a consistent rhythm so opportunities do not get missed and the GM is never guessing about sales activity. Daily: Respond to enquiries, progress quotes, follow up active opportunities, keep clients informed, and save work in the Sales folder. Weekly: Review the sales pipeline with the GM: new enquiries, quotes sent, quotes won and lost, outstanding follow-ups, expected work and marketing activity. Before promising work: Confirm capacity, timing and operational feasibility with the Operations Manager or GM before committing a client to a start date. Clear Boundaries and Working Relationships <TABLE> Sales & Marketing | General Manager | Operations Manager Brings in, quotes and follows up work. | Sets sales expectations and manages performance. | Confirms delivery capacity and manages daily job execution. Owns enquiries and pre-job client communication. | Approves exceptions, pricing boundaries and major commitments. | Receives a clear handover once work is confirmed. Does not schedule teams or manage sites. | Coordinates sales, operations and administration. | Does not take ownership of lead generation or quote follow-up. </TABLE> Concluding Expectations Success in this role means no good enquiry is ignored, no quote is forgotten, and no client is left uncertain about the next step. The salesperson is expected to be organised, proactive and accountable for moving opportunities forward. The goal is not only to send more quotes. It is to create a dependable sales process that brings in profitable work, gives the GM clear visibility, and allows Operations to deliver what has been promised. Sales principle: Every enquiry deserves a response; every quote deserves a follow-up. SALES ROLE PROFILE Sales & Marketing Role & Responsibilities (images embedded: 0)', true, 'manager', 'x/SALES PORTFOLIO-20260923T091822Z-1-001/SALES PORTFOLIO/JOB RESPONSIBILITIES.docx', true, '{}'::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, category = excluded.category, content = excluded.content, text = excluded.text, locked = excluded.locked, owner_role = excluded.owner_role, _src = excluded._src, _seed = excluded._seed, data = excluded.data where public.docs._seed and public.docs.updated_by is not distinct from public.docs.created_by;
insert into public.docs (id, title, category, content, text, locked, owner_role, _src, _seed, data) values ('doc-strategy-sales-workflow-sales-process-so-45b045', 'Sales Workflow (Sales Process SOP Overview)', 'procedure', '<h2>Landscapers Inc.</h2>
<h2>Sales Workflow</h2>
<h3>Purpose</h3>
<p>The purpose of this workflow is to ensure every sales enquiry is managed consistently, professionally, and efficiently from initial enquiry through to project handover.</p>
<p>Every lead must follow the same process to ensure no opportunities are missed and every client receives excellent service.</p>
<h2>Sales Workflow</h2>
<h3>Step 1 – Receive the Enquiry</h3>
<p>Enquiries may be received via:</p>
<p>Phone call</p>
<p>Email</p>
<p>Website</p>
<p>WhatsApp</p>
<p>Referral</p>
<p>Walk-in</p>
<p>Social media</p>
<h4>Responsibility</h4>
<p>Salesperson</p>
<h4>Action</h4>
<p>Respond professionally.</p>
<p>Gather the client’s information.</p>
<p>Understand the service required.</p>
<h3>Step 2 – Record the Lead</h3>
<p>The salesperson must immediately record the enquiry in the Sales Pipeline.</p>
<p>Information captured includes:</p>
<p>Lead ID</p>
<p>Date</p>
<p>Company</p>
<p>Contact Person</p>
<p>Telephone Number</p>
<p>Email Address</p>
<p>Service Required</p>
<p>Lead Source</p>
<p>Assigned Salesperson</p>
<p>Current Stage</p>
<h4>Current Stage</h4>
<p>New Lead</p>
<p>Contacted</p>
<p>Site Visit Booked</p>
<p>Quote Sent</p>
<p>Negotiating</p>
<p>Won</p>
<p>Lost</p>
<h3>Step 3 – Qualify the Lead</h3>
<p>Determine:</p>
<p>Client requirements</p>
<p>Property location</p>
<p>Decision maker</p>
<p>Budget (where appropriate)</p>
<p>Whether a site visit is required</p>
<p>Update the Sales Pipeline accordingly.</p>
<h3>Step 4 – Conduct the Site Visit</h3>
<p>Where required:</p>
<p>Visit the site.</p>
<p>Assess the scope of work.</p>
<p>Take measurements.</p>
<p>Take photographs.</p>
<p>Discuss the client’s expectations.</p>
<p>Record any special requirements.</p>
<h3>Step 5 – Prepare the Quotation</h3>
<p>Prepare an accurate quotation using the approved company quotation template.</p>
<p>Ensure:</p>
<p>Pricing is correct.</p>
<p>Scope is clearly defined.</p>
<p>Validity period is included.</p>
<p>Terms and conditions are attached.</p>
<h3>Step 6 – Update the Quote Register</h3>
<p>Every quotation issued must be recorded.</p>
<p>Capture:</p>
<p>Quote Number</p>
<p>Client</p>
<p>Date Issued</p>
<p>Quote Value</p>
<p>Status</p>
<p>Expiry Date</p>
<h3>Step 7 – Send the Quote</h3>
<p>Send the quotation promptly.</p>
<p>Confirm that the client has received it.</p>
<h3>Step 8 – Follow Up</h3>
<p>The salesperson is responsible for following up all outstanding quotations.</p>
<p>Possible outcomes include:</p>
<p>Awaiting decision</p>
<p>Negotiating</p>
<p>Additional information requested</p>
<p>Won</p>
<p>Lost</p>
<p>The Sales Pipeline and Quote Register must always reflect the latest status.</p>
<h3>Step 9 – Quote Accepted</h3>
<p>Once accepted:</p>
<p>Update the Sales Pipeline to Won.</p>
<p>Update the Quote Register to Won.</p>
<p>Create the client in the CRM (Client Register).</p>
<p>The CRM must include:</p>
<p>Company Name</p>
<p>Contact Person</p>
<p>Telephone Number</p>
<p>Email Address</p>
<p>Site Address</p>
<p>Services Provided</p>
<p>Contract/Retainer Value</p>
<p>Notes</p>
<h3>Step 10 – Create the Client Folder</h3>
<p>Create a client folder containing:</p>
<p>Signed quotation</p>
<p>Contract</p>
<p>Site photographs</p>
<p>Plans (if applicable)</p>
<p>Supporting documents</p>
<p>Client correspondence</p>
<h3>Step 11 – Handover to Operations</h3>
<p>Provide Operations with:</p>
<p>Client details</p>
<p>Signed quotation</p>
<p>Scope of work</p>
<p>Site information</p>
<p>Photos</p>
<p>Special instructions</p>
<p>Required start date</p>
<p>Operations now assumes responsibility for delivering the project.</p>
<h3>Step 12 – Finance</h3>
<p>Finance is responsible for:</p>
<p>Raising invoices</p>
<p>Recording payments</p>
<p>Updating the accounting system</p>
<p>Following up outstanding debtors where necessary</p>
<h3>Step 13 – Management Reporting</h3>
<p>The General Manager reviews sales performance weekly.</p>
<p>Key metrics include:</p>
<p>New Leads</p>
<p>Quotes Issued</p>
<p>Quote Value</p>
<p>Quotes Won</p>
<p>Quotes Lost</p>
<p>Conversion Rate</p>
<p>Revenue Won</p>
<p>Outstanding Quotes</p>
<p>Salesperson Performance</p>
<p>The CEO reviews these reports with the General Manager during management meetings to monitor performance and support business growth.</p>
<h2>Workflow Summary</h2>
<p>Enquiry Received</p>
<p>↓</p>
<p>Sales Pipeline</p>
<p>↓</p>
<p>Qualify Lead</p>
<p>↓</p>
<p>Site Visit</p>
<p>↓</p>
<p>Prepare Quote</p>
<p>↓</p>
<p>Quote Register</p>
<p>↓</p>
<p>Send Quote</p>
<p>↓</p>
<p>Follow Up</p>
<p>↓</p>
<p>Won</p>
<p>↓</p>
<p>CRM (Client Register)</p>
<p>↓</p>
<p>Create Client Folder</p>
<p>↓</p>
<p>Handover to Operations</p>
<p>↓</p>
<p>Operations Delivers</p>
<p>↓</p>
<p>Finance Invoices</p>
<p>↓</p>
<p>Management Reporting</p>
<p>(images embedded: 0)</p>', 'Landscapers Inc. Sales Workflow Purpose The purpose of this workflow is to ensure every sales enquiry is managed consistently, professionally, and efficiently from initial enquiry through to project handover. Every lead must follow the same process to ensure no opportunities are missed and every client receives excellent service. Sales Workflow Step 1 – Receive the Enquiry Enquiries may be received via: Phone call Email Website WhatsApp Referral Walk-in Social media Responsibility Salesperson Action Respond professionally. Gather the client’s information. Understand the service required. Step 2 – Record the Lead The salesperson must immediately record the enquiry in the Sales Pipeline. Information captured includes: Lead ID Date Company Contact Person Telephone Number Email Address Service Required Lead Source Assigned Salesperson Current Stage Current Stage New Lead Contacted Site Visit Booked Quote Sent Negotiating Won Lost Step 3 – Qualify the Lead Determine: Client requirements Property location Decision maker Budget (where appropriate) Whether a site visit is required Update the Sales Pipeline accordingly. Step 4 – Conduct the Site Visit Where required: Visit the site. Assess the scope of work. Take measurements. Take photographs. Discuss the client’s expectations. Record any special requirements. Step 5 – Prepare the Quotation Prepare an accurate quotation using the approved company quotation template. Ensure: Pricing is correct. Scope is clearly defined. Validity period is included. Terms and conditions are attached. Step 6 – Update the Quote Register Every quotation issued must be recorded. Capture: Quote Number Client Date Issued Quote Value Status Expiry Date Step 7 – Send the Quote Send the quotation promptly. Confirm that the client has received it. Step 8 – Follow Up The salesperson is responsible for following up all outstanding quotations. Possible outcomes include: Awaiting decision Negotiating Additional information requested Won Lost The Sales Pipeline and Quote Register must always reflect the latest status. Step 9 – Quote Accepted Once accepted: Update the Sales Pipeline to Won. Update the Quote Register to Won. Create the client in the CRM (Client Register). The CRM must include: Company Name Contact Person Telephone Number Email Address Site Address Services Provided Contract/Retainer Value Notes Step 10 – Create the Client Folder Create a client folder containing: Signed quotation Contract Site photographs Plans (if applicable) Supporting documents Client correspondence Step 11 – Handover to Operations Provide Operations with: Client details Signed quotation Scope of work Site information Photos Special instructions Required start date Operations now assumes responsibility for delivering the project. Step 12 – Finance Finance is responsible for: Raising invoices Recording payments Updating the accounting system Following up outstanding debtors where necessary Step 13 – Management Reporting The General Manager reviews sales performance weekly. Key metrics include: New Leads Quotes Issued Quote Value Quotes Won Quotes Lost Conversion Rate Revenue Won Outstanding Quotes Salesperson Performance The CEO reviews these reports with the General Manager during management meetings to monitor performance and support business growth. Workflow Summary Enquiry Received ↓ Sales Pipeline ↓ Qualify Lead ↓ Site Visit ↓ Prepare Quote ↓ Quote Register ↓ Send Quote ↓ Follow Up ↓ Won ↓ CRM (Client Register) ↓ Create Client Folder ↓ Handover to Operations ↓ Operations Delivers ↓ Finance Invoices ↓ Management Reporting (images embedded: 0)', true, 'manager', 'x/SALES PORTFOLIO-20260923T091822Z-1-001/SALES PORTFOLIO/SALES PROCESS SOP OVERVIEW.docx', true, '{}'::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, category = excluded.category, content = excluded.content, text = excluded.text, locked = excluded.locked, owner_role = excluded.owner_role, _src = excluded._src, _seed = excluded._seed, data = excluded.data where public.docs._seed and public.docs.updated_by is not distinct from public.docs.created_by;
