insert into public.docs (id, title, category, content, text, locked, owner_role, doc_code, _src, _seed, data) values ('doc-training-perform-basic-fire-fighting-fa40ab', 'Training guide — Perform basic fire fighting', 'general', '<p>Facilitator/Learner Guide · Skills Program 1 (SASSETA E - Patrol Security officer/Grade E), item 8; Book 8 · Unit standard 12484 · NQF 2 · 4 credits</p>
<p>People who qualify can choose and use the right fire fighting equipment to put out or control fires in the workplace. The guide says the skills are needed in manufacturing and engineering and count towards exit-level outcomes of manufacturing and engineering qualifications.</p>

<h3>Skills programme matrix</h3><ul><li>programme: Skills Program 1: SASSETA E — grade role: Patrol Security officer/Grade E — row: 1 — unit standard no: 246694 — title: Explain the requirements for becoming a security service provider — nqf level: 3 — credits: 4</li><li>programme: Skills Program 1: SASSETA E — grade role: Patrol Security officer/Grade E — row: 2 — unit standard no: 244184 — title: Apply legal aspects in a security environment — nqf level: 3 — credits: 8</li><li>programme: Skills Program 1: SASSETA E — grade role: Patrol Security officer/Grade E — row: 3 — unit standard no: 244182 — title: Give evidence in court — nqf level: 3 — credits: 4</li><li>programme: Skills Program 1: SASSETA E — grade role: Patrol Security officer/Grade E — row: 4 — unit standard no: 244176 — title: Use security equipment — nqf level: 2 — credits: 2</li><li>programme: Skills Program 1: SASSETA E — grade role: Patrol Security officer/Grade E — row: 5 — unit standard no: 244181 — title: Perform hand over and take over responsibilities — nqf level: 3 — credits: 2</li><li>programme: Skills Program 1: SASSETA E — grade role: Patrol Security officer/Grade E — row: 6 — unit standard no: 244177 — title: Conduct a security patrol in area of responsibility — nqf level: 3 — credits: 7</li><li>programme: Skills Program 1: SASSETA E — grade role: Patrol Security officer/Grade E — row: 7 — unit standard no: 244179 — title: Handle complaints and problems — nqf level: 3 — credits: 6</li><li>programme: Skills Program 1: SASSETA E — grade role: Patrol Security officer/Grade E — row: 8 — unit standard no: 12484 — title: Perform basic fire fighting — nqf level: 2 — credits: 4</li><li>programme: Skills Program 1: SASSETA E — grade role: Patrol Security officer/Grade E — row: 9 — unit standard no: 116534 — title: Carry out basic first aid treatment in the workplace — nqf level: 3 — credits: 2</li><li>programme: Skills Program 2: SASSETA D — grade role: Access control officer/Grade D — row: 1 — unit standard no: 244189 — title: Conduct access and egress control — nqf level: 4 — credits: 7</li><li>programme: Skills Program 2: SASSETA D — grade role: Access control officer/Grade D — row: 2 — unit standard no: 242825 — title: Conduct evacuations and emergency drills — nqf level: 4 — credits: 4</li><li>programme: Skills Program 2: SASSETA D — grade role: Access control officer/Grade D — row: 3 — unit standard no: 11505 — title: Identify, handle and defuse security related conflict — nqf level: 4 — credits: 12</li><li>programme: Skills Program 2: SASSETA D — grade role: Access control officer/Grade D — row: 4 — unit standard no: 117705 — title: Demonstrate knowledge of the Firearms Control Act 2000 (Act No. 60 of 2000) — nqf level: 3 — credits: 3</li><li>programme: Skills Program 2: SASSETA D — grade role: Access control officer/Grade D — row: 5 — unit standard no: 113924 — title: Apply basic business ethics in a work environment — nqf level: 2 — credits: 2</li><li>programme: Skills Program 2: SASSETA D — grade role: Access control officer/Grade D — row: 6 — unit standard no: 119465 — title: Write/present/sign texts for a range of communicative contexts — nqf level: 3 — credits: 5</li><li>programme: Skills Program 2: SASSETA D — grade role: Access control officer/Grade D — row: 7 — unit standard no: 114979 — title: Operate a computer workstation in a business environment — nqf level: 3 — credits: 2</li><li>programme: Skills Program 3: SASSETA C — grade role: Asset &amp; Reaction officer/Grade C — row: 1 — unit standard no: 113909 — title: Coach a team member in order to enhance individual performance in work environment — nqf level: 3 — credits: 5</li><li>programme: Skills Program 3: SASSETA C — grade role: Asset &amp; Reaction officer/Grade C — row: 2 — unit standard no: 13912 — title: Apply knowledge of self and team in order to develop a plan to enhance team performance — nqf level: 3 — credits: 5</li><li>programme: Skills Program 3: SASSETA C — grade role: Asset &amp; Reaction officer/Grade C — row: 3 — unit standard no: 244578 — title: Describe how to manage reactions arising from a traumatic event — nqf level: 3 — credits: 2</li><li>programme: Skills Program 3: SASSETA C — grade role: Asset &amp; Reaction officer/Grade C — row: 4 — unit standard no: 113852 — title: Apply occupational health, safety and environmental principles — nqf level: 3 — credits: 10</li><li>programme: Skills Program 3: SASSETA C — grade role: Asset &amp; Reaction officer/Grade C — row: 5 — unit standard no: 13936 — title: Outline the legal environment of a selected industry — nqf level: 3 — credits: 2</li><li>programme: Skills Program 3: SASSETA C — grade role: Asset &amp; Reaction officer/Grade C — row: 6 — unit standard no: 119472 — title: Accommodate audience and context needs in oral/signed communication — nqf level: 3 — credits: 5</li><li>programme: Skills Program 3: SASSETA C — grade role: Asset &amp; Reaction officer/Grade C — row: 7 — unit standard no: 11508 — title: Write security reports and take statements — nqf level: 4 — credits: 10</li></ul>
<h3>Skills programme totals</h3><ul><li>programme: Skills Program 1: SASSETA E — unit standards: 9 — total credits calculated: 39</li><li>programme: Skills Program 2: SASSETA D — unit standards: 7 — total credits calculated: 35</li><li>programme: Skills Program 3: SASSETA C — unit standards: 7 — total credits calculated: 39</li></ul>
<h3>Course modules</h3><ul><li>study unit: Program guide — title: Program Guide — printed pages: 5-6 — topics: 1. Introduction (p5); 2. Purpose of this learning program (p5); 3. Standards and qualifications (p5); 4. Assessment (p5); 5. Security program matrix (p6) — title on unit page: Program Guide</li><li>study unit: Study unit 1 — title: Types of Fires — printed pages: 7-13 — topics: 1. Definition of a fire (p8); 2. Elements of a fire (p8); 3. Classification of fire (p9); 4. Conditions of a fire (p10); 5. Common sources of heat (p11); 6. Transmission ways in which a fire spread (p12) — title on unit page: Types of Fires</li><li>study unit: Study unit 2 — title: Fire Prevention — printed pages: 14-19 — topics: 1. Goals of fire prevention (p15); 2. Fire hazards (p15); 3. Preventative measures (p17) — title on unit page: Fire Prevention</li><li>study unit: Study unit 3 — title: Housekeeping — printed pages: 20-23 — topics: 1. Why is good housekeeping important? (p21); 2. General housekeeping guideline (p21); 3. Do''s and Don''ts (p22) — title on unit page: Housekeeping</li><li>study unit: Study unit 4 — title: Fire Fighting — printed pages: 24-31 — topics: 1. Know the use of different types of fire extinguishers (p25); 2. How to operate a fire extinguisher (p30); 3. Fire hoses (p31) — title on unit page: Fire Fighting Equipment</li><li>study unit: Study unit 5 — title: Fighting Fire — printed pages: 32-37 — topics: 1. Make the correct decision in the process of fighting a fire (p33); 2. Fighting a fire (p33); 3. Fighting the fire (p34); 4. Procedure after the fire (p35); 5. Handing over to the emergency services (p35); 6. ROAST (p36); 7. Methods of extinguishment (p36) — title on unit page: Fighting Fire</li><li>study unit: Unit Standard — title: Unit Standard — printed pages: 38-51 — topics: NOT IN SCAN — title on unit page: Unit Standard</li><li>study unit: Learner Workbook — title: Learner Workbook Section — printed pages: 52-58 — topics: NOT IN SCAN — title on unit page: Learner Workbook Section</li></ul>
<h3>Learning outcomes</h3><ul><li>study unit: 1 — outcomes: Define a fire; Identify the elements of a fire; Demonstrate knowledge of the different types of fire; Identify the various causes of a fire</li><li>study unit: 2 — outcomes: Demonstrate knowledge of the goals of preventing a fire; Demonstrate skills and knowledge to set up a strategy to prevent a fire; List general measures to prevent a fire</li><li>study unit: 3 — outcomes: Demonstrate the importance of housekeeping; Apply good housekeeping guidelines according to company SOP; Demonstrate knowledge of storage guidelines</li><li>study unit: 4 — outcomes: Know the different types of fire extinguishers; Know the use of the different types of fire extinguishers; Identify the correct fire extinguisher to fight a specific class of fire; How to operate a fire extinguisher; Know the location and operation of a fire hose</li><li>study unit: 5 — outcomes: Make the correct decision in the process of fighting a fire; Stop a fire by using the correct equipment and procedure; Apply general precautions when fighting a fire; Identify and apply the correct procedures after a fire has been put out according to SOP</li></ul>
<h3>Fire science facts</h3><ul><li>topic: Definition of fire — fact: Combustion in which flammable materials burn and give off heat, flames and smoke.</li><li>topic: Elements of a fire — fact: Four elements must be present at the same time: oxygen, heat (to reach ignition temperature), fuel and the chemical reaction. The diagram shows the fire triangle (heat, fuel, oxygen).</li><li>topic: Heat sources in the triangle diagram — fact: Friction, static, electricity, spontaneous, chemical, open light</li><li>topic: Fuel states in the triangle diagram — fact: Solids, liquids, gasses</li><li>topic: Oxygen — fact: Air holds 21% oxygen; a fire needs 16% to keep burning.</li><li>topic: Conditions to expect in a fire — fact: Darkness, heat, smoke. Fire is fast: a small flame can be out of control in under 30 seconds. Fire is hot: about 100 degrees at floor level rising to about 600 degrees at eye level, leading to flashover. Fire is dark: it quickly produces black smoke. Fire is deadly: smoke and toxic gases (CO) kill more people than flames do.</li><li>topic: Common sources of heat — fact: Electricity, smoking, friction, cutting and welding, spontaneous ignition, static electricity, chemical reaction. The diagram also shows naked flame, hot surface, mechanically generated sparks and electrically generated sparks.</li><li>topic: Transmission of fire — fact: Conduction (heat moving through the material itself; metals conduct well, wood poorly), convection (heat carried by moving hot air or gas; the convection column draws in cooler air), radiation (heat travelling as rays or waves).</li><li>topic: Methods of extinguishment — fact: Cooling, starvation, smother. Temperature reduction by cooling with water is the most common method.</li><li>topic: Toxic smoke — fact: A poster in the guide says two to three breaths of toxic smoke can make you unconscious. Burning synthetic materials can give off hydrogen cyanide, acrolein and ammonia as well as carbon monoxide.</li></ul>
<h3>Fire classes</h3><ul><li>class: A — description: Ordinary combustibles such as paper, wood and textile fibres — extinguishing approach: Cooling, blanketing or wetting agent</li><li>class: B — description: Flammable liquids such as gasoline, thinners, oil-based paints and greases — extinguishing approach: Carbon dioxide, dry chemical and halogenated agent types</li><li>class: C — description: Energised electrical equipment — extinguishing approach: Non-conducting gaseous clean agent or smothering agent; carbon dioxide is the most common</li><li>class: D — description: Combustible metals such as magnesium, sodium, potassium, titanium and aluminium — extinguishing approach: Special dry powder agents matched to the specific metal</li><li>class: F — description: Commercial cooking appliances with vegetable oils, animal oils or fats at high temperatures — extinguishing approach: Wet potassium acetate, low pH-based agent</li></ul>
<h3>Fire extinguisher types</h3><ul><li>type: Water — colour code: Signal Red — best for: Organic solids such as wood, cloth, paper, plastics and coal — danger: Do not use on burning fat or oil, or on electrical appliances — how to use: Aim at the base of the flames and keep moving across the fire until every part is out — how it works: Cools the fuel surface and slows pyrolysis</li><li>type: Dry Powder (multi-purpose) — colour code: Blue — best for: Organic solids such as wood, cloth, paper, plastics and coal — danger: Safe on live electrical equipment but does not get into small spaces well, so the fire may re-ignite. It cools poorly, so watch for flare-ups and smouldering in deep-seated fires such as upholstery or bedding. Not for domestic chip or fat pan fires. — how to use: Aim at the base of the flames and sweep towards the far edge. If the extinguisher has a shut-off, wait for the air to clear and attack again if flames are still visible. — how it works: Acts as thermal ballast and gives some chemical inhibition. Knocks flames down fast but may not stop re-ignition.</li><li>type: Foam (AFFF - aqueous film-forming foam, multi-purpose) — colour code: Cream — best for: Solids, and liquids such as grease, fats, oil, paint and petrol. Not for domestic chip or fat pan fires. Can be used on Class A fires but is not recommended for them. — danger: Do not use on domestic chip or fat pan fires — how to use: On solids, aim at the base of the flames. On liquids, do not aim straight into the liquid: aim at the inside edge of the container or a nearby surface and let the foam spread across. — how it works: Water-based with a foaming agent. The foam floats on the burning liquid and separates the flames from the fuel.</li><li>type: Carbon Dioxide (CO2) — colour code: Black — best for: Live electrical equipment where the supply cannot be isolated, and flammable liquids such as grease, fats, oil, paint and petrol. Not for domestic chip or fat pan fires. — danger: Cools poorly, so watch for re-ignition. The fumes can harm you in confined spaces, so ventilate once the fire is controlled. — how to use: Aim the horn at the base of the flames and keep the jet moving across the fire — how it works: Works on classes B and C by smothering the fire and pushing out the air</li><li>type: Wet chemical — colour code: Canary Yellow — best for: Class F fires: cooking oils and fats such as lard, olive oil, sunflower oil, maize oil and butter — danger: Check the manufacturer''s instructions. Not recommended for Class B fires or home use. — how to use: Use the extended applicator in slow circles to spray onto the burning fat until the surface turns soapy. The gentle spray avoids splashing hot oil. — how it works: Potassium acetate solution (sometimes with potassium citrate or bicarbonate) sprayed as a mist. It cools the fire and saponifies the oil into a foam layer. Cannot be used on Class B. The guide says tests show a 6 litre extinguisher can put out a fire in a 75 litre deep fat fryer.</li></ul>
<h3>Fire hazards</h3><ul><li>category: 2.1 Electrical hazards — items: Damaged wiring; Damaged plugs; Damp or wet wires; Overloaded motors; Broken switches, outlets, or sockets; Problems with lighting fixtures; Faulty heating elements; Overloaded circuits; Liquids near computers; Computers without surge protectors</li><li>category: 2.2 Housekeeping hazards — items: Piles of scrap, waste materials, and trash; Sawdust, metal or plastic powder that can form an explosive mixture with air; Obstructed aisles; Blocked emergency exits; Material covering up fire extinguishers, exit signs, and alarms; Blocked sprinkler heads</li><li>category: 2.3 Friction hazards — items: Hot bearings; Misaligned or broken machine parts; Choking or jamming materials; Poor adjustment of moving parts; Inadequate lubrication</li><li>category: 2.4 Process or operation-related hazards — items: Cutting and welding with open flames and sparks; Molten metal that can start a fire after the work is done; Processes that heat materials to high temperatures; Dryers that can overheat; Grinding that throws sparks and dust; Processes that release flammable vapours</li><li>category: 2.5 Storage hazards — items: Material stacked too high, blocking sprinkler heads (18-inch clearance needed below the head); Flammable material stored too close to heat; Flammables not kept in special containers or cabinets; poor ventilation in storage areas; Materials that react with each other stored together; Material in damaged containers; Material in unlabelled containers; Containers not tightly sealed</li><li>category: 2.6 Smoking hazards — items: Ignoring ''No Smoking'' signs; Smoking near flammable or combustible materials; Leaving matches or cigarettes on tables or workbenches; Dropping butts on the floor or grass without putting them out in an ashtray; Throwing lit butts or matches out of windows or doors; Smoking in bed; Leaving a cigarette or cigar unattended; Smoking where sawdust, plastic or metal powder has built up</li></ul>
<h3>Fire prevention goals</h3><ul><li>goals: Identify fire hazards; Eliminate fire hazards; Comply with by-laws — principle: Prevent a fire by finding hazards and keeping heat, fuel and oxygen apart.</li></ul>
<h3>Preventative measures</h3><ul><li>measure: Smoke detectors — guidance: Fit smoke detectors on every floor and check them regularly</li><li>measure: Emergency numbers — guidance: Call emergency services from outside the fire area and keep the emergency number list up to date</li><li>measure: Emergency plan — guidance: Everyone knows the evacuation plan and emergency doors; there is always a second way out; agree a meeting point and do a head count; keep emergency exits and routes clear</li><li>measure: Smokers — guidance: Never smoke when sleepy or going to sleep</li><li>measure: Heaters — guidance: Keep portable heaters at least a metre from anything that can burn; never leave a heater on when you are out of the office</li><li>measure: Matches — guidance: Keep matches away from children and not near a fireplace. The guide says half of fire deaths happen at night.</li><li>measure: Cooking — guidance: Keep cooking areas clear of combustibles; turn pot handles inward; wear close-fitting sleeves; for a grease fire, slide a lid over the pot and turn off the stove</li><li>measure: Tumble dryer — guidance: Do not run it while nobody is home; clean it often</li><li>measure: Electrical appliances and wiring — guidance: Do not overload sockets (use a power strip); do not run cords under carpets or rugs, over nails or in walkways; use heavy-duty cords for big or heat-producing appliances; buy devices with a SANS rating; pull the plug, not the cord; replace cracked or hot cords and plugs; call an electrician if lights flicker or fuses keep blowing; do not use bulbs above the fitting''s rated wattage</li></ul>
<h3>Housekeeping guidelines</h3><ul><li>type: why_important — items: To reduce amounts of flammable and combustible materials; To reduce ignition hazards; To ensure safe emergency evacuation of occupants; To allow for quick emergency response</li><li>type: general_guidelines — items: Keep work areas free of combustible material; Do not obstruct fire equipment, exits and aisles; Do not store material under staircases; Clean up spills regularly; Store flammable liquids in approved flammable liquid stores</li><li>type: dos — items: Keep floors clean and clear of waste; Keep workplaces adequately lighted; Keep light fixtures clean; Inspect and clean machinery and tools regularly; Clean up spills immediately</li><li>type: donts — items: Use aisles and stairways as storage areas; Block emergency exits, fire equipment or first aid stations with stored materials; Allow materials to build up on floors; Store compressed gases near heat sources</li><li>type: note — items: A good housekeeping programme plans how materials are stored and moved from arrival to exit; Employees should report unusual conditions or hazards and obey posted warning signs</li></ul>
<h3>Procedures</h3><ul><li>procedure id: PROC-PASS — name: Operate a portable fire extinguisher (PASS) — steps: Pull the pin; Aim the hose or nozzle; Squeeze the lever; Sweep the agent</li><li>procedure id: PROC-REACT — name: REACT on discovering fire or smoke — steps: Remove persons in immediate danger; Ensure doors are closed (confine fire/smoke); Activate the building alarm; Call the Fire Department; Treat all fires as dangerous — note: Before fighting a fire, make sure you will not put yourself or others in danger.</li><li>procedure id: PROC-ROAST — name: ROAST in case of fire — steps: Report - call the emergency number to report the fire (the guide''s box reads ''Call 911'', low resolution); Observe - find where the fire is; Abort - if possible, shut off available sources of fuel or oxygen; Spray - if necessary, spray sparingly with water or fire extinguishers; Take off - have employees exit the building for safety</li><li>procedure id: PROC-HOSE — name: Operate a fire hose reel (generic) — steps: Make sure the nozzle or jet is closed; Turn on the main valve (some reels will not release the nozzle until this is done); Pull the hose off the drum towards the fire; Open the nozzle or valve and direct the stream at the fire — context: Hose reels are for occupants of multi-storey buildings, especially when trapped and unable to reach an exit. Reels are connected to the mains water supply and reach about 30 m. Some are in cabinets, others are visible on a wall in a hall or corridor, and signs show where they are.</li><li>procedure id: PROC-DECIDE — name: Decide whether to fight a fire — before fighting: Know what is burning, or you will not know which extinguisher to use; Even with an ABC extinguisher, something in the fire may explode or give off toxic fumes; You only have a few seconds to act — do not fight if: You have not called the fire department first; Your escape route may be blocked; The fire is spreading fast; You do not know what is burning; You do not know how to use the equipment; You do not have the right type or size of extinguisher; You might breathe in toxic smoke; The fire is clearly too big for the equipment you have; Your instincts tell you not to</li><li>procedure id: PROC-FIGHT — name: Fighting the fire - rules — steps: Keep a safe distance within the effective range of the extinguisher; Know what is burning and which extinguisher to use; Switch off electricity; Know how to use the extinguisher; Never turn your back on the fire; Move around the edge of the fire to cover it fully; Overhaul: make sure every piece of burning material is out so the fire cannot rekindle; Find hidden burning material, put it out and remove it safely; Remove heat by cooling; If the fire gets out of control, leave immediately; Always keep the exit at your back</li><li>procedure id: PROC-AFTER — name: Procedure after the fire — steps: Watch the fire area for re-ignition; If the fire flares up again, repeat; Call the fire department to inspect the fire area; Replace used equipment</li><li>procedure id: PROC-HANDOVER — name: Hand over to the emergency services — inform officer of: First indication of where the fire started; How the fire developed before they arrived; Actions taken before they arrived; Actions of the last person on site; Colour of smoke; Wind direction; Time the fire started; Nearby hazards known to you, such as LPG, that can affect operations</li></ul>
<h3>Legal references</h3><ul><li>reference: Occupational Health and Safety Act, No 85 of 1993 — usage: Main Act; its purpose is quoted in the introduction</li><li>reference: Occupational Health and Safety Act 83 of 1993 — usage: Listed among "The Preceding Acts" (number conflicts with 85 of 1993 above)</li><li>reference: Factory Act 22 of 1941 — usage: Preceding Act. The first workplace safety law; very prescriptive, and compliance rested on the factory engineer.</li><li>reference: Machinery and Occupational Safety Act 6 of 1983 (MOSACT) — usage: Preceding Act. Moved accountability from the factory engineer to the CEO.</li><li>reference: Machinery and Occupational Safety Act 97 of 1991 — usage: Preceding Act</li><li>reference: National Occupational Health and Safety Act (NOSH Act) — usage: Described as new legislation Government is releasing, benchmarked internationally. Penalties may rise to R1 million or 10 years imprisonment.</li><li>reference: Constitution of South Africa, Chapter 2 Bill of Rights, section 24 (Environment) — usage: Right to an environment not harmful to health and well-being, and to environmental protection</li><li>reference: OHS Act s14 - General duties of employees at work — usage: Take care of own and others'' health and safety; co-operate; obey lawful orders and H&amp;S rules; report unsafe situations; report any incident to the employer or H&amp;S rep as soon as practicable, no later than the end of the shift</li><li>reference: OHS Act s8 - General duties of employers to their employees — usage: Provide a safe workplace; safe systems, plant and machinery; remove or reduce hazards before relying on PPE; safe handling, storage and transport; hazard identification and precautions; information, instructions, training and supervision; enforcement; supervision by a trained person; tell employees the scope of their authority (s37(1)(b))</li><li>reference: Unlabelled extract (a)-(b) about substances — usage: Make sure a substance is safe when properly used, and that information on its use, risks, safe conditions and accident procedures is available. The guide gives no section number; the text is cut off at "subst".</li><li>reference: OHS Act s13 - Duty to inform — usage: (a) Make employees aware of hazards and precautions; (b) tell H&amp;S reps in advance about inspections, investigations, formal inquiries by an inspector and s40 exemption applications; (c) tell the H&amp;S rep about any incident in their area as soon as reasonably practicable</li><li>reference: OHS Act s17 - Health and safety representatives — usage: See the compliance rules. Amended by cl. 4 of G.N.R.2471 dd. 29/12/1993; refers to the Arbitration Act 1965 (Act 42 of 1965) and the Labour Relations Act 1956 (Act 28 of 1956)</li><li>reference: OHS Act s18 - Functions of health and safety representatives — usage: Functions (1)(a)-(j) and entitlements (2)(a)-(f); amended by cl. 5 of G.N.R.2471 dd. 29/12/1993; s18(3) employer provides facilities and training; s18(4) no civil liability</li><li>reference: OHS Act s19 - Health and safety committees — usage: Committee required where two or more reps are designated; meets at least every three months; employer nominees may not outnumber the H&amp;S reps; can co-opt advisory members who do not vote</li><li>reference: OHS Act s20 - Functions of health and safety committees — usage: Make recommendations to the employer or an inspector; discuss incidents where someone was injured, became ill or died; keep records of recommendations and reports; no civil liability; employer must make sure the committee complies with s19(4)</li><li>reference: OHS Act s26 - Victimization forbidden — usage: An employer may not dismiss, cut pay or worsen conditions because an employee gave information, obeyed an inspector, gave evidence or used their rights under the Act (also covers information under s12(2) and reports under s25)</li><li>reference: OHS Act s36, s37(1)(b), s40 — usage: Mentioned in passing: s36 information from inspectors, s37(1)(b) scope of authority, s40 exemptions</li><li>reference: Firearms Control Act 2000 (Act No. 60 of 2000) — usage: Appears only in the Fire Fighting guide''s security skills matrix (US 117705)</li></ul>
<h3>Compliance rules</h3><ul><li>rule id: HSR-01 — topic: When H&amp;S reps are required — rule: An employer with more than 20 employees at a workplace must designate H&amp;S reps in writing, for a set period, within four months of starting business or of passing 20 employees — basis: OHS Act s17(1)</li><li>rule id: HSR-02 — topic: How many reps — rule: Shops and offices: at least one rep per 100 employees or part thereof. All other workplaces: at least one per 50 employees or part thereof. Employees working away from their usual workplace count at the workplace where they report for duty. — basis: OHS Act s17(5)</li><li>rule id: HSR-03 — topic: Inspector may require reps — rule: An inspector can direct an employer to designate more reps, including at workplaces with 20 or fewer employees — basis: OHS Act s17(6)</li><li>rule id: HSR-04 — topic: Who is eligible — rule: Only full-time employees who know the conditions and activities at that workplace or section — basis: OHS Act s17(4)</li><li>rule id: HSR-05 — topic: How reps are chosen — rule: Employer and employees (or their recognised representatives) consult in good faith on nomination or election, term of office and designation. If consultation fails, an agreed arbitrator decides. If there is no agreement on an arbitrator within 14 days, the employer notifies the President of the Industrial Court. — basis: OHS Act s17(2)-(3)</li><li>rule id: HSR-06 — topic: Working hours — rule: Designation, functions and training of H&amp;S reps happen during ordinary working hours and count as work time — basis: OHS Act s17(7)</li><li>rule id: HSR-07 — topic: Appointment letter — rule: Designate H&amp;S reps in writing for a specific period (the guide says normally two years) and keep the appointment letter on file — basis: Learner guide commentary on s17</li><li>rule id: HSR-08 — topic: Core functions of the H&amp;S rep — rule: Do workplace H&amp;S inspections; help with incident and accident investigations; attend 100% of H&amp;S committee meetings during the appointment; represent employees'' H&amp;S interests — basis: Learner guide</li><li>rule id: HSR-09 — topic: Inspection frequency — rule: The H&amp;S rep inspects monthly or quarterly (monthly preferred). Inspection sheets or reports go to the H&amp;S committee (the rest of this text is missing from the scan). — basis: Learner guide section 5</li><li>rule id: HSR-10 — topic: Committee composition — rule: Employer representatives may never outnumber employee representatives. Once appointed in writing, H&amp;S reps automatically join the committee. — basis: Learner guide section 7 / OHS Act s19(2)</li><li>rule id: HSR-11 — topic: Committee meetings — rule: At least once every 3 months. The whole team attends and contributes; co-opted visitors may be invited; the committee nominates a chairperson at its first meeting. An inspector may order a meeting, including at the written request of more than 10% of employees. — basis: OHS Act s19(4) / learner guide</li><li>rule id: HSR-12 — topic: Committee records — rule: Keep a record of every recommendation to the employer and every report to an inspector — basis: OHS Act s20(2)</li><li>rule id: HSR-13 — topic: Incident reporting by employees — rule: Report any incident affecting health or causing injury to the employer or H&amp;S rep as soon as practicable, no later than the end of that shift — basis: OHS Act s14(e)</li><li>rule id: HSR-14 — topic: Employer informs the H&amp;S rep — rule: Tell the H&amp;S rep about incidents in their area as soon as reasonably practicable, and in advance about inspector inspections and inquiries — basis: OHS Act s13(b)-(c)</li><li>rule id: HSR-15 — topic: Safety team visibility — rule: Display the names of the H&amp;S Representative, Evacuation leader, First Aider and Fire Warden clearly in the workplace — basis: Learner guide</li><li>rule id: HSR-16 — topic: Hierarchy of control — rule: Remove or reduce hazards before relying on personal protective equipment — basis: OHS Act s8(2)(b)</li></ul>
<h3>Hs rep functions</h3><ul><li>section: s18(1) functions — items: (a) review the effectiveness of H&amp;S measures; (b) identify potential hazards and potential major incidents; (c) with the employer, examine causes of incidents; (d) investigate employee complaints about H&amp;S; (e) make representations to the employer, the H&amp;S committee or, failing that, an inspector; (f) make representations on general H&amp;S matters; (g) inspect the workplace, articles, substances, plant, machinery and H&amp;S equipment at agreed intervals, after giving the employer reasonable notice; (h) take part in consultations with inspectors and accompany them on inspections; (i) receive information from inspectors (s36); (j) attend H&amp;S committee meetings</li><li>section: s18(2) entitlements — items: (a) visit an incident site at any reasonable time and attend inspections in loco; (b) attend any investigation or formal inquiry under the Act; (c) inspect documents the employer must keep under the Act; (d) accompany an inspector on any inspection; (e) with employer approval, which may not be unreasonably withheld, be accompanied by a technical adviser; (f) take part in internal H&amp;S audits</li></ul>
<h3>Safety team roles</h3><ul><li>role: H&amp;S Representative — assigned person: not stated in source</li><li>role: Evacuation leader — assigned person: not stated in source</li><li>role: First Aider — assigned person: not stated in source</li><li>role: Fire Warden — assigned person: not stated in source</li></ul>
<h3>Safety signage</h3><ul><li>sign: Construction work in progress - parents are advised to warn children of the dangers of entering this site — category: warning (yellow)</li><li>sign: Danger - Demolition work in progress — category: warning (yellow)</li><li>sign: No admittance for unauthorised personnel — category: prohibition (red)</li><li>sign: Use ear protectors — category: mandatory (blue)</li><li>sign: This is a hard hat area — category: mandatory (blue)</li><li>sign: High visibility jackets must be worn — category: mandatory (blue)</li><li>sign: Protective footwear must be worn — category: mandatory (blue)</li><li>sign: Warning - Look out for overhead loads — category: warning (yellow)</li><li>sign: Flammable liquids area - No smoking — category: fire guide illustration</li><li>sign: No smoking — category: fire guide illustration</li><li>sign: Do not block - Fire exit — category: fire guide illustration</li><li>sign: Fire kills - you can prevent it — category: fire guide illustration</li><li>sign: Fire hose reel (cabinet and pictogram) — category: fire guide illustration</li></ul>
<h3>Ppe items</h3><ul><li>item: High-visibility reflective vest</li><li>item: Half-mask respirator with twin filters, shown with goggles</li><li>item: Safety glasses</li><li>item: Hard hat with attached ear muffs</li><li>item: Work gloves</li></ul>
<h3>Classroom rules</h3><ul><li>type: do — items: Participate actively; Ask questions for clarity; Give constructive criticism; Maintain confidentiality; Have a pen and pencil at hand; Use the note pad provided for notes; Keep your valuables safe</li><li>type: dont — items: No answering calls in class; No eating or drinking in class; No discrimination is allowed in class; Avoid unnecessary movement in class; Unnecessary noise is prohibited; Do not steal other fellows'' equipment</li><li>type: learner_materials — items: Learner''s guide; Pens and pencils; Highlighter; Ruler; Note pad</li></ul>
<p><em>Transcribed from the scanned learner guide; the original PDF is in the document vault.</em></p>', 'Facilitator/Learner Guide · Skills Program 1 (SASSETA E - Patrol Security officer/Grade E), item 8; Book 8 · Unit standard 12484 · NQF 2 · 4 credits People who qualify can choose and use the right fire fighting equipment to put out or control fires in the workplace. The guide says the skills are needed in manufacturing and engineering and count towards exit-level outcomes of manufacturing and engineering qualifications. Skills programme matrix programme: Skills Program 1: SASSETA E — grade role: Patrol Security officer/Grade E — row: 1 — unit standard no: 246694 — title: Explain the requirements for becoming a security service provider — nqf level: 3 — credits: 4 programme: Skills Program 1: SASSETA E — grade role: Patrol Security officer/Grade E — row: 2 — unit standard no: 244184 — title: Apply legal aspects in a security environment — nqf level: 3 — credits: 8 programme: Skills Program 1: SASSETA E — grade role: Patrol Security officer/Grade E — row: 3 — unit standard no: 244182 — title: Give evidence in court — nqf level: 3 — credits: 4 programme: Skills Program 1: SASSETA E — grade role: Patrol Security officer/Grade E — row: 4 — unit standard no: 244176 — title: Use security equipment — nqf level: 2 — credits: 2 programme: Skills Program 1: SASSETA E — grade role: Patrol Security officer/Grade E — row: 5 — unit standard no: 244181 — title: Perform hand over and take over responsibilities — nqf level: 3 — credits: 2 programme: Skills Program 1: SASSETA E — grade role: Patrol Security officer/Grade E — row: 6 — unit standard no: 244177 — title: Conduct a security patrol in area of responsibility — nqf level: 3 — credits: 7 programme: Skills Program 1: SASSETA E — grade role: Patrol Security officer/Grade E — row: 7 — unit standard no: 244179 — title: Handle complaints and problems — nqf level: 3 — credits: 6 programme: Skills Program 1: SASSETA E — grade role: Patrol Security officer/Grade E — row: 8 — unit standard no: 12484 — title: Perform basic fire fighting — nqf level: 2 — credits: 4 programme: Skills Program 1: SASSETA E — grade role: Patrol Security officer/Grade E — row: 9 — unit standard no: 116534 — title: Carry out basic first aid treatment in the workplace — nqf level: 3 — credits: 2 programme: Skills Program 2: SASSETA D — grade role: Access control officer/Grade D — row: 1 — unit standard no: 244189 — title: Conduct access and egress control — nqf level: 4 — credits: 7 programme: Skills Program 2: SASSETA D — grade role: Access control officer/Grade D — row: 2 — unit standard no: 242825 — title: Conduct evacuations and emergency drills — nqf level: 4 — credits: 4 programme: Skills Program 2: SASSETA D — grade role: Access control officer/Grade D — row: 3 — unit standard no: 11505 — title: Identify, handle and defuse security related conflict — nqf level: 4 — credits: 12 programme: Skills Program 2: SASSETA D — grade role: Access control officer/Grade D — row: 4 — unit standard no: 117705 — title: Demonstrate knowledge of the Firearms Control Act 2000 (Act No. 60 of 2000) — nqf level: 3 — credits: 3 programme: Skills Program 2: SASSETA D — grade role: Access control officer/Grade D — row: 5 — unit standard no: 113924 — title: Apply basic business ethics in a work environment — nqf level: 2 — credits: 2 programme: Skills Program 2: SASSETA D — grade role: Access control officer/Grade D — row: 6 — unit standard no: 119465 — title: Write/present/sign texts for a range of communicative contexts — nqf level: 3 — credits: 5 programme: Skills Program 2: SASSETA D — grade role: Access control officer/Grade D — row: 7 — unit standard no: 114979 — title: Operate a computer workstation in a business environment — nqf level: 3 — credits: 2 programme: Skills Program 3: SASSETA C — grade role: Asset & Reaction officer/Grade C — row: 1 — unit standard no: 113909 — title: Coach a team member in order to enhance individual performance in work environment — nqf level: 3 — credits: 5 programme: Skills Program 3: SASSETA C — grade role: Asset & Reaction officer/Grade C — row: 2 — unit standard no: 13912 — title: Apply knowledge of self and team in order to develop a plan to enhance team performance — nqf level: 3 — credits: 5 programme: Skills Program 3: SASSETA C — grade role: Asset & Reaction officer/Grade C — row: 3 — unit standard no: 244578 — title: Describe how to manage reactions arising from a traumatic event — nqf level: 3 — credits: 2 programme: Skills Program 3: SASSETA C — grade role: Asset & Reaction officer/Grade C — row: 4 — unit standard no: 113852 — title: Apply occupational health, safety and environmental principles — nqf level: 3 — credits: 10 programme: Skills Program 3: SASSETA C — grade role: Asset & Reaction officer/Grade C — row: 5 — unit standard no: 13936 — title: Outline the legal environment of a selected industry — nqf level: 3 — credits: 2 programme: Skills Program 3: SASSETA C — grade role: Asset & Reaction officer/Grade C — row: 6 — unit standard no: 119472 — title: Accommodate audience and context needs in oral/signed communication — nqf level: 3 — credits: 5 programme: Skills Program 3: SASSETA C — grade role: Asset & Reaction officer/Grade C — row: 7 — unit standard no: 11508 — title: Write security reports and take statements — nqf level: 4 — credits: 10 Skills programme totals programme: Skills Program 1: SASSETA E — unit standards: 9 — total credits calculated: 39 programme: Skills Program 2: SASSETA D — unit standards: 7 — total credits calculated: 35 programme: Skills Program 3: SASSETA C — unit standards: 7 — total credits calculated: 39 Course modules study unit: Program guide — title: Program Guide — printed pages: 5-6 — topics: 1. Introduction (p5); 2. Purpose of this learning program (p5); 3. Standards and qualifications (p5); 4. Assessment (p5); 5. Security program matrix (p6) — title on unit page: Program Guide study unit: Study unit 1 — title: Types of Fires — printed pages: 7-13 — topics: 1. Definition of a fire (p8); 2. Elements of a fire (p8); 3. Classification of fire (p9); 4. Conditions of a fire (p10); 5. Common sources of heat (p11); 6. Transmission ways in which a fire spread (p12) — title on unit page: Types of Fires study unit: Study unit 2 — title: Fire Prevention — printed pages: 14-19 — topics: 1. Goals of fire prevention (p15); 2. Fire hazards (p15); 3. Preventative measures (p17) — title on unit page: Fire Prevention study unit: Study unit 3 — title: Housekeeping — printed pages: 20-23 — topics: 1. Why is good housekeeping important? (p21); 2. General housekeeping guideline (p21); 3. Do''s and Don''ts (p22) — title on unit page: Housekeeping study unit: Study unit 4 — title: Fire Fighting — printed pages: 24-31 — topics: 1. Know the use of different types of fire extinguishers (p25); 2. How to operate a fire extinguisher (p30); 3. Fire hoses (p31) — title on unit page: Fire Fighting Equipment study unit: Study unit 5 — title: Fighting Fire — printed pages: 32-37 — topics: 1. Make the correct decision in the process of fighting a fire (p33); 2. Fighting a fire (p33); 3. Fighting the fire (p34); 4. Procedure after the fire (p35); 5. Handing over to the emergency services (p35); 6. ROAST (p36); 7. Methods of extinguishment (p36) — title on unit page: Fighting Fire study unit: Unit Standard — title: Unit Standard — printed pages: 38-51 — topics: NOT IN SCAN — title on unit page: Unit Standard study unit: Learner Workbook — title: Learner Workbook Section — printed pages: 52-58 — topics: NOT IN SCAN — title on unit page: Learner Workbook Section Learning outcomes study unit: 1 — outcomes: Define a fire; Identify the elements of a fire; Demonstrate knowledge of the different types of fire; Identify the various causes of a fire study unit: 2 — outcomes: Demonstrate knowledge of the goals of preventing a fire; Demonstrate skills and knowledge to set up a strategy to prevent a fire; List general measures to prevent a fire study unit: 3 — outcomes: Demonstrate the importance of housekeeping; Apply good housekeeping guidelines according to company SOP; Demonstrate knowledge of storage guidelines study unit: 4 — outcomes: Know the different types of fire extinguishers; Know the use of the different types of fire extinguishers; Identify the correct fire extinguisher to fight a specific class of fire; How to operate a fire extinguisher; Know the location and operation of a fire hose study unit: 5 — outcomes: Make the correct decision in the process of fighting a fire; Stop a fire by using the correct equipment and procedure; Apply general precautions when fighting a fire; Identify and apply the correct procedures after a fire has been put out according to SOP Fire science facts topic: Definition of fire — fact: Combustion in which flammable materials burn and give off heat, flames and smoke. topic: Elements of a fire — fact: Four elements must be present at the same time: oxygen, heat (to reach ignition temperature), fuel and the chemical reaction. The diagram shows the fire triangle (heat, fuel, oxygen). topic: Heat sources in the triangle diagram — fact: Friction, static, electricity, spontaneous, chemical, open light topic: Fuel states in the triangle diagram — fact: Solids, liquids, gasses topic: Oxygen — fact: Air holds 21% oxygen; a fire needs 16% to keep burning. topic: Conditions to expect in a fire — fact: Darkness, heat, smoke. Fire is fast: a small flame can be out of control in under 30 seconds. Fire is hot: about 100 degrees at floor level rising to about 600 degrees at eye level, leading to flashover. Fire is dark: it quickly produces black smoke. Fire is deadly: smoke and toxic gases (CO) kill more people than flames do. topic: Common sources of heat — fact: Electricity, smoking, friction, cutting and welding, spontaneous ignition, static electricity, chemical reaction. The diagram also shows naked flame, hot surface, mechanically generated sparks and electrically generated sparks. topic: Transmission of fire — fact: Conduction (heat moving through the material itself; metals conduct well, wood poorly), convection (heat carried by moving hot air or gas; the convection column draws in cooler air), radiation (heat travelling as rays or waves). topic: Methods of extinguishment — fact: Cooling, starvation, smother. Temperature reduction by cooling with water is the most common method. topic: Toxic smoke — fact: A poster in the guide says two to three breaths of toxic smoke can make you unconscious. Burning synthetic materials can give off hydrogen cyanide, acrolein and ammonia as well as carbon monoxide. Fire classes class: A — description: Ordinary combustibles such as paper, wood and textile fibres — extinguishing approach: Cooling, blanketing or wetting agent class: B — description: Flammable liquids such as gasoline, thinners, oil-based paints and greases — extinguishing approach: Carbon dioxide, dry chemical and halogenated agent types class: C — description: Energised electrical equipment — extinguishing approach: Non-conducting gaseous clean agent or smothering agent; carbon dioxide is the most common class: D — description: Combustible metals such as magnesium, sodium, potassium, titanium and aluminium — extinguishing approach: Special dry powder agents matched to the specific metal class: F — description: Commercial cooking appliances with vegetable oils, animal oils or fats at high temperatures — extinguishing approach: Wet potassium acetate, low pH-based agent Fire extinguisher types type: Water — colour code: Signal Red — best for: Organic solids such as wood, cloth, paper, plastics and coal — danger: Do not use on burning fat or oil, or on electrical appliances — how to use: Aim at the base of the flames and keep moving across the fire until every part is out — how it works: Cools the fuel surface and slows pyrolysis type: Dry Powder (multi-purpose) — colour code: Blue — best for: Organic solids such as wood, cloth, paper, plastics and coal — danger: Safe on live electrical equipment but does not get into small spaces well, so the fire may re-ignite. It cools poorly, so watch for flare-ups and smouldering in deep-seated fires such as upholstery or bedding. Not for domestic chip or fat pan fires. — how to use: Aim at the base of the flames and sweep towards the far edge. If the extinguisher has a shut-off, wait for the air to clear and attack again if flames are still visible. — how it works: Acts as thermal ballast and gives some chemical inhibition. Knocks flames down fast but may not stop re-ignition. type: Foam (AFFF - aqueous film-forming foam, multi-purpose) — colour code: Cream — best for: Solids, and liquids such as grease, fats, oil, paint and petrol. Not for domestic chip or fat pan fires. Can be used on Class A fires but is not recommended for them. — danger: Do not use on domestic chip or fat pan fires — how to use: On solids, aim at the base of the flames. On liquids, do not aim straight into the liquid: aim at the inside edge of the container or a nearby surface and let the foam spread across. — how it works: Water-based with a foaming agent. The foam floats on the burning liquid and separates the flames from the fuel. type: Carbon Dioxide (CO2) — colour code: Black — best for: Live electrical equipment where the supply cannot be isolated, and flammable liquids such as grease, fats, oil, paint and petrol. Not for domestic chip or fat pan fires. — danger: Cools poorly, so watch for re-ignition. The fumes can harm you in confined spaces, so ventilate once the fire is controlled. — how to use: Aim the horn at the base of the flames and keep the jet moving across the fire — how it works: Works on classes B and C by smothering the fire and pushing out the air type: Wet chemical — colour code: Canary Yellow — best for: Class F fires: cooking oils and fats such as lard, olive oil, sunflower oil, maize oil and butter — danger: Check the manufacturer''s instructions. Not recommended for Class B fires or home use. — how to use: Use the extended applicator in slow circles to spray onto the burning fat until the surface turns soapy. The gentle spray avoids splashing hot oil. — how it works: Potassium acetate solution (sometimes with potassium citrate or bicarbonate) sprayed as a mist. It cools the fire and saponifies the oil into a foam layer. Cannot be used on Class B. The guide says tests show a 6 litre extinguisher can put out a fire in a 75 litre deep fat fryer. Fire hazards category: 2.1 Electrical hazards — items: Damaged wiring; Damaged plugs; Damp or wet wires; Overloaded motors; Broken switches, outlets, or sockets; Problems with lighting fixtures; Faulty heating elements; Overloaded circuits; Liquids near computers; Computers without surge protectors category: 2.2 Housekeeping hazards — items: Piles of scrap, waste materials, and trash; Sawdust, metal or plastic powder that can form an explosive mixture with air; Obstructed aisles; Blocked emergency exits; Material covering up fire extinguishers, exit signs, and alarms; Blocked sprinkler heads category: 2.3 Friction hazards — items: Hot bearings; Misaligned or broken machine parts; Choking or jamming materials; Poor adjustment of moving parts; Inadequate lubrication category: 2.4 Process or operation-related hazards — items: Cutting and welding with open flames and sparks; Molten metal that can start a fire after the work is done; Processes that heat materials to high temperatures; Dryers that can overheat; Grinding that throws sparks and dust; Processes that release flammable vapours category: 2.5 Storage hazards — items: Material stacked too high, blocking sprinkler heads (18-inch clearance needed below the head); Flammable material stored too close to heat; Flammables not kept in special containers or cabinets; poor ventilation in storage areas; Materials that react with each other stored together; Material in damaged containers; Material in unlabelled containers; Containers not tightly sealed category: 2.6 Smoking hazards — items: Ignoring ''No Smoking'' signs; Smoking near flammable or combustible materials; Leaving matches or cigarettes on tables or workbenches; Dropping butts on the floor or grass without putting them out in an ashtray; Throwing lit butts or matches out of windows or doors; Smoking in bed; Leaving a cigarette or cigar unattended; Smoking where sawdust, plastic or metal powder has built up Fire prevention goals goals: Identify fire hazards; Eliminate fire hazards; Comply with by-laws — principle: Prevent a fire by finding hazards and keeping heat, fuel and oxygen apart. Preventative measures measure: Smoke detectors — guidance: Fit smoke detectors on every floor and check them regularly measure: Emergency numbers — guidance: Call emergency services from outside the fire area and keep the emergency number list up to date measure: Emergency plan — guidance: Everyone knows the evacuation plan and emergency doors; there is always a second way out; agree a meeting point and do a head count; keep emergency exits and routes clear measure: Smokers — guidance: Never smoke when sleepy or going to sleep measure: Heaters — guidance: Keep portable heaters at least a metre from anything that can burn; never leave a heater on when you are out of the office measure: Matches — guidance: Keep matches away from children and not near a fireplace. The guide says half of fire deaths happen at night. measure: Cooking — guidance: Keep cooking areas clear of combustibles; turn pot handles inward; wear close-fitting sleeves; for a grease fire, slide a lid over the pot and turn off the stove measure: Tumble dryer — guidance: Do not run it while nobody is home; clean it often measure: Electrical appliances and wiring — guidance: Do not overload sockets (use a power strip); do not run cords under carpets or rugs, over nails or in walkways; use heavy-duty cords for big or heat-producing appliances; buy devices with a SANS rating; pull the plug, not the cord; replace cracked or hot cords and plugs; call an electrician if lights flicker or fuses keep blowing; do not use bulbs above the fitting''s rated wattage Housekeeping guidelines type: why_important — items: To reduce amounts of flammable and combustible materials; To reduce ignition hazards; To ensure safe emergency evacuation of occupants; To allow for quick emergency response type: general_guidelines — items: Keep work areas free of combustible material; Do not obstruct fire equipment, exits and aisles; Do not store material under staircases; Clean up spills regularly; Store flammable liquids in approved flammable liquid stores type: dos — items: Keep floors clean and clear of waste; Keep workplaces adequately lighted; Keep light fixtures clean; Inspect and clean machinery and tools regularly; Clean up spills immediately type: donts — items: Use aisles and stairways as storage areas; Block emergency exits, fire equipment or first aid stations with stored materials; Allow materials to build up on floors; Store compressed gases near heat sources type: note — items: A good housekeeping programme plans how materials are stored and moved from arrival to exit; Employees should report unusual conditions or hazards and obey posted warning signs Procedures procedure id: PROC-PASS — name: Operate a portable fire extinguisher (PASS) — steps: Pull the pin; Aim the hose or nozzle; Squeeze the lever; Sweep the agent procedure id: PROC-REACT — name: REACT on discovering fire or smoke — steps: Remove persons in immediate danger; Ensure doors are closed (confine fire/smoke); Activate the building alarm; Call the Fire Department; Treat all fires as dangerous — note: Before fighting a fire, make sure you will not put yourself or others in danger. procedure id: PROC-ROAST — name: ROAST in case of fire — steps: Report - call the emergency number to report the fire (the guide''s box reads ''Call 911'', low resolution); Observe - find where the fire is; Abort - if possible, shut off available sources of fuel or oxygen; Spray - if necessary, spray sparingly with wat', true, 'manager', 'US 12484', 'x/Company Documents-20260923T091637Z-1-001/Company Documents/Fire Fighting.pdf | scan page 1 (printed page 1); x/Company Documents-20260923T091637Z-1-001/Company Documents/Fire Fighting.pdf | scan page 3 (printed page 5)', true, '{}'::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, category = excluded.category, content = excluded.content, text = excluded.text, locked = excluded.locked, owner_role = excluded.owner_role, doc_code = excluded.doc_code, _src = excluded._src, _seed = excluded._seed, data = excluded.data where public.docs._seed and public.docs.updated_by is not distinct from public.docs.created_by;
